n the TUI, but never that the model *reads* it. An earlier attempt\nwas inconclusive because the configured provider errored on a **native**\nopencode session too, so the failure could not be attributed to fabrication.\n\n**That ambiguity is now resolved, and the news is bad.** Measured 2026-08-05\nagainst opencode 1.18.5 with a known-good provider:\n\n- A **native** opencode session with a planted marker resumes and recalls it\n  correctly via `opencode run -s <id> -m opencode/deepseek-v4-flash-free`.\n  Verified twice, before and after the failing runs below.\n- A **fabricated** session, same directory, same project, same model, minutes\n  apart, **never produces an assistant response at all.** `opencode run -s`\n  appends the user message to the session and exits **0 with completely empty\n  output** — no text, and no events even under `--format json`. Exporting the\n  session afterward shows the user turns accumulating with no assistant reply\n  between them.\n- Reproduced across three models (`deepseek-v4-flash-free`, `big-pickle`,\n  `ling-3.0-flash-free`), so it is structural, not model-specific.\n- When the session is filed under the **global** project (fabricating from a\n  directory opencode has no project for), the same continuation instead fails\n  loudly: `UnknownError / Unexpected server error`.\n\nTwo structural differences between a native and a fabricated session, either of\nwhich is a candidate root cause — confirm or eliminate them rather than\nassuming:\n\n1. Fabricated assistant messages carry only a `text` part, where native ones\n   carry `step-start`, …, `step-finish` around it.\n2. The turnbridge import notice is a `user` message, so a fabricated session\n   opens with two consecutive `user` messages before the first assistant one.\n   Many chat APIs reject consecutive same-role messages.\n\nSo this task has **two deliverables**: the probe, and a fix. Diagnose the root\ncause, fix it in `src/targets/opencode.ts`, and make the probe pass for real.\nA probe that correctly reports FAIL is not sufficient here — the point is a\nfabricated opencode session you can actually talk to.\n\nRequirements:","oldString":"## Gap 1 — headless model-recall probe\n\nWrite `scripts/probe-opencode-content.mjs`.\n\nThe problem it solves: for opencode we have verified that fabricated history\n*renders* in the TUI, but never that the model *reads* it. An earlier attempt\nwas inconclusive because the configured provider errored on a **native**\nopencode session too, so the failure could not be attributed to fabrication.\n\nRequirements:","originalFile":"# Task: close the two opencode verification gaps in turnbridge\n\nRepo: `~/repos/evintunador/turnbridge` (branch off `main`).\n\nTurnbridge bridges conversations between coding-agent CLIs by fabricating\nnative session files/records that the target CLI's own resume loads. The\nopencode target adapter shipped 2026-08-02, but two verification gaps remain\nopen in `docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md` §8. Close both.\n\n## Read first\n\n- `scripts/probe-codex-content.mjs` — the model-recall probe for Codex. Your\n  new opencode probe is the analogue of this.\n- `scripts/probe-large-history.mjs` — the long-history probe. You will extend it.\n- `src/targets/opencode.ts` — the adapter. Note what `fabricate()` returns in\n  its `LaunchPlan`, especially `notes`.\n- `docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md` §8 — the gaps you are closing.\n\n## Gap 1 — headless model-recall probe\n\nWrite `scripts/probe-opencode-content.mjs`.\n\nThe problem it solves: for opencode we have verified that fabricated history\n*renders* in the TUI, but never that the model *reads* it. An earlier attempt\nwas inconclusive because the configured provider errored on a **native**\nopencode session too, so the failure could not be attributed to fabrication.\n\nRequirements:\n\n1. Seed a two-turn conversation into a throwaway git repo via\n   `conversation-ledger`'s `appendEvents`, with a random secret marker in the\n   user turn. Fabricate an opencode session from it with\n   `targetFor(\"opencode\").fabricate(...)`.\n2. Resume headlessly and ask the model to reply with only the marker:\n   `opencode run -s <sessionId> -m opencode/deepseek-v4-flash-free \"<prompt>\"`.\n   Pin that model explicitly — do not rely on the machine's default model,\n   which is a local MLX server that is frequently down.\n3. **Run a native control first.** Create a normal opencode session with the\n   same model, plant a marker in it the same way a user would, resume it, and\n   ask for recall. If the control fails, the probe must report that the\n   provider is broken and exit distinctly — it must NOT report a fabrication\n   failure. This distinction is the entire reason the earlier attempt was\n   inconclusive; a probe that cannot make it is not worth having.\n4. Exit 0 only when the control passes AND the fabricated session recalls the\n   marker. Print a clear PASS/FAIL line naming which of the two failed.\n\n## Gap 2 — long histories on opencode\n\nExtend `scripts/probe-large-history.mjs` so `opencode` is a supported target\nalongside `claude-code` and `codex`:\n\n    PROBE_TURNS=300 node scripts/probe-large-history.mjs opencode\n\nKeep the existing structure: it already takes target names as argv, seeds a\nhistory with a marker in the first turn and another in the last, resumes, and\nasks for both. The claude-code and codex paths must behave exactly as they do\ntoday — verify this by running them after your change.\n\nRequirements:\n\n1. Add the opencode resume invocation (headless, model pinned as above).\n2. The results table must report an accurate transcript size in KB for\n   opencode, the same as it does for the other two targets. Check what it\n   actually prints — a size of 0 means you have not finished this.\n3. Clean up after yourself completely. Confirm by listing opencode's sessions\n   before and after a run: the counts must match. A probe that leaves debris\n   in the user's session picker on every run is a defect.\n4. Do not raise the default `PROBE_TURNS`. 300 is the deliberate cap.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `npm test` passes (currently 88 tests, all must still pass).\n- `npm run build` is clean.\n- Both probes actually **executed** against the real opencode binary, with\n  their real output pasted into the PR description. Do not open a PR\n  describing what the probes \"should\" do — the output is the deliverable.\n- `PROBE_TURNS=300 node scripts/probe-large-history.mjs claude-code codex`\n  still produces the same verdicts as before your change.\n- Session count in `opencode session list` is unchanged after running both\n  probes to completion.\n\n## Then update the docs\n\n- `docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md` §8: mark the two closed unknowns\n  RESOLVED in place, each with the date and the opencode version tested,\n  matching the style the claude/codex specs use for their resolved unknowns.\n  Record the measured numbers, not just \"works\".\n- `docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`, the Roadmap section: the opencode bullet\n  currently ends with \"Remaining opencode gaps: no headless model-recall probe\n  ..., and long histories unmeasured\". Update it to reflect reality.\n- Add the new probe to the roadmap's list of revalidation tooling.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Node 20+, ESM, no new dependencies.\n- Match the surrounding code's style: these probes lead with a comment\n  explaining what question the probe answers and why that question is the\n  right one. Follow that.\n- Do not modify `src/targets/opencode.ts` unless a probe proves an actual\n  adapter bug — if it does, fix it and say so explicitly in the PR.\n- Do not touch the version pins in `scripts/check-adapter-drift.mjs`.\n","replaceAll":false,"structuredPatch":[{"lines":["   its `LaunchPlan`, especially `notes`."," - `docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md` §8 — the gaps you are closing."," ","-## Gap 1 — headless model-recall probe","+## Gap 1 — headless model-recall probe