erated\n  ~237 times until killed — possibly a weak free model, possibly the missing\n  `step-start`/`step-finish` parts on fabricated assistant messages.\n\n**Contract amended 2026-08-06 (user's call, and a standing correction to how I\nweigh this):** the fabricated session *has to work in the target*; fidelity to\nthe source is a property of a functioning session, not a substitute for one.\nSupporting competing CLIs means catering to each one's quirks, so where a\ntarget's native records demand a shape (resolvable provider id, `step-start`/\n`step-finish` parts), match it and put provenance somewhere that doesn't break\nresume — e.g. the import notice. `providerID` is a dispatch field, not a\nprovenance field. Every fabricated field now sorts into three tiers, recorded in\n`docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`'s fabrication contract: (1) portable — shared\ncode writes it; (2) portable by default but overridden where a target requires\nit (source model id, foreign tool calls); (3) target-specific, adapter owns it\noutright (opencode `providerID`/`projectID`/step parts, Claude `parentUuid`,\nCodex `event_msg`/`response_item` twins). Tiers 2-3 are what the adapter\nabstraction is *for*. I had over-indexed on preserving true provenance and\nunder-indexed on \"it has to actually function\" — don't repeat that trade.","oldString":"  Unconfirmed side observation: after the fix the run answered then regenerated\n  ~237 times until killed — possibly a weak free model, possibly the missing\n  `step-start`/`step-finish` parts on fabricated assistant messages.","originalFile":"---\nname: turnbridge-mvp-decisions\ndescription: Turnbridge intent decisions (2026-07-19 MVP, 2026-07-21 end-state) and implementation state\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1c3052566c2e\n  modified: 2026-08-06T04:41:34.834Z\n---\n\nDecisions made by the user on 2026-07-19 that override the original design docs:\n\n- Turnbridge is the **resume layer only**; conversation-ledger owns capture\n  (its `cledger install` hooks + adapters already existed and are reused).\n- Cross-CLI rehydration **defaults to native session fabrication** (writing\n  target-native session files so the CLI's own resume loads them);\n  `--bootstrap` (transcript-file rehydration) is the explicit fallback.\n- MVP audience: the user hopping CLIs **and** sharing chats with collaborators\n  who use different CLIs — own chats by default, `--all` for others', identity\n  via git `user.email` stamped as `actor.id` (upstream cledger change).\n- Command surface: standalone `turnbridge resume [claude|codex]` is default;\n  the PATH shim (`turnbridge shim install`) is opt-in, may become default\n  later if it proves stable over a few weeks.\n- Picker UX the user described: pick conversation → asked which CLI → offered\n  to save that choice as default.\n\nImplementation state: MVP works end-to-end; fabrication verified against real\nbinaries (claude 2.1.215, codex-cli 0.144.6) via planted-secret resume tests.\nVersion-pinned format specs live in turnbridge `docs/specs/`. Upstream\nconversation-ledger gained `package.json` exports + `gitUserIdentity` actor\nstamping (note: full transcript rescans can duplicate pre-identity events\nsince `actor.id` is part of the event-id hash).\n\nEnd-state decisions from 2026-07-21 (drive TODO priority):\n- **Public npm release** is the goal — the `file:../conversation-ledger` dep\n  must become a published semver dep before turnbridge can publish.\n- **Embrace cledger 0.8.0 auto-re-anchor** (readEvents now may write re_anchor\n  events + print stderr as a side effect): keep it on for lineage-survives-\n  rebase; turnbridge just keeps the stderr noise out of its picker UI.\n- **Verify interactive resume paths now** — all fabrication verification was\n  headless (`-p` / `codex exec`); interactive resume for both CLIs was never\n  content-confirmed.\n- **Deepen Claude Code ⇄ Codex** rather than adding a third CLI target\n  (though the user expects both deepening and broadening before release).\n  *Superseded 2026-08-03:* opencode is now a full third member in both\n  directions — target adapter in turnbridge (PR #3), capture in cledger 0.18.0.\n  See [[local-model-oneshot-evaluation]] for how that PR was produced and\n  reviewed.\n  *Open bug found 2026-08-05, root-caused 2026-08-06 (opencode 1.18.5):* a\n  **fabricated opencode session cannot be continued**, in the TUI *and* the CLI\n  (human-confirmed in the TUI: the prompt is persisted as a `user` message and\n  no assistant reply is ever generated). Cause, from `--print-logs`:\n  `ProviderModelNotFoundError: Model not found: turnbridge/<model>`. The adapter\n  writes `providerID: \"turnbridge\"` (src/targets/opencode.ts 321/327/388/408),\n  and opencode resolves the agent loop's model from the session's stored\n  provider — **not** from `-m` or the TUI model picker, which is why neither\n  overrides it. Logs show `loop step=0` → `exiting loop`, no `stream` line,\n  where native sessions log `process` → `stream` → `llm runtime selected`.\n  No network request is made, so the provider is never implicated.\n  The spec's §6 claim that the `Model turnbridge/<model> is not valid` toast is\n  cosmetic (\"composer falls back ... renders in full either way\") is **wrong**:\n  the composer falls back, the agent loop does not.\n  Patching only those 4 `providerID` values to `\"opencode\"` makes the model\n  respond **and correctly recall a marker planted in the bridged history** — so\n  fabrication is sound and the model does read bridged context. Real fix must\n  keep the provider resolvable while preserving source-model provenance\n  honestly (the contract forbids substituting a recognized-but-false id).\n  Unconfirmed side observation: after the fix the run answered then regenerated\n  ~237 times until killed — possibly a weak free model, possibly the missing\n  `step-start`/`step-finish` parts on fabricated assistant messages.\n- npm publish of both packages deliberately deferred for a few days of\n  dogfooding; turnbridge's `prepublishOnly` refuses while the ledger dep is\n  `file:`. Both npm names were unclaimed as of 2026-07-21.\n- Drift-agent stage 2 (CI agent drafts revalidation PRs) deferred — the\n  commented-out job lives in `.github/workflows/adapter-drift.yml`.\n- As of 2026-07-21: interactive resume verified on both CLIs via\n  `npm run smoke:interactive` (expect-based pty; macOS `script` can't run\n  ptyless) + `scripts/probe-codex-content.mjs`; codex 0.145 validated\n  (prefix list); 61 tests; ledger reads sequenced + stderr-buffered.\n\nTwo non-obvious findings from the 2026-07-19 follow-up session:\n- **Codex TUI scrollback renders from `event_msg` records, not `response_item`.**\n  The model still SEES history from response_items (user confirmed resumed\n  Codex knew the prior context), but the screen looks empty without event_msg\n  `user_message`/`agent_message` twins. Codex fabrication now emits both;\n  shapes byte-match real rollout files.\n- **Bridging is a fork, not a move.** Fabricating a target session → target CLI\n  captures it as a NEW conversation under the id turnbridge generated (Codex\n  resume preserves the v4 id and appends). So both source and target show in\n  the picker — a real branch. Turnbridge records a `continuation` ledger event\n  (kind `continuation`, `links` rel `continues`, content `imported_through_seq`)\n  at fabricate time; picker labels lineage both directions and warns on\n  re-bridge. Default shows BOTH endpoints (no abandonment assumed). That edge\n  is also intent-recall's dedup key for the copied prefix. Related: [[evintunador-workflow-preferences]].\n","replaceAll":false,"structuredPatch":[{"lines":["   node_type: memory","   type: project","   originSessionId: c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1c3052566c2e","-  modified: 2026-08-06T04:41:34.834Z","+  modified: 2026-08-11T02:16:10.285Z"," ---"," "," Decisions made by the user on 2026-07-19 that 