{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Confirmed — and the answer is better than I expected.\n\n**The model does read bridged history.** With only the `providerID` patched (4 strings, nothing else changed), the resumed session returned `TB-FAB-0q846j` — the marker planted in the Claude Code history it was bridged from. Fabrication was never the problem.\n\n**The whole bug is one wrong string.** `providerID: \"turnbridge\"` isn't a real opencode provider, so the agent loop throws `ProviderModelNotFoundError` and exits at step 0 without ever calling a model. `-m` and your TUI model picker both had no effect because neither touches the session's stored provider — which is why this looked provider-shaped and wasn't.\n\nYour TUI test was what made this tractable. It ruled out \"CLI-only quirk\" in one shot and sent me looking at session state instead of the network.\n\n**Two caveats I want to be straight about:**\n\n1. The fix isn't just hardcoding `\"opencode\"`. That provider id is deliberate — the fabrication contract forbids substituting a recognized-but-false model id, precisely so a bridged session doesn't claim turns were produced by a model that didn't produce them. The real fix has to keep the provider resolvable *and* preserve provenance honestly. I've written that tension into the spec rather than pre-deciding it.\n2. After the fix the run answered correctly, then regenerated ~237 times (\"The loop has resumed…\") until I killed it. I don't know whether that's the weak free model degenerating or something structural — fabricated assistant messages lack the `step-start`/`step-finish` parts native ones have, so the loop may never see a terminal state. Flagged as unconfirmed for the local model to settle.\n\nAlso worth correcting in the repo: spec §6 currently records the `Model turnbridge/<model> is not valid` toast as cosmetic, saying the composer falls back and \"History renders in full either way.\" The composer does fall