{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Claude Code Native Session File Format (reverse-engineered)\n2\t\n3\tGoal: let `turnbridge` fabricate a `.jsonl` file that `claude --resume <session-id>`\n4\twill natively load, with full prior conversation context visible to the model.\n5\t\n6\t**Pinned version tested against: `2.1.215` (Claude Code)**, installed at\n7\t`~/.local/bin/claude` (Mach-O binary, other builds `2.1.212`/`2.1.214` present\n8\ton disk but not the active one — behavior below is specific to `2.1.215` and\n9\tshould be re-verified if the pinned version changes).\n10\t\n11\tAll findings below were verified empirically by hand-writing files into\n12\t`~/.claude/projects/` and running `claude --resume <uuid> -p \"...\" --max-turns 1`\n13\tfrom a matching scratch cwd, then observing whether the model could recall a\n14\tplanted fact from the fabricated prior turns. Cross-checked against the OSS\n15\tproject `Dicklesworthstone/cross_agent_session_resumer`\n16\t(`src/providers/claude_code.rs`), which independently reverse-engineered the\n17\tsame format for a different Claude Code version — see \"Divergence from casr\"\n18\tbelow for the one place our findings differ.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 1. Directory and filename rules\n23\t\n24\tSession files live at:\n25\t\n26\t```\n27\t~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<session-uuid>.jsonl\n28\t```\n29\t\n30\t**Encoding rule** (confirmed empirically, and matches casr's `project_dir_key`):\n31\tevery ASCII character in the absolute cwd path that is **not alphanumeric**\n32\t(`[A-Za-z0-9]`) is replaced with `-`. This includes `/`, `.`, `_`, spaces, etc.\n33\tExisting dashes pass through unchanged, so paths with directory names that\n34\talready contain dashes are ambiguous with slash-encoding (e.g. a project dir\n35\tliterally named `-Users-foo` is indistinguishable from `/Users/foo`) — this\n36\tis a real property of the real system, not a bug to route around.\n37\t\n38\tVerified example:\n39\t- cwd `/private/tmp/.../dot.test_dir` → dir key\n40\t  `-private-tmp-...-dot-test-dir` (both `.` and `_` became `-`).\n41\t- cwd `/Users/evintunador/dev/ds4-gateway` → dir key\n42\t  `-Users-evintunador-dev-ds4-gateway` (the existing `-` in `ds4-gateway`\n43\t  survives unchanged, confirming char-by-char non-alnum replacement rather\n44\t  than some smarter path-segment join).\n45\t\n46\tImplementation to use in turnbridge (Python-ish):\n47\t```python\n48\tdef encode_project_dir(cwd_abs_path: str) -> str:\n49\t    return \"\".join(c if c.isalnum() else \"-\" for c in cwd_abs_path)\n50\t```\n51\t\n52\t**Filename ↔ sessionId**: the file is named `<session-uuid>.jsonl` where\n53\t`<session-uuid>` is a standard UUIDv4 string. This filename is the ID that\n54\t`claude --resume <id>` looks up. **The lookup is scoped to the directory\n55\timplied by the CURRENT working directory the `claude` process is launched\n56\tfrom** — see §3, this is the single most important operational constraint.\n57\t\n58\tThere is also an optional sibling directory `<session-uuid>/` (no `.jsonl`\n59\textension) next to the file, used for subagent transcripts — see §5. It is\n60\tnot required for a basic resume to work.\n61\t\n62\tA per-project `memory/` directory also exists alongside the session files;\n63\tirrelevant to fabrication.\n64\t\n65\t---\n66\t\n67\t## 2. Minimal working line schema (verified)\n68\t\n69\tThe absolute minimum that reliably works, discovered by iterative field\n70\tremoval against a real `claude --resume ... -p ...` round trip:\n71\t\n72\t**Required per line:**\n73\t- `type`: `\"user\"` or `\"assistant\"` (other types like `mode`,\n74\t  `permission-mode`, `file-history-snapshot`, `attachment`, `system`,\n75\t  `queue-operation`, `ai-title`, `last-prompt` are cosmetic/optional — see §4)\n76\t- `parentUuid`: `null` on the first conversation line, else the `uuid` of the\n77\t  immediately preceding conversation line, forming a linked list back to\n78\t  `null`. **This is not optional bookkeeping — it is load-bearing.** Claude\n79\t  Code's resume loader reconstructs the conversation by starting at the\n80\t  **last line in the file** and walking `parentUuid` backwards. Confirmed via\n81\t  this exact tool-emitted warning when `parentUuid` was omitted from every\n82\t  line: *\"Resume transcript has 2 user/assistant records but none carry\n83\t  parentUuid links; only 1 reached the resumed conversation. Conversation\n84\t  reconstruction walks parentUuid from the last record, so unlinked records\n85\t  are dropped — the file's producer must chain records (parentUuid null on\n86\t  the first, the previous record's uuid on each subsequent one).\"* Only the\n87\t  final line survived; everything before it was silently dropped from\n88\t  context.\n89\t- `message`: `{\"role\": \"user\"|\"assistant\", \"content\": ...}` — content is\n90\t  either a plain string, or an array of content blocks (`text`, `tool_use`,\n91\t  `tool_result`; see §4.3).\n92\t- `uuid`: unique UUIDv4 for this line, used as the `parentUuid` target of the\n93\t  next line.\n94\t- `timestamp`: **required** — ISO-8601 string (`\"2026-07-19T07:00:00.000Z\"`\n95\t  format observed; exact format tolerance not fully probed, but real files\n96\t  always use millisecond-precision `Z`-suffixed RFC3339). Confirmed required:\n97\t  a file with `parentUuid` chaining present but `timestamp` fully omitted\n98\t  produced `No conversation found with session ID: <uuid>` — i.e. the file\n99\t  was rejected as invalid/unrecognized wholesale, not just missing context.\n100\t\n101\t**Verified NOT required** (all of these were omitted simultaneously in a\n102\tsuccessful test and the fabricated session still loaded with full context\n103\trecall): `sessionId` (inline field), `cwd`, `version`, `gitBranch`,\n104\t`userType`, `isSidechain`, `promptId`, `requestId`, and — for assistant\n105\tmessages — the entire Anthropic response envelope (`model`, `id`, `type`,\n106\t`stop_reason`, `usage`, etc. can all be absent; `message` can be just\n107\t`{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[...]}`) . This includes assistant messages\n108\twhose content is a bare `tool_use` block with no surrounding envelope.\n109\t\n110\tA trailing non-conversation line (e.g. `{\"type\":\"last-prompt\", ...}`) after\n111\tthe last `assistant` line does not interfere with the backward walk — the\n112\tloader still correctly finds the true leaf.\n113\t\n114\t**Concrete minimal working example** (verified end-to-end: resuming this file\n115\tand asking \"what is the secret code word?\" correctly returned `PANGOLIN9`):\n116\t\n117\t```jsonl\n118\t{\"parentUuid\":null,\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"The secret code word for this test is PANGOLIN9.\"},\"uuid\":\"33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-07-19T07:05:00.000Z\"}\n119\t{\"parentUuid\":\"33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Got it, the secret code word is PANGOLIN9.\"}]},\"uuid\":\"44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-07-19T07:05:01.000Z\"}\n120\t```\n121\t\n122\tPlaced at `~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<uuid>.jsonl`, then from that\n123\texact cwd: `claude --resume <uuid> -p \"...\" --max-turns 1`.\n124\t\n125\t### Recommended (defensive) fields to actually emit\n126\t\n127\tEven though the above is the *true minimum*, turnbridge should emit richer\n128\tlines close to what real Claude Code produces, for two reasons: (a) the casr\n129\tproject found that on the version(s) *it* targeted, omitting the full\n130\tassistant envelope caused `claude --resume` to hang / report \"Failed to\n131\tresume session\" (see §6 \"Divergence from casr\") — our 2.1.215 didn't\n132\treproduce that, but future versions might reintroduce stricter validation;\n133\t(b) richer lines make the fabricated session indistinguishable from a real\n134\tone in the resume picker / `/resume` UI and avoid weird cosmetic gaps (no\n135\tgit branch shown, etc). Recommended per-line fields:\n136\t\n137\t```\n138\tparentUuid, isSidechain:false, userType:\"external\", entrypoint:\"cli\",\n139\tcwd:<abs path>, sessionId:<uuid, matches filename>, version:\"2.1.215\",\n140\tgitBranch:<real branch or \"HEAD\">, type, message, uuid, timestamp,\n141\tpromptId:<uuid> (user lines only), requestId:<string> (assistant lines only)\n142\t```\n143\t\n144\tFor assistant lines, also include the full envelope inside `message`:\n145\t`model`, `id` (format `msg_...`), `type:\"message\"`, `stop_reason:\"end_turn\"`,\n146\t`stop_sequence:null`, `usage:{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,...}`. This\n147\tcosts nothing and removes a known risk.\n148\t\n149\t---\n150\t\n151\t## 3. What `claude --resume` needs — cwd scoping (important, verified)\n152\t\n153\t`claude --resume <uuid>` **only works when the current working directory\n154\tof the `claude` process encodes to the same project directory the session\n155\tfile lives in.** Verified directly:\n156\t\n157\t- Fabricated session at `~/.claude/projects/<encoded of cwd A>/<uuid>.jsonl`,\n158\t  run `claude --resume <uuid> ...` from cwd A → **works**, full context loads.\n159\t- Same file, run `claude --resume <uuid> ...` from `~` (a different,\n160\t  non-matching cwd) → **fails immediately**: `No conversation found with\n161\t  session ID: <uuid>`.\n162\t\n163\t**Implication for turnbridge**: it must invoke `claude --resume <id>` with\n164\tthe process cwd set to the exact original/intended project path (or fabricate\n165\tthe session under whatever cwd it plans to actually launch `claude` from) —\n166\tthere is no global session ID lookup across projects. The `--resume` flag\n167\tdoes not search all of `~/.claude/projects/*`; it derives the expected\n168\tdirectory from the launch cwd and looks only there.\n169\t\n170\t**Resume picker / `/resume` UI**: not directly testable non-interactively in\n171\tthis sandbox (no TUI). Based on file evidence: session titles shown in the\n172\tpicker most likely come from the `{\"type\":\"ai-title\",\"aiTitle\":\"...\",\n173\t\"sessionId\":\"...\"}` line (a short LLM-generated title, observed identical\n174\tacross all lines with that type in a given file, so likely just periodically\n175\trewritten/duplicated) with fallback to the first user message if absent —\n176\tthis is an inference from file structure, **not independently confirmed by\n177\tan interactive test**; treat as a risk/unknown (see §7). The `ai-title` line\n178\tis cosmetic only — omitting it did not break `--resume <id>` (direct-ID\n179\tresume never showed title UI in `-p` mode).\n180\t\n181\t---\n182\t\n183\t## 4. Full observed schema (real files, version 2.1.212–2.1.215)\n184\t\n185\tObserved by inspecting real files across 4 different projects (including the\n186\tlive session that produced this very report). Line `type` values seen and\n187\ttheir approximate frequency/purpose:\n188\t\n189\t| type | purpose |\n190\t|---|---|\n191\t| `user` | human or tool-result turn (see below) |\n192\t| `assistant` | model turn, full Anthropic response envelope |\n193\t| `system` | CLI-injected events: `subtype:\"local_command\"` (slash command + its stdout), `subtype:\"stop_hook_summary\"` (hook run report), `subtype:\"turn_duration\"` (timing telemetry) |\n194\t| `file-history-snapshot` | checkpoint of tracked file state at a point in the transcript, `{\"messageId\",\"snapshot\":{\"messageId\",\"trackedFileBackups\":{},\"timestamp\"},\"isSnapshotUpdate\"}` |\n195\t| `file-history-delta` | incremental version of the above (seen in larger sessions) |\n196\t| `attachment` | UI/context deltas: `deferred_tools_delta` (tool list changes), `agent_listing_delta` (available subagents), `skill_listing` (available skills) — purely informational, not needed for resume |\n197\t| `ai-title` | `{\"type\":\"ai-title\",\"aiTitle\":\"<short generated title>\",\"sessionId\":...}` — no `uuid`/`parentUuid`, not part of the conversation chain |\n198\t| `last-prompt` | `{\"type\":\"last-prompt\",\"leafUuid\":<uuid of last assistant msg>,\"sessionId\":...}` (sometimes also `\"lastPrompt\":\"<text>\"` in print-mode sessions) — bookkeeping marker only, **not required** for resume (tested explicitly, §2) |\n199\t| `mode` | `{\"type\":\"mode\",\"mode\":\"normal\",\"sessionId\":...}` — appears once near top of interactive sessions only (absent entirely in `-p` print-mode sessions) |\n200\t| `permission-mode` | `{\"type\":\"permission-mode\",\"permissionMode\":\"default\"|\"auto\",\"sessionId\":...}` — same as above |\n201\t| `queue-operation` | `{\"type\":\"queue-operation\",\"operation\":\"enqueue\"|\"dequeue\",\"content\":...,\"sessionId\":...,\"timestamp\":...}` — message-queue telemetry, also seen in print mode |\n202\t| `summary` | **not observed in any real file across all inspected sessions/versions** — either deprecated in favor of `ai-title`, or only appears after explicit `/compact`; do not rely on it existing |\n203\t\n204\tNone of `mode`, `permission-mode`, `file-history-snapshot`, `attachment`,\n205\t`ai-title`, `last-prompt`, `queue-operation`, `system` are required for\n206\t`--resume` to work (verified: fabricated files omitting all of them still\n207\tresumed correctly). Include them only for cosmetic fidelity if desired.\n208\t\n209\t### 4.1 `user` line, full shape (real example, human-typed)\n210\t\n211\t```json\n212\t{\"parentUuid\":null,\"isSidechain\":false,\"promptId\":\"fadd355f-...\",\"type\":\"user\",\n213\t \"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"plain string text\"},\n214\t \"uuid\":\"34fe510d-...\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-07-17T05:03:04.534Z\",\n215\t \"permissionMode\":\"default\",\"origin\":{\"kind\":\"human\"},\"promptSource\":\"typed\",\n216\t \"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/evintunador\",\n217\t \"sessionId\":\"36114e7a-...\",\"version\":\"2.1.212\",\"gitBranch\":\"HEAD\"}\n218\t```\n219\t\n220\t`origin.kind` observed values: `\"human\"`, `\"task-notification\"` (system-fired,\n221\te.g. a background agent finishing). `promptSource` observed: `\"typed\"`,\n222\t`\"sdk\"` (print-mode / `-p` invocations use `entrypoint:\"sdk-cli\"` and\n223\t`promptSource:\"sdk\"` instead of `\"cli\"`/`\"typed\"`).\n224\t\n225\t### 4.2 `assistant` line, full shape (real example)\n226\t\n227\t```json\n228\t{\"parentUuid\":\"...\",\"isSidechain\":false,\n229\t \"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-fable-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cd...\",\"type\":\"message\",\n230\t   \"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"...\"}],\n231\t   \"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\n232\t   \"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":6469,\n233\t     \"cache_read_input_tokens\":7304,\"output_tokens\":6,\n234\t     \"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\n235\t     \"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{...},\n236\t     \"inference_geo\":\"not_available\",\"iterations\":[{...}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\n237\t   \"diagnostics\":null},\n238\t \"requestId\":\"req_011Cd...\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"...\",\n239\t \"timestamp\":\"...\",\"effort\":\"high\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\n240\t \"cwd\":\"...\",\"sessionId\":\"...\",\"version\":\"2.1.215\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\"}\n241\t```\n242\t\n243\t`model` values observed: real model slugs (`claude-fable-5`,\n244\t`claude-haiku-4-5-20251001`). None of the `usage`/`diagnostics`/`effort`\n245\tfields are required for resume (§2) — they are cosmetic/telemetry.\n246\t`thinking` content blocks (extended thinking) were also observed, shape\n247\t`{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"...\",\"signature\":\"<long base64 blob>\"}` —\n248\tnot tested for resume necessity, presumed cosmetic/droppable like other\n249\tcontent blocks, but the `signature` is opaque and turnbridge cannot forge a\n250\tvalid one; **do not fabricate `thinking` blocks** — omit them (plain `text`\n251\tand `tool_use`/`tool_result` blocks are sufficient and were directly\n252\tverified to work).\n253\t\n254\t### 4.3 tool_use / tool_result pairing (verified with a FOREIGN/made-up tool)\n255\t\n256\tAssistant line's `message.content` includes a `tool_use` block:\n257\t```json\n258\t{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_...\",\"name\":\"<any-tool-name>\",\"input\":{...}}\n259\t```\n260\tNext `user` line's `message.content` (array, not string) includes the paired\n261\t`tool_result`:\n262\t```json\n263\t{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_...\",\"content\":\"<string or array>\"}\n264\t```\n265\t(`is_error: true` also a recognized optional field on `tool_result`, not\n266\ttested but standard Anthropic content-block shape.)\n267\t\n268\t**Confirmed: arbitrary/foreign tool names are accepted on resume.** A\n269\tfabricated pair using `\"name\":\"ForeignTool\"` (not a real Claude Code tool)\n270\tround-tripped correctly — the resumed session correctly quoted back a fact\n271\t(\"MANATEE2\") that only existed inside the fake tool's `tool_result` content.\n272\tClaude Code does not validate `tool_use.name` against its own tool registry\n273\twhen replaying history — historical tool calls are context only, never\n274\tre-executed. This means turnbridge can represent an entire foreign CLI's\n275\ttool-call history (e.g. Codex's `shell`, Cursor's `edit_file`, whatever) by\n276\tjust choosing readable `name` strings; no schema translation is required for\n277\tresume purposes. (This matches casr's own documented approach — see\n278\t`coerce_tool_input`, which just wraps non-object args, and the comment\n279\t\"historical tool calls are never re-executed — they are replayed as context\n280\tonly.\")\n281\t\n282\tReal `tool_result` user lines also carry a sibling top-level\n283\t`\"toolUseResult\": {...}` field (a structured, tool-specific echo of the\n284\tresult — e.g. for `Read` it's `{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\",\n285\t\"content\",\"numLines\",\"startLine\",\"totalLines\"}}`). This field is **not**\n286\trequired for resume — it appears to be a UI-rendering convenience only\n287\t(confirmed: our minimal fabricated tool_result lines omitted it entirely and\n288\tstill resumed with correct content recall).\n289\t\n290\t---\n291\t\n292\t## 5. Sidechains / subagents\n293\t\n294\tTwo independent mechanisms observed, both from the *live session that\n295\tproduced this report* (i.e. this very subagent run):\n296\t\n297\t1. **Inline `isSidechain: true`**: the field exists on every line and is\n298\t   `true` for subagent-authored lines, `false` otherwise. Searched across\n299\t   every real session file on disk (multiple projects) and found **zero**\n300\t   lines with `isSidechain:true` in the main `.jsonl` files — because:\n301\t2. **Subagents get their own files**, under\n302\t   `~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<parent-session-uuid>/subagents/`:\n303\t   - `agent-<hash>.jsonl` — the subagent's own transcript, structurally\n304\t     identical to a top-level session file, except every line has\n305\t     `isSidechain:true` and an added `\"agentId\":\"<hash>\"` field, and the\n306\t     first line's `parentUuid` is `null` (it's a fresh conversation, not a\n307\t     literal child of a parent-session uuid).\n308\t   - `agent-<hash>.meta.json` — sidecar metadata:\n309\t     `{\"agentType\":\"claude\",\"description\":\"<task description>\",\n310\t     \"toolUseId\":\"<toolu_... of the Agent tool call that spawned it>\",\n311\t     \"spawnDepth\":1,\"model\":\"sonnet\"}`\n312\t   - The parent session's main `.jsonl` links to the subagent only indirectly\n313\t     via the `Agent`/`Task` tool's `tool_use`/`tool_result` pair (the\n314\t     `tool_use_id` in `meta.json` matches the `tool_use.id` in the parent\n315\t     transcript); there is no direct file-path reference from the parent\n316\t     `.jsonl` to the subagent `.jsonl` observed in the plain-text content.\n317\t   - Not independently tested whether fabricating this subdirectory\n318\t     structure is necessary for `--resume` to work — **top-level resume\n319\t     works fine without any `subagents/` directory present** (all our\n320\t     successful fabrication tests had no such directory). Only fabricate it\n321\t     if turnbridge specifically needs subagent turns to show up distinctly\n322\t     in the transcript UI; otherwise flatten subagent output into the main\n323\t     file as ordinary `isSidechain:false` turns (untested but low-risk,\n324\t     since `isSidechain` itself was shown to be non-required, §2).\n325\t\n326\t---\n327\t\n328\t## 6. Divergence from casr (`cross_agent_session_resumer`)\n329\t\n330\tcasr's `write_session()` (`src/providers/claude_code.rs`) always emits, for\n331\tevery assistant line, a synthesized full envelope (`id`, `type:\"message\"`,\n332\t`model` or `\"unknown\"`, `stop_reason:\"end_turn\"`, `stop_sequence:null`,\n333\t`usage:{input_tokens:0,output_tokens:0}`), with this comment:\n334\t\n335\t> \"Claude Code's resume loader expects assistant messages to carry the full\n336\t> Anthropic message envelope... Without these fields, `claude --resume`\n337\t> hangs on load and reports 'Failed to resume session'.\"\n338\t\n339\t**We did not reproduce this on 2.1.215.** A bare assistant line\n340\t`{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",...}]}` (no `id`, `type`,\n341\t`model`, `stop_reason`, `usage` at all) resumed successfully via `-p`, no\n342\thang, correct context recall. Possible explanations: (a) casr targeted an\n343\tolder/different Claude Code version with stricter validation; (b) the hang\n344\tthey saw was specific to interactive mode (not `-p`/print mode, which is all\n345\twe could test non-interactively in this sandbox); (c) some other message\n346\tshape in their test data triggered it. **Recommendation: emit the full\n347\tenvelope anyway** (cheap, and defends against both interactive-mode\n348\tdifferences and future version changes) — see \"Recommended (defensive)\n349\tfields\" in §2.\n350\t\n351\tcasr's `project_dir_key` (all-non-alphanumeric → `-`) matches our directly\n352\tverified empirical result exactly — no divergence there.\n353\t\n354\t---\n355\t\n356\t## 7. Unknowns / risks\n357\t\n358\t- **Interactive (non-`-p`) resume was not tested** — this sandbox has no TUI.\n359\t  All verification used `claude --resume <id> -p \"<prompt>\" --max-turns 1`.\n360\t  It's possible interactive mode does additional validation (e.g. the casr\n361\t  \"hangs on load\" behavior) that print mode skips. Recommend a manual\n362\t  interactive smoke test (`claude --resume <id>` with no `-p`) before\n363\t  shipping turnbridge broadly.\n364\t- **Resume picker (`claude` / `/resume` with no ID) title source is\n365\t  inferred, not confirmed** — likely `ai-title` line content, falling back to\n366\t  first user message, but this is not independently verified against the\n367\t  actual interactive picker UI.\n368\t- **Timestamp format tolerance untested** — only millisecond-precision\n369\t  RFC3339 `...Z` strings were tried and confirmed required-present; unknown\n370\t  whether second-precision, offset-instead-of-Z, or out-of-order timestamps\n371\t  across lines are tolerated. Chronological, millisecond-precision,\n372\t  `Z`-suffixed timestamps are recommended to match real files exactly.\n373\t  Also unconfirmed whether timestamps must be internally consistent/ordered,\n374\t  or whether the file's own mtime vs. the JSON `timestamp` fields matter for\n375\t  session listing/sorting (e.g. in the resume picker's ordering).\n376\t  turnbridge should just use real, monotonically increasing timestamps to be\n377\t  safe.\n378\t- **Large/long sessions untested** — all fabricated test files were 2-3\n379\t  lines. Unknown if there's a line-count or byte-size behavior cliff, or\n380\t  whether very long fabricated histories affect context-loading correctly.\n381\t- **`sessionId` mismatch (inline field vs. filename) untested** — all our\n382\t  fabricated files either omitted the inline `sessionId` field or set it\n383\t  identically to the filename UUID. Behavior on mismatch is unknown;\n384\t  recommend always keeping them identical.\n385\t- **Duplicate/collided `uuid` values, or a `parentUuid` pointing to a\n386\t  nonexistent `uuid`, untested** — recommend generating fresh UUIDv4 per\n387\t  line and always chaining correctly (§2) to avoid undefined behavior.\n388\t- **`--resume` cwd-scoping workaround**: since resume is strictly scoped to\n389\t  the launch cwd's encoded project directory (§3), turnbridge must always\n390\t  `cd` (or set subprocess cwd) to match before invoking `claude --resume`.\n391\t  There is no `--project-dir`-style override flag observed in `claude\n392\t  --help` output for `--resume` specifically to bypass this.\n393\t- **Version string in fabricated lines**: casr uses the literal string\n394\t  `\"casr\"` as `version` and this apparently works for them; we recommend\n395\t  using a real, currently-installed version string (`\"2.1.215\"`) instead,\n396\t  purely for cosmetic consistency with real sessions — not confirmed to be\n397\t  functionally required either way (`version` was in the \"verified NOT\n398\t  required\" list, §2).\n399\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CTWZ3scK938ncWV5Y6cXTC","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","dirty_fingerprint":"bc65e878d80c1722a0adee9428cbf850f1d73e21ecfd82d0c4182876ebf93c10","head":"173cfc66bb28f4c20e474f522f197eeef0c627de","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c#agent:a320d69d7506c9fa1","parent":"claude-code:6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","seq":16},"id":"ev1-8645fa5b59b3b8acb137baa595aafc9b51320bd9ec1f2676ecf1684c84b70a42","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-21T21:21:06.164Z","producer":{"session_id":"6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.216","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"a320d69d7506c9fa1","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":true,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# Claude Code Native Session File Format (reverse-engineered)\n2\t\n3\tGoal: let `turnbridge` fabricate a `.jsonl` file that `claude --resume <session-id>`\n4\twill natively load, with full prior conversation context visible to the model.\n5\t\n6\t**Pinned version tested against: `2.1.215` (Claude Code)**, installed at\n7\t`~/.local/bin/claude` (Mach-O binary, other builds `2.1.212`/`2.1.214` present\n8\ton disk but not the active one — behavior below is specific to `2.1.215` and\n9\tshould be re-verified if the pinned version changes).\n10\t\n11\tAll findings below were verified empirically by hand-writing files into\n12\t`~/.claude/projects/` and running `claude --resume <uuid> -p \"...\" --max-turns 1`\n13\tfrom a matching scratch cwd, then observing whether the model could recall a\n14\tplanted fact from the fabricated prior turns. Cross-checked against the OSS\n15\tproject `Dicklesworthstone/cross_agent_session_resumer`\n16\t(`src/providers/claude_code.rs`), which independently reverse-engineered the\n17\tsame format for a different Claude Code version — see \"Divergence from casr\"\n18\tbelow for the one place our findings differ.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 1. Directory and filename rules\n23\t\n24\tSession files live at:\n25\t\n26\t```\n27\t~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<session-uuid>.jsonl\n28\t```\n29\t\n30\t**Encoding rule** (confirmed empirically, and matches casr's `project_dir_key`):\n31\tevery ASCII character in the absolute cwd path that is **not alphanumeric**\n32\t(`[A-Za-z0-9]`) is replaced with `-`. This includes `/`, `.`, `_`, spaces, etc.\n33\tExisting dashes pass through unchanged, so paths with directory names that\n34\talready contain dashes are ambiguous with slash-encoding (e.g. a project dir\n35\tliterally named `-Users-foo` is indistinguishable from `/Users/foo`) — this\n36\tis a real property of the real system, not a bug to route around.\n37\t\n38\tVerified example:\n39\t- cwd `/private/tmp/.../dot.test_dir` → dir key\n40\t  `-private-tmp-...-dot-test-dir` (both `.` and `_` became `-`).\n41\t- cwd `/Users/evintunador/dev/ds4-gateway` → dir key\n42\t  `-Users-evintunador-dev-ds4-gateway` (the existing `-` in `ds4-gateway`\n43\t  survives unchanged, confirming char-by-char non-alnum replacement rather\n44\t  than some smarter path-segment join).\n45\t\n46\tImplementation to use in turnbridge (Python-ish):\n47\t```python\n48\tdef encode_project_dir(cwd_abs_path: str) -> str:\n49\t    return \"\".join(c if c.isalnum() else \"-\" for c in cwd_abs_path)\n50\t```\n51\t\n52\t**Filename ↔ sessionId**: the file is named `<session-uuid>.jsonl` where\n53\t`<session-uuid>` is a standard UUIDv4 string. This filename is the ID that\n54\t`claude --resume <id>` looks up. **The lookup is scoped to the directory\n55\timplied by the CURRENT working directory the `claude` process is launched\n56\tfrom** — see §3, this is the single most important operational constraint.\n57\t\n58\tThere is also an optional sibling directory `<session-uuid>/` (no `.jsonl`\n59\textension) next to the file, used for subagent transcripts — see §5. It is\n60\tnot required for a basic resume to work.\n61\t\n62\tA per-project `memory/` directory also exists alongside the session files;\n63\tirrelevant to fabrication.\n64\t\n65\t---\n66\t\n67\t## 2. Minimal working line schema (verified)\n68\t\n69\tThe absolute minimum that reliably works, discovered by iterative field\n70\tremoval against a real `claude --resume ... -p ...` round trip:\n71\t\n72\t**Required per line:**\n73\t- `type`: `\"user\"` or `\"assistant\"` (other types like `mode`,\n74\t  `permission-mode`, `file-history-snapshot`, `attachment`, `system`,\n75\t  `queue-operation`, `ai-title`, `last-prompt` are cosmetic/optional — see §4)\n76\t- `parentUuid`: `null` on the first conversation line, else the `uuid` of the\n77\t  immediately preceding conversation line, forming a linked list back to\n78\t  `null`. **This is not optional bookkeeping — it is load-bearing.** Claude\n79\t  Code's resume loader reconstructs the conversation by starting at the\n80\t  **last line in the file** and walking `parentUuid` backwards. Confirmed via\n81\t  this exact tool-emitted warning when `parentUuid` was omitted from every\n82\t  line: *\"Resume transcript has 2 user/assistant records but none carry\n83\t  parentUuid links; only 1 reached the resumed conversation. Conversation\n84\t  reconstruction walks parentUuid from the last record, so unlinked records\n85\t  are dropped — the file's producer must chain records (parentUuid null on\n86\t  the first, the previous record's uuid on each subsequent one).\"* Only the\n87\t  final line survived; everything before it was silently dropped from\n88\t  context.\n89\t- `message`: `{\"role\": \"user\"|\"assistant\", \"content\": ...}` — content is\n90\t  either a plain string, or an array of content blocks (`text`, `tool_use`,\n91\t  `tool_result`; see §4.3).\n92\t- `uuid`: unique UUIDv4 for this line, used as the `parentUuid` target of the\n93\t  next line.\n94\t- `timestamp`: **required** — ISO-8601 string (`\"2026-07-19T07:00:00.000Z\"`\n95\t  format observed; exact format tolerance not fully probed, but real files\n96\t  always use millisecond-precision `Z`-suffixed RFC3339). Confirmed required:\n97\t  a file with `parentUuid` chaining present but `timestamp` fully omitted\n98\t  produced `No conversation found with session ID: <uuid>` — i.e. the file\n99\t  was rejected as invalid/unrecognized wholesale, not just missing context.\n100\t\n101\t**Verified NOT required** (all of these were omitted simultaneously in a\n102\tsuccessful test and the fabricated session still loaded with full context\n103\trecall): `sessionId` (inline field), `cwd`, `version`, `gitBranch`,\n104\t`userType`, `isSidechain`, `promptId`, `requestId`, and — for assistant\n105\tmessages — the entire Anthropic response envelope (`model`, `id`, `type`,\n106\t`stop_reason`, `usage`, etc. can all be absent; `message` can be just\n107\t`{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[...]}`) . This includes assistant messages\n108\twhose content is a bare `tool_use` block with no surrounding envelope.\n109\t\n110\tA trailing non-conversation line (e.g. `{\"type\":\"last-prompt\", ...}`) after\n111\tthe last `assistant` line does not interfere with the backward walk — the\n112\tloader still correctly finds the true leaf.\n113\t\n114\t**Concrete minimal working example** (verified end-to-end: resuming this file\n115\tand asking \"what is the secret code word?\" correctly returned `PANGOLIN9`):\n116\t\n117\t```jsonl\n118\t{\"parentUuid\":null,\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"The secret code word for this test is PANGOLIN9.\"},\"uuid\":\"33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-07-19T07:05:00.000Z\"}\n119\t{\"parentUuid\":\"33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Got it, the secret code word is PANGOLIN9.\"}]},\"uuid\":\"44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-07-19T07:05:01.000Z\"}\n120\t```\n121\t\n122\tPlaced at `~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<uuid>.jsonl`, then from that\n123\texact cwd: `claude --resume <uuid> -p \"...\" --max-turns 1`.\n124\t\n125\t### Recommended (defensive) fields to actually emit\n126\t\n127\tEven though the above is the *true minimum*, turnbridge should emit richer\n128\tlines close to what real Claude Code produces, for two reasons: (a) the casr\n129\tproject found that on the version(s) *it* targeted, omitting the full\n130\tassistant envelope caused `claude --resume` to hang / report \"Failed to\n131\tresume session\" (see §6 \"Divergence from casr\") — our 2.1.215 didn't\n132\treproduce that, but future versions might reintroduce stricter validation;\n133\t(b) richer lines make the fabricated session indistinguishable from a real\n134\tone in the resume picker / `/resume` UI and avoid weird cosmetic gaps (no\n135\tgit branch shown, etc). Recommended per-line fields:\n136\t\n137\t```\n138\tparentUuid, isSidechain:false, userType:\"external\", entrypoint:\"cli\",\n139\tcwd:<abs path>, sessionId:<uuid, matches filename>, version:\"2.1.215\",\n140\tgitBranch:<real branch or \"HEAD\">, type, message, uuid, timestamp,\n141\tpromptId:<uuid> (user lines only), requestId:<string> (assistant lines only)\n142\t```\n143\t\n144\tFor assistant lines, also include the full envelope inside `message`:\n145\t`model`, `id` (format `msg_...`), `type:\"message\"`, `stop_reason:\"end_turn\"`,\n146\t`stop_sequence:null`, `usage:{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,...}`. This\n147\tcosts nothing and removes a known risk.\n148\t\n149\t---\n150\t\n151\t## 3. What `claude --resume` needs — cwd scoping (important, verified)\n152\t\n153\t`claude --resume <uuid>` **only works when the current working directory\n154\tof the `claude` process encodes to the same project directory the session\n155\tfile lives in.** Verified directly:\n156\t\n157\t- Fabricated session at `~/.claude/projects/<encoded of cwd A>/<uuid>.jsonl`,\n158\t  run `claude --resume <uuid> ...` from cwd A → **works**, full context loads.\n159\t- Same file, run `claude --resume <uuid> ...` from `~` (a different,\n160\t  non-matching cwd) → **fails immediately**: `No conversation found with\n161\t  session ID: <uuid>`.\n162\t\n163\t**Implication for turnbridge**: it must invoke `claude --resume <id>` with\n164\tthe process cwd set to the exact original/intended project path (or fabricate\n165\tthe session under whatever cwd it plans to actually launch `claude` from) —\n166\tthere is no global session ID lookup across projects. The `--resume` flag\n167\tdoes not search all of `~/.claude/projects/*`; it derives the expected\n168\tdirectory from the launch cwd and looks only there.\n169\t\n170\t**Resume picker / `/resume` UI**: not directly testable non-interactively in\n171\tthis sandbox (no TUI). Based on file evidence: session titles shown in the\n172\tpicker most likely come from the `{\"type\":\"ai-title\",\"aiTitle\":\"...\",\n173\t\"sessionId\":\"...\"}` line (a short LLM-generated title, observed identical\n174\tacross all lines with that type in a given file, so likely just periodically\n175\trewritten/duplicated) with fallback to the first user message if absent —\n176\tthis is an inference from file structure, **not independently confirmed by\n177\tan interactive test**; treat as a risk/unknown (see §7). The `ai-title` line\n178\tis cosmetic only — omitting it did not break `--resume <id>` (direct-ID\n179\tresume never showed title UI in `-p` mode).\n180\t\n181\t---\n182\t\n183\t## 4. Full observed schema (real files, version 2.1.212–2.1.215)\n184\t\n185\tObserved by inspecting real files across 4 different projects (including the\n186\tlive session that produced this very report). Line `type` values seen and\n187\ttheir approximate frequency/purpose:\n188\t\n189\t| type | purpose |\n190\t|---|---|\n191\t| `user` | human or tool-result turn (see below) |\n192\t| `assistant` | model turn, full Anthropic response envelope |\n193\t| `system` | CLI-injected events: `subtype:\"local_command\"` (slash command + its stdout), `subtype:\"stop_hook_summary\"` (hook run report), `subtype:\"turn_duration\"` (timing telemetry) |\n194\t| `file-history-snapshot` | checkpoint of tracked file state at a point in the transcript, `{\"messageId\",\"snapshot\":{\"messageId\",\"trackedFileBackups\":{},\"timestamp\"},\"isSnapshotUpdate\"}` |\n195\t| `file-history-delta` | incremental version of the above (seen in larger sessions) |\n196\t| `attachment` | UI/context deltas: `deferred_tools_delta` (tool list changes), `agent_listing_delta` (available subagents), `skill_listing` (available skills) — purely informational, not needed for resume |\n197\t| `ai-title` | `{\"type\":\"ai-title\",\"aiTitle\":\"<short generated title>\",\"sessionId\":...}` — no `uuid`/`parentUuid`, not part of the conversation chain |\n198\t| `last-prompt` | `{\"type\":\"last-prompt\",\"leafUuid\":<uuid of last assistant msg>,\"sessionId\":...}` (sometimes also `\"lastPrompt\":\"<text>\"` in print-mode sessions) — bookkeeping marker only, **not required** for resume (tested explicitly, §2) |\n199\t| `mode` | `{\"type\":\"mode\",\"mode\":\"normal\",\"sessionId\":...}` — appears once near top of interactive sessions only (absent entirely in `-p` print-mode sessions) |\n200\t| `permission-mode` | `{\"type\":\"permission-mode\",\"permissionMode\":\"default\"|\"auto\",\"sessionId\":...}` — same as above |\n201\t| `queue-operation` | `{\"type\":\"queue-operation\",\"operation\":\"enqueue\"|\"dequeue\",\"content\":...,\"sessionId\":...,\"timestamp\":...}` — message-queue telemetry, also seen in print mode |\n202\t| `summary` | **not observed in any real file across all inspected sessions/versions** — either deprecated in favor of `ai-title`, or only appears after explicit `/compact`; do not rely on it existing |\n203\t\n204\tNone of `mode`, `permission-mode`, `file-history-snapshot`, `attachment`,\n205\t`ai-title`, `last-prompt`, `queue-operation`, `system` are required for\n206\t`--resume` to work (verified: fabricated files omitting all of them still\n207\tresumed correctly). Include them only for cosmetic fidelity if desired.\n208\t\n209\t### 4.1 `user` line, full shape (real example, human-typed)\n210\t\n211\t```json\n212\t{\"parentUuid\":null,\"isSidechain\":false,\"promptId\":\"fadd355f-...\",\"type\":\"user\",\n213\t \"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"plain string text\"},\n214\t \"uuid\":\"34fe510d-...\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-07-17T05:03:04.534Z\",\n215\t \"permissionMode\":\"default\",\"origin\":{\"kind\":\"human\"},\"promptSource\":\"typed\",\n216\t \"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/evintunador\",\n217\t \"sessionId\":\"36114e7a-...\",\"version\":\"2.1.212\",\"gitBranch\":\"HEAD\"}\n218\t```\n219\t\n220\t`origin.kind` observed values: `\"human\"`, `\"task-notification\"` (system-fired,\n221\te.g. a background agent finishing). `promptSource` observed: `\"typed\"`,\n222\t`\"sdk\"` (print-mode / `-p` invocations use `entrypoint:\"sdk-cli\"` and\n223\t`promptSource:\"sdk\"` instead of `\"cli\"`/`\"typed\"`).\n224\t\n225\t### 4.2 `assistant` line, full shape (real example)\n226\t\n227\t```json\n228\t{\"parentUuid\":\"...\",\"isSidechain\":false,\n229\t \"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-fable-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cd...\",\"type\":\"message\",\n230\t   \"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"...\"}],\n231\t   \"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\n232\t   \"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":6469,\n233\t     \"cache_read_input_tokens\":7304,\"output_tokens\":6,\n234\t     \"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\n235\t     \"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{...},\n236\t     \"inference_geo\":\"not_available\",\"iterations\":[{...}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\n237\t   \"diagnostics\":null},\n238\t \"requestId\":\"req_011Cd...\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"...\",\n239\t \"timestamp\":\"...\",\"effort\":\"high\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\n240\t \"cwd\":\"...\",\"sessionId\":\"...\",\"version\":\"2.1.215\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\"}\n241\t```\n242\t\n243\t`model` values observed: real model slugs (`claude-fable-5`,\n244\t`claude-haiku-4-5-20251001`). None of the `usage`/`diagnostics`/`effort`\n245\tfields are required for resume (§2) — they are cosmetic/telemetry.\n246\t`thinking` content blocks (extended thinking) were also observed, shape\n247\t`{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"...\",\"signature\":\"<long base64 blob>\"}` —\n248\tnot tested for resume necessity, presumed cosmetic/droppable like other\n249\tcontent blocks, but the `signature` is opaque and turnbridge cannot forge a\n250\tvalid one; **do not fabricate `thinking` blocks** — omit them (plain `text`\n251\tand `tool_use`/`tool_result` blocks are sufficient and were directly\n252\tverified to work).\n253\t\n254\t### 4.3 tool_use / tool_result pairing (verified with a FOREIGN/made-up tool)\n255\t\n256\tAssistant line's `message.content` includes a `tool_use` block:\n257\t```json\n258\t{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_...\",\"name\":\"<any-tool-name>\",\"input\":{...}}\n259\t```\n260\tNext `user` line's `message.content` (array, not string) includes the paired\n261\t`tool_result`:\n262\t```json\n263\t{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_...\",\"content\":\"<string or array>\"}\n264\t```\n265\t(`is_error: true` also a recognized optional field on `tool_result`, not\n266\ttested but standard Anthropic content-block shape.)\n267\t\n268\t**Confirmed: arbitrary/foreign tool names are accepted on resume.** A\n269\tfabricated pair using `\"name\":\"ForeignTool\"` (not a real Claude Code tool)\n270\tround-tripped correctly — the resumed session correctly quoted back a fact\n271\t(\"MANATEE2\") that only existed inside the fake tool's `tool_result` content.\n272\tClaude Code does not validate `tool_use.name` against its own tool registry\n273\twhen replaying history — historical tool calls are context only, never\n274\tre-executed. This means turnbridge can represent an entire foreign CLI's\n275\ttool-call history (e.g. Codex's `shell`, Cursor's `edit_file`, whatever) by\n276\tjust choosing readable `name` strings; no schema translation is required for\n277\tresume purposes. (This matches casr's own documented approach — see\n278\t`coerce_tool_input`, which just wraps non-object args, and the comment\n279\t\"historical tool calls are never re-executed — they are replayed as context\n280\tonly.\")\n281\t\n282\tReal `tool_result` user lines also carry a sibling top-level\n283\t`\"toolUseResult\": {...}` field (a structured, tool-specific echo of the\n284\tresult — e.g. for `Read` it's `{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\",\n285\t\"content\",\"numLines\",\"startLine\",\"totalLines\"}}`). This field is **not**\n286\trequired for resume — it appears to be a UI-rendering convenience only\n287\t(confirmed: our minimal fabricated tool_result lines omitted it entirely and\n288\tstill resumed with correct content recall).\n289\t\n290\t---\n291\t\n292\t## 5. Sidechains / subagents\n293\t\n294\tTwo independent mechanisms observed, both from the *live session that\n295\tproduced this report* (i.e. this very subagent run):\n296\t\n297\t1. **Inline `isSidechain: true`**: the field exists on every line and is\n298\t   `true` for subagent-authored lines, `false` otherwise. Searched across\n299\t   every real session file on disk (multiple projects) and found **zero**\n300\t   lines with `isSidechain:true` in the main `.jsonl` files — because:\n301\t2. **Subagents get their own files**, under\n302\t   `~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<parent-session-uuid>/subagents/`:\n303\t   - `agent-<hash>.jsonl` — the subagent's own transcript, structurally\n304\t     identical to a top-level session file, except every line has\n305\t     `isSidechain:true` and an added `\"agentId\":\"<hash>\"` field, and the\n306\t     first line's `parentUuid` is `null` (it's a fresh conversation, not a\n307\t     literal child of a parent-session uuid).\n308\t   - `agent-<hash>.meta.json` — sidecar metadata:\n309\t     `{\"agentType\":\"claude\",\"description\":\"<task description>\",\n310\t     \"toolUseId\":\"<toolu_... of the Agent tool call that spawned it>\",\n311\t     \"spawnDepth\":1,\"model\":\"sonnet\"}`\n312\t   - The parent session's main `.jsonl` links to the subagent only indirectly\n313\t     via the `Agent`/`Task` tool's `tool_use`/`tool_result` pair (the\n314\t     `tool_use_id` in `meta.json` matches the `tool_use.id` in the parent\n315\t     transcript); there is no direct file-path reference from the parent\n316\t     `.jsonl` to the subagent `.jsonl` observed in the plain-text content.\n317\t   - Not independently tested whether fabricating this subdirectory\n318\t     structure is necessary for `--resume` to work — **top-level resume\n319\t     works fine without any `subagents/` directory present** (all our\n320\t     successful fabrication tests had no such directory). Only fabricate it\n321\t     if turnbridge specifically needs subagent turns to show up distinctly\n322\t     in the transcript UI; otherwise flatten subagent output into the main\n323\t     file as ordinary `isSidechain:false` turns (untested but low-risk,\n324\t     since `isSidechain` itself was shown to be non-required, §2).\n325\t\n326\t---\n327\t\n328\t## 6. Divergence from casr (`cross_agent_session_resumer`)\n329\t\n330\tcasr's `write_session()` (`src/providers/claude_code.rs`) always emits, for\n331\tevery assistant line, a synthesized full envelope (`id`, `type:\"message\"`,\n332\t`model` or `\"unknown\"`, `stop_reason:\"end_turn\"`, `stop_sequence:null`,\n333\t`usage:{input_tokens:0,output_tokens:0}`), with this comment:\n334\t\n335\t> \"Claude Code's resume loader expects assistant messages to carry the full\n336\t> Anthropic message envelope... Without these fields, `claude --resume`\n337\t> hangs on load and reports 'Failed to resume session'.\"\n338\t\n339\t**We did not reproduce this on 2.1.215.** A bare assistant line\n340\t`{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",...}]}` (no `id`, `type`,\n341\t`model`, `stop_reason`, `usage` at all) resumed successfully via `-p`, no\n342\thang, correct context recall. Possible explanations: (a) casr targeted an\n343\tolder/different Claude Code version with stricter validation; (b) the hang\n344\tthey saw was specific to interactive mode (not `-p`/print mode, which is all\n345\twe could test non-interactively in this sandbox); (c) some other message\n346\tshape in their test data triggered it. **Recommendation: emit the full\n347\tenvelope anyway** (cheap, and defends against both interactive-mode\n348\tdifferences and future version changes) — see \"Recommended (defensive)\n349\tfields\" in §2.\n350\t\n351\tcasr's `project_dir_key` (all-non-alphanumeric → `-`) matches our directly\n352\tverified empirical result exactly — no divergence there.\n353\t\n354\t---\n355\t\n356\t## 7. Unknowns / risks\n357\t\n358\t- **Interactive (non-`-p`) resume was not tested** — this sandbox has no TUI.\n359\t  All verification used `claude --resume <id> -p \"<prompt>\" --max-turns 1`.\n360\t  It's possible interactive mode does additional validation (e.g. the casr\n361\t  \"hangs on load\" behavior) that print mode skips. Recommend a manual\n362\t  interactive smoke test (`claude --resume <id>` with no `-p`) before\n363\t  shipping turnbridge broadly.\n364\t- **Resume picker (`claude` / `/resume` with no ID) title source is\n365\t  inferred, not confirmed** — likely `ai-title` line content, falling back to\n366\t  first user message, but this is not independently verified against the\n367\t  actual interactive picker UI.\n368\t- **Timestamp format tolerance untested** — only millisecond-precision\n369\t  RFC3339 `...Z` strings were tried and confirmed required-present; unknown\n370\t  whether second-precision, offset-instead-of-Z, or out-of-order timestamps\n371\t  across lines are tolerated. Chronological, millisecond-precision,\n372\t  `Z`-suffixed timestamps are recommended to match real files exactly.\n373\t  Also unconfirmed whether timestamps must be internally consistent/ordered,\n374\t  or whether the file's own mtime vs. the JSON `timestamp` fields matter for\n375\t  session listing/sorting (e.g. in the resume picker's ordering).\n376\t  turnbridge should just use real, monotonically increasing timestamps to be\n377\t  safe.\n378\t- **Large/long sessions untested** — all fabricated test files were 2-3\n379\t  lines. Unknown if there's a line-count or byte-size behavior cliff, or\n380\t  whether very long fabricated histories affect context-loading correctly.\n381\t- **`sessionId` mismatch (inline field vs. filename) untested** — all our\n382\t  fabricated files either omitted the inline `sessionId` field or set it\n383\t  identically to the filename UUID. Behavior on mismatch is unknown;\n384\t  recommend always keeping them identical.\n385\t- **Duplicate/collided `uuid` values, or a `parentUuid` pointing to a\n386\t  nonexistent `uuid`, untested** — recommend generating fresh UUIDv4 per\n387\t  line and always chaining correctly (§2) to avoid undefined behavior.\n388\t- **`--resume` cwd-scoping workaround**: since resume is strictly scoped to\n389\t  the launch cwd's encoded project directory (§3), turnbridge must always\n390\t  `cd` (or set subprocess cwd) to match before invoking `claude --resume`.\n391\t  There is no `--project-dir`-style override flag observed in `claude\n392\t  --help` output for `--resume` specifically to bypass this.\n393\t- **Version string in fabricated lines**: casr uses the literal string\n394\t  `\"casr\"` as `version` and this apparently works for them; we recommend\n395\t  using a real, currently-installed version string (`\"2.1.215\"`) instead,\n396\t  purely for cosmetic consistency with real sessions — not confirmed to be\n397\t  functionally required either way (`version` was in the \"verified NOT\n398\t  required\" list, §2).\n399\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CTWZ3scK938ncWV5Y6cXTC","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"97ecff56-1b8e-4a1f-b47c-def03274d960","promptId":"5baaa4e1-1fa5-494b-8425-5ca6e70ddfd1","sessionId":"6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","slug":"hazy-floating-cupcake","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"97ecff56-1b8e-4a1f-b47c-def03274d960","timestamp":"2026-07-21T21:21:06.164Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"# Claude Code Native Session File Format (reverse-engineered)\n\nGoal: let `turnbridge` fabricate a `.jsonl` file that `claude --resume <session-id>`\nwill natively load, with full prior conversation context visible to the model.\n\n**Pinned version tested against: `2.1.215` (Claude Code)**, installed at\n`~/.local/bin/claude` (Mach-O binary, other builds `2.1.212`/`2.1.214` present\non disk but not the active one — behavior below is specific to `2.1.215` and\nshould be re-verified if the pinned version changes).\n\nAll findings below were verified empirically by hand-writing files into\n`~/.claude/projects/` and running `claude --resume <uuid> -p \"...\" --max-turns 1`\nfrom a matching scratch cwd, then observing whether the model could recall a\nplanted fact from the fabricated prior turns. Cross-checked against the OSS\nproject `Dicklesworthstone/cross_agent_session_resumer`\n(`src/providers/claude_code.rs`), which independently reverse-engineered the\nsame format for a different Claude Code version — see \"Divergence from casr\"\nbelow for the one place our findings differ.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Directory and filename rules\n\nSession files live at:\n\n```\n~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<session-uuid>.jsonl\n```\n\n**Encoding rule** (confirmed empirically, and matches casr's `project_dir_key`):\nevery ASCII character in the absolute cwd path that is **not alphanumeric**\n(`[A-Za-z0-9]`) is replaced with `-`. This includes `/`, `.`, `_`, spaces, etc.\nExisting dashes pass through unchanged, so paths with directory names that\nalready contain dashes are ambiguous with slash-encoding (e.g. a project dir\nliterally named `-Users-foo` is indistinguishable from `/Users/foo`) — this\nis a real property of the real system, not a bug to route around.\n\nVerified example:\n- cwd `/private/tmp/.../dot.test_dir` → dir key\n  `-private-tmp-...-dot-test-dir` (both `.` and `_` became `-`).\n- cwd `/Users/evintunador/dev/ds4-gateway` → dir key\n  `-Users-evintunador-dev-ds4-gateway` (the existing `-` in `ds4-gateway`\n  survives unchanged, confirming char-by-char non-alnum replacement rather\n  than some smarter path-segment join).\n\nImplementation to use in turnbridge (Python-ish):\n```python\ndef encode_project_dir(cwd_abs_path: str) -> str:\n    return \"\".join(c if c.isalnum() else \"-\" for c in cwd_abs_path)\n```\n\n**Filename ↔ sessionId**: the file is named `<session-uuid>.jsonl` where\n`<session-uuid>` is a standard UUIDv4 string. This filename is the ID that\n`claude --resume <id>` looks up. **The lookup is scoped to the directory\nimplied by the CURRENT working directory the `claude` process is launched\nfrom** — see §3, this is the single most important operational constraint.\n\nThere is also an optional sibling directory `<session-uuid>/` (no `.jsonl`\nextension) next to the file, used for subagent transcripts — see §5. It is\nnot required for a basic resume to work.\n\nA per-project `memory/` directory also exists alongside the session files;\nirrelevant to fabrication.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Minimal working line schema (verified)\n\nThe absolute minimum that reliably works, discovered by iterative field\nremoval against a real `claude --resume ... -p ...` round trip:\n\n**Required per line:**\n- `type`: `\"user\"` or `\"assistant\"` (other types like `mode`,\n  `permission-mode`, `file-history-snapshot`, `attachment`, `system`,\n  `queue-operation`, `ai-title`, `last-prompt` are cosmetic/optional — see §4)\n- `parentUuid`: `null` on the first conversation line, else the `uuid` of the\n  immediately preceding conversation line, forming a linked list back to\n  `null`. **This is not optional bookkeeping — it is load-bearing.** Claude\n  Code's resume loader reconstructs the conversation by starting at the\n  **last line in the file** and walking `parentUuid` backwards. Confirmed via\n  this exact tool-emitted warning when `parentUuid` was omitted from every\n  line: *\"Resume transcript has 2 user/assistant records but none carry\n  parentUuid links; only 1 reached the resumed conversation. Conversation\n  reconstruction walks parentUuid from the last record, so unlinked records\n  are dropped — the file's producer must chain records (parentUuid null on\n  the first, the previous record's uuid on each subsequent one).\"* Only the\n  final line survived; everything before it was silently dropped from\n  context.\n- `message`: `{\"role\": \"user\"|\"assistant\", \"content\": ...}` — content is\n  either a plain string, or an array of content blocks (`text`, `tool_use`,\n  `tool_result`; see §4.3).\n- `uuid`: unique UUIDv4 for this line, used as the `parentUuid` target of the\n  next line.\n- `timestamp`: **required** — ISO-8601 string (`\"2026-07-19T07:00:00.000Z\"`\n  format observed; exact format tolerance not fully probed, but real files\n  always use millisecond-precision `Z`-suffixed RFC3339). Confirmed required:\n  a file with `parentUuid` chaining present but `timestamp` fully omitted\n  produced `No conversation found with session ID: <uuid>` — i.e. the file\n  was rejected as invalid/unrecognized wholesale, not just missing context.\n\n**Verified NOT required** (all of these were omitted simultaneously in a\nsuccessful test and the fabricated session still loaded with full context\nrecall): `sessionId` (inline field), `cwd`, `version`, `gitBranch`,\n`userType`, `isSidechain`, `promptId`, `requestId`, and — for assistant\nmessages — the entire Anthropic response envelope (`model`, `id`, `type`,\n`stop_reason`, `usage`, etc. can all be absent; `message` can be just\n`{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[...]}`) . This includes assistant messages\nwhose content is a bare `tool_use` block with no surrounding envelope.\n\nA trailing non-conversation line (e.g. `{\"type\":\"last-prompt\", ...}`) after\nthe last `assistant` line does not interfere with the backward walk — the\nloader still correctly finds the true leaf.\n\n**Concrete minimal working example** (verified end-to-end: resuming this file\nand asking \"what is the secret code word?\" correctly returned `PANGOLIN9`):\n\n```jsonl\n{\"parentUuid\":null,\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"The secret code word for this test is PANGOLIN9.\"},\"uuid\":\"33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-07-19T07:05:00.000Z\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Got it, the secret code word is PANGOLIN9.\"}]},\"uuid\":\"44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-07-19T07:05:01.000Z\"}\n```\n\nPlaced at `~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<uuid>.jsonl`, then from that\nexact cwd: `claude --resume <uuid> -p \"...\" --max-turns 1`.\n\n### Recommended (defensive) fields to actually emit\n\nEven though the above is the *true minimum*, turnbridge should emit richer\nlines close to what real Claude Code produces, for two reasons: (a) the casr\nproject found that on the version(s) *it* targeted, omitting the full\nassistant envelope caused `claude --resume` to hang / report \"Failed to\nresume session\" (see §6 \"Divergence from casr\") — our 2.1.215 didn't\nreproduce that, but future versions might reintroduce stricter validation;\n(b) richer lines make the fabricated session indistinguishable from a real\none in the resume picker / `/resume` UI and avoid weird cosmetic gaps (no\ngit branch shown, etc). Recommended per-line fields:\n\n```\nparentUuid, isSidechain:false, userType:\"external\", entrypoint:\"cli\",\ncwd:<abs path>, sessionId:<uuid, matches filename>, version:\"2.1.215\",\ngitBranch:<real branch or \"HEAD\">, type, message, uuid, timestamp,\npromptId:<uuid> (user lines only), requestId:<string> (assistant lines only)\n```\n\nFor assistant lines, also include the full envelope inside `message`:\n`model`, `id` (format `msg_...`), `type:\"message\"`, `stop_reason:\"end_turn\"`,\n`stop_sequence:null`, `usage:{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,...}`. This\ncosts nothing and removes a known risk.\n\n---\n\n## 3. What `claude --resume` needs — cwd scoping (important, verified)\n\n`claude --resume <uuid>` **only works when the current working directory\nof the `claude` process encodes to the same project directory the session\nfile lives in.** Verified directly:\n\n- Fabricated session at `~/.claude/projects/<encoded of cwd A>/<uuid>.jsonl`,\n  run `claude --resume <uuid> ...` from cwd A → **works**, full context loads.\n- Same file, run `claude --resume <uuid> ...` from `~` (a different,\n  non-matching cwd) → **fails immediately**: `No conversation found with\n  session ID: <uuid>`.\n\n**Implication for turnbridge**: it must invoke `claude --resume <id>` with\nthe process cwd set to the exact original/intended project path (or fabricate\nthe session under whatever cwd it plans to actually launch `claude` from) —\nthere is no global session ID lookup across projects. The `--resume` flag\ndoes not search all of `~/.claude/projects/*`; it derives the expected\ndirectory from the launch cwd and looks only there.\n\n**Resume picker / `/resume` UI**: not directly testable non-interactively in\nthis sandbox (no TUI). Based on file evidence: session titles shown in the\npicker most likely come from the `{\"type\":\"ai-title\",\"aiTitle\":\"...\",\n\"sessionId\":\"...\"}` line (a short LLM-generated title, observed identical\nacross all lines with that type in a given file, so likely just periodically\nrewritten/duplicated) with fallback to the first user message if absent —\nthis is an inference from file structure, **not independently confirmed by\nan interactive test**; treat as a risk/unknown (see §7). The `ai-title` line\nis cosmetic only — omitting it did not break `--resume <id>` (direct-ID\nresume never showed title UI in `-p` mode).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Full observed schema (real files, version 2.1.212–2.1.215)\n\nObserved by inspecting real files across 4 different projects (including the\nlive session that produced this very report). Line `type` values seen and\ntheir approximate frequency/purpose:\n\n| type | purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `user` | human or tool-result turn (see below) |\n| `assistant` | model turn, full Anthropic response envelope |\n| `system` | CLI-injected events: `subtype:\"local_command\"` (slash command + its stdout), `subtype:\"stop_hook_summary\"` (hook run report), `subtype:\"turn_duration\"` (timing telemetry) |\n| `file-history-snapshot` | checkpoint of tracked file state at a point in the transcript, `{\"messageId\",\"snapshot\":{\"messageId\",\"trackedFileBackups\":{},\"timestamp\"},\"isSnapshotUpdate\"}` |\n| `file-history-delta` | incremental version of the above (seen in larger sessions) |\n| `attachment` | UI/context deltas: `deferred_tools_delta` (tool list changes), `agent_listing_delta` (available subagents), `skill_listing` (available skills) — purely informational, not needed for resume |\n| `ai-title` | `{\"type\":\"ai-title\",\"aiTitle\":\"<short generated title>\",\"sessionId\":...}` — no `uuid`/`parentUuid`, not part of the conversation chain |\n| `last-prompt` | `{\"type\":\"last-prompt\",\"leafUuid\":<uuid of last assistant msg>,\"sessionId\":...}` (sometimes also `\"lastPrompt\":\"<text>\"` in print-mode sessions) — bookkeeping marker only, **not required** for resume (tested explicitly, §2) |\n| `mode` | `{\"type\":\"mode\",\"mode\":\"normal\",\"sessionId\":...}` — appears once near top of interactive sessions only (absent entirely in `-p` print-mode sessions) |\n| `permission-mode` | `{\"type\":\"permission-mode\",\"permissionMode\":\"default\"|\"auto\",\"sessionId\":...}` — same as above |\n| `queue-operation` | `{\"type\":\"queue-operation\",\"operation\":\"enqueue\"|\"dequeue\",\"content\":...,\"sessionId\":...,\"timestamp\":...}` — message-queue telemetry, also seen in print mode |\n| `summary` | **not observed in any real file across all inspected sessions/versions** — either deprecated in favor of `ai-title`, or only appears after explicit `/compact`; do not rely on it existing |\n\nNone of `mode`, `permission-mode`, `file-history-snapshot`, `attachment`,\n`ai-title`, `last-prompt`, `queue-operation`, `system` are required for\n`--resume` to work (verified: fabricated files omitting all of them still\nresumed correctly). Include them only for cosmetic fidelity if desired.\n\n### 4.1 `user` line, full shape (real example, human-typed)\n\n```json\n{\"parentUuid\":null,\"isSidechain\":false,\"promptId\":\"fadd355f-...\",\"type\":\"user\",\n \"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"plain string text\"},\n \"uuid\":\"34fe510d-...\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-07-17T05:03:04.534Z\",\n \"permissionMode\":\"default\",\"origin\":{\"kind\":\"human\"},\"promptSource\":\"typed\",\n \"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/evintunador\",\n \"sessionId\":\"36114e7a-...\",\"version\":\"2.1.212\",\"gitBranch\":\"HEAD\"}\n```\n\n`origin.kind` observed values: `\"human\"`, `\"task-notification\"` (system-fired,\ne.g. a background agent finishing). `promptSource` observed: `\"typed\"`,\n`\"sdk\"` (print-mode / `-p` invocations use `entrypoint:\"sdk-cli\"` and\n`promptSource:\"sdk\"` instead of `\"cli\"`/`\"typed\"`).\n\n### 4.2 `assistant` line, full shape (real example)\n\n```json\n{\"parentUuid\":\"...\",\"isSidechain\":false,\n \"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-fable-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cd...\",\"type\":\"message\",\n   \"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"...\"}],\n   \"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\n   \"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":6469,\n     \"cache_read_input_tokens\":7304,\"output_tokens\":6,\n     \"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\n     \"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{...},\n     \"inference_geo\":\"not_available\",\"iterations\":[{...}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\n   \"diagnostics\":null},\n \"requestId\":\"req_011Cd...\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"...\",\n \"timestamp\":\"...\",\"effort\":\"high\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\n \"cwd\":\"...\",\"sessionId\":\"...\",\"version\":\"2.1.215\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\"}\n```\n\n`model` values observed: real model slugs (`claude-fable-5`,\n`claude-haiku-4-5-20251001`). None of the `usage`/`diagnostics`/`effort`\nfields are required for resume (§2) — they are cosmetic/telemetry.\n`thinking` content blocks (extended thinking) were also observed, shape\n`{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"...\",\"signature\":\"<long base64 blob>\"}` —\nnot tested for resume necessity, presumed cosmetic/droppable like other\ncontent blocks, but the `signature` is opaque and turnbridge cannot forge a\nvalid one; **do not fabricate `thinking` blocks** — omit them (plain `text`\nand `tool_use`/`tool_result` blocks are sufficient and were directly\nverified to work).\n\n### 4.3 tool_use / tool_result pairing (verified with a FOREIGN/made-up tool)\n\nAssistant line's `message.content` includes a `tool_use` block:\n```json\n{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_...\",\"name\":\"<any-tool-name>\",\"input\":{...}}\n```\nNext `user` line's `message.content` (array, not string) includes the paired\n`tool_result`:\n```json\n{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_...\",\"content\":\"<string or array>\"}\n```\n(`is_error: true` also a recognized optional field on `tool_result`, not\ntested but standard Anthropic content-block shape.)\n\n**Confirmed: arbitrary/foreign tool names are accepted on resume.** A\nfabricated pair using `\"name\":\"ForeignTool\"` (not a real Claude Code tool)\nround-tripped correctly — the resumed session correctly quoted back a fact\n(\"MANATEE2\") that only existed inside the fake tool's `tool_result` content.\nClaude Code does not validate `tool_use.name` against its own tool registry\nwhen replaying history — historical tool calls are context only, never\nre-executed. This means turnbridge can represent an entire foreign CLI's\ntool-call history (e.g. Codex's `shell`, Cursor's `edit_file`, whatever) by\njust choosing readable `name` strings; no schema translation is required for\nresume purposes. (This matches casr's own documented approach — see\n`coerce_tool_input`, which just wraps non-object args, and the comment\n\"historical tool calls are never re-executed — they are replayed as context\nonly.\")\n\nReal `tool_result` user lines also carry a sibling top-level\n`\"toolUseResult\": {...}` field (a structured, tool-specific echo of the\nresult — e.g. for `Read` it's `{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\",\n\"content\",\"numLines\",\"startLine\",\"totalLines\"}}`). This field is **not**\nrequired for resume — it appears to be a UI-rendering convenience only\n(confirmed: our minimal fabricated tool_result lines omitted it entirely and\nstill resumed with correct content recall).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Sidechains / subagents\n\nTwo independent mechanisms observed, both from the *live session that\nproduced this report* (i.e. this very subagent run):\n\n1. **Inline `isSidechain: true`**: the field exists on every line and is\n   `true` for subagent-authored lines, `false` otherwise. Searched across\n   every real session file on disk (multiple projects) and found **zero**\n   lines with `isSidechain:true` in the main `.jsonl` files — because:\n2. **Subagents get their own files**, under\n   `~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<parent-session-uuid>/subagents/`:\n   - `agent-<hash>.jsonl` — the subagent's own transcript, structurally\n     identical to a top-level session file, except every line has\n     `isSidechain:true` and an added `\"agentId\":\"<hash>\"` field, and the\n     first line's `parentUuid` is `null` (it's a fresh conversation, not a\n     literal child of a parent-session uuid).\n   - `agent-<hash>.meta.json` — sidecar metadata:\n     `{\"agentType\":\"claude\",\"description\":\"<task description>\",\n     \"toolUseId\":\"<toolu_... of the Agent tool call that spawned it>\",\n     \"spawnDepth\":1,\"model\":\"sonnet\"}`\n   - The parent session's main `.jsonl` links to the subagent only indirectly\n     via the `Agent`/`Task` tool's `tool_use`/`tool_result` pair (the\n     `tool_use_id` in `meta.json` matches the `tool_use.id` in the parent\n     transcript); there is no direct file-path reference from the parent\n     `.jsonl` to the subagent `.jsonl` observed in the plain-text content.\n   - Not independently tested whether fabricating this subdirectory\n     structure is necessary for `--resume` to work — **top-level resume\n     works fine without any `subagents/` directory present** (all our\n     successful fabrication tests had no such directory). Only fabricate it\n     if turnbridge specifically needs subagent turns to show up distinctly\n     in the transcript UI; otherwise flatten subagent output into the main\n     file as ordinary `isSidechain:false` turns (untested but low-risk,\n     since `isSidechain` itself was shown to be non-required, §2).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Divergence from casr (`cross_agent_session_resumer`)\n\ncasr's `write_session()` (`src/providers/claude_code.rs`) always emits, for\nevery assistant line, a synthesized full envelope (`id`, `type:\"message\"`,\n`model` or `\"unknown\"`, `stop_reason:\"end_turn\"`, `stop_sequence:null`,\n`usage:{input_tokens:0,output_tokens:0}`), with this comment:\n\n> \"Claude Code's resume loader expects assistant messages to carry the full\n> Anthropic message envelope... Without these fields, `claude --resume`\n> hangs on load and reports 'Failed to resume session'.\"\n\n**We did not reproduce this on 2.1.215.** A bare assistant line\n`{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",...}]}` (no `id`, `type`,\n`model`, `stop_reason`, `usage` at all) resumed successfully via `-p`, no\nhang, correct context recall. Possible explanations: (a) casr targeted an\nolder/different Claude Code version with stricter validation; (b) the hang\nthey saw was specific to interactive mode (not `-p`/print mode, which is all\nwe could test non-interactively in this sandbox); (c) some other message\nshape in their test data triggered it. **Recommendation: emit the full\nenvelope anyway** (cheap, and defends against both interactive-mode\ndifferences and future version changes) — see \"Recommended (defensive)\nfields\" in §2.\n\ncasr's `project_dir_key` (all-non-alphanumeric → `-`) matches our directly\nverified empirical result exactly — no divergence there.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Unknowns / risks\n\n- **Interactive (non-`-p`) resume was not tested** — this sandbox has no TUI.\n  All verification used `claude --resume <id> -p \"<prompt>\" --max-turns 1`.\n  It's possible interactive mode does additional validation (e.g. the casr\n  \"hangs on load\" behavior) that print mode skips. Recommend a manual\n  interactive smoke test (`claude --resume <id>` with no `-p`) before\n  shipping turnbridge broadly.\n- **Resume picker (`claude` / `/resume` with no ID) title source is\n  inferred, not confirmed** — likely `ai-title` line content, falling back to\n  first user message, but this is not independently verified against the\n  actual interactive picker UI.\n- **Timestamp format tolerance untested** — only millisecond-precision\n  RFC3339 `...Z` strings were tried and confirmed required-present; unknown\n  whether second-precision, offset-instead-of-Z, or out-of-order timestamps\n  across lines are tolerated. Chronological, millisecond-precision,\n  `Z`-suffixed timestamps are recommended to match real files exactly.\n  Also unconfirmed whether timestamps must be internally consistent/ordered,\n  or whether the file's own mtime vs. the JSON `timestamp` fields matter for\n  session listing/sorting (e.g. in the resume picker's ordering).\n  turnbridge should just use real, monotonically increasing timestamps to be\n  safe.\n- **Large/long sessions untested** — all fabricated test files were 2-3\n  lines. Unknown if there's a line-count or byte-size behavior cliff, or\n  whether very long fabricated histories affect context-loading correctly.\n- **`sessionId` mismatch (inline field vs. filename) untested** — all our\n  fabricated files either omitted the inline `sessionId` field or set it\n  identically to the filename UUID. Behavior on mismatch is unknown;\n  recommend always keeping them identical.\n- **Duplicate/collided `uuid` values, or a `parentUuid` pointing to a\n  nonexistent `uuid`, untested** — recommend generating fresh UUIDv4 per\n  line and always chaining correctly (§2) to avoid undefined behavior.\n- **`--resume` cwd-scoping workaround**: since resume is strictly scoped to\n  the launch cwd's encoded project directory (§3), turnbridge must always\n  `cd` (or set subprocess cwd) to match before invoking `claude --resume`.\n  There is no `--project-dir`-style override flag observed in `claude\n  --help` output for `--resume` specifically to bypass this.\n- **Version string in fabricated lines**: casr uses the literal string\n  `\"casr\"` as `version` and this apparently works for them; we recommend\n  using a real, currently-installed version string (`\"2.1.215\"`) instead,\n  purely for cosmetic consistency with real sessions — not confirmed to be\n  functionally required either way (`version` was in the \"verified NOT\n  required\" list, §2).\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/docs/specs/claude-session-format.md","numLines":399,"startLine":1,"totalLines":399},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"1033bc4b-5bbd-4541-a724-2371b809b3a7","version":"2.1.216"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T02:16:45.306Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Codex CLI native \"rollout\" session file format — reverse-engineering spec\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: enable `turnbridge` to FABRICATE a `~/.codex/sessions/.../rollout-*.jsonl` file that\n4\tCodex's native `resume` (both interactive TUI and `codex exec resume`) will load with the\n5\tfabricated prior conversation visible to the model.\n6\t\n7\tPinned environment:\n8\t- `codex --version` → **codex-cli 0.144.6** (some session files on disk were written by 0.144.5;\n9\t  format is stable across that point release).\n10\t- Tested on macOS (darwin), zsh shell, `$CODEX_HOME` = `~/.codex` (default).\n11\t\n12\tEverything in this document was verified empirically against this exact build on 2026-07-18/19,\n13\tby hand-writing fabricated rollout files and confirming Codex resumed them with visible prior\n14\tcontext (see \"Verification experiment\" below). It is not derived solely from docs or other\n15\tprojects' guesses.\n16\t\n17\t---\n18\t\n19\t## 1. Resume command forms\n20\t\n21\t### Interactive (TUI)\n22\t```\n23\tcodex resume [SESSION_ID] [PROMPT] [OPTIONS]\n24\t```\n25\t- `SESSION_ID` — a UUID (any version; v4 works, not just v7 — see §6) **or** a session *name*.\n26\t  If parseable as a UUID it's treated as an id, not a name.\n27\t- If `SESSION_ID` is omitted: shows an interactive **picker** listing recorded sessions.\n28\t  - The picker **filters by current working directory** by default (only sessions whose\n29\t    `session_meta.cwd` matches — exact mechanism not further probed, but `--all` explicitly\n30\t    \"disables cwd filtering\").\n31\t  - `--last` bypasses the picker and resumes the most recently recorded session (still cwd\n32\t    -filtered unless combined with `--all`).\n33\t  - `--all` shows all sessions regardless of cwd, and adds a CWD column.\n34\t  - `--include-non-interactive` — only meaningful for the picker; non-interactive (`exec`-\n35\t    originated) sessions are excluded from the picker/`--last` selection unless passed.\n36\t- Requires a real TTY. In a non-tty environment it fails immediately with\n37\t  `Error: stdin is not a terminal` (verified) — this happens *before* any session-content\n38\t  validation as far as we could tell; when run inside a pty (via `script`), it proceeded past\n39\t  that check and began rendering the TUI (terminal capability query escape sequences observed),\n40\t  i.e. it did **not** reject our fabricated file.\n41\t- `[PROMPT]` optional — if given, is sent as the first new user turn immediately after resuming.\n42\t\n43\t### Non-interactive (scriptable) — **this is the form to target for programmatic fabrication/verification**\n44\t```\n45\tcodex exec resume [SESSION_ID] [PROMPT] [OPTIONS]\n46\t```\n47\t- Same `SESSION_ID` semantics as above (`--last` and `--all` also present).\n48\t- `--json` — print NDJSON events to stdout (`thread.started`, `turn.started`,\n49\t  `item.completed`, `turn.completed`, ...). This is the reliable way to script verification.\n50\t- `--skip-git-repo-check` — needed if invoked outside a git repo, or if the fabricated file's\n51\t  target cwd differs from where `codex` was actually launched.\n52\t- `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox` — needed if you want the resumed agent to\n53\t  actually run shell commands without prompting (used only for one diagnostic test below).\n54\t- **Important finding**: resume-by-id works from **any** current working directory — cwd\n55\t  filtering only applies to the *picker*, not to direct-id resume. The actual working directory\n56\t  the resumed agent operates in is the **process's real cwd at invocation time** (i.e. wherever\n57\t  you ran `codex exec resume` from), **not** the fabricated `session_meta.cwd` value. We\n58\t  verified this directly: resumed a session whose fabricated `cwd` pointed at a scratch repo,\n59\t  invoked `codex exec resume` from `/tmp` instead, and asked the agent to run `pwd` — it printed\n60\t  `/tmp`. So `session_meta.cwd` is effectively cosmetic/display metadata (used for the picker's\n61\t  cwd filter and any UI that shows \"resumed session in <dir>\"), not authoritative for execution.\n62\t  Implication for turnbridge: set `cwd` to whatever you want *displayed*/matched by the picker,\n63\t  but don't rely on it to sandbox or root the resumed agent — that's controlled by the real\n64\t  invocation cwd / `-C`/`--cd`.\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t## 2. File/directory naming rules\n69\t\n70\t```\n71\t~/.codex/sessions/<YYYY>/<MM>/<DD>/rollout-<local-timestamp>-<uuid>.jsonl\n72\t```\n73\t- `$CODEX_HOME` defaults to `~/.codex`; sessions live under `$CODEX_HOME/sessions`.\n74\t- `<YYYY>/<MM>/<DD>` is the **local** date (not UTC) at session-creation time. Verified: local\n75\t  clock was `2026-07-18 23:59 PDT` while the in-file UTC timestamps read `2026-07-19T06:59...Z`,\n76\t  and the file was correctly placed under `.../2026/07/18/`.\n77\t- `<local-timestamp>` in the filename is local time, format `YYYY-MM-DDTHH-MM-SS` (colons\n78\t  replaced with hyphens, seconds precision, no milliseconds, no offset suffix).\n79\t  Example real filename: `rollout-2026-07-18T16-16-52-019f7784-b1c3-7561-9237-586478b394d2.jsonl`.\n80\t- `<uuid>` is the session id, **and must exactly match** `session_meta.payload.id` /\n81\t  `.session_id` inside the file (this is how `resume <id>` locates the file — presumably an\n82\t  index/scan over the sessions dir matching this literal id, since resume-by-id worked\n83\t  immediately with no separate registration step).\n84\t- UUID format: does not need to be UUIDv7. A real random UUIDv4 was accepted and resumed\n85\t  successfully. (Real Codex-generated ids happen to be UUIDv7 — time-ordered, e.g.\n86\t  `019f776a-c87a-7990-b54f-8040658be7fd` — presumably just because that's what Codex's own id\n87\t  generator emits, not because resume validates the version nibble.)\n88\t- All timestamps *inside* the JSONL (`timestamp` fields) are UTC, ISO-8601 with `Z` suffix and\n89\t  millisecond precision, e.g. `\"2026-07-18T22:48:34.755Z\"`.\n90\t- One session = one file. Lines are newline-delimited JSON objects (JSONL), UTF-8. Some real\n91\t  files contain raw literal control characters inside string values in a way that breaks strict\n92\t  parsers like `jq` (`jq: parse error: Invalid string: control characters...`) but that Python's\n93\t  `json.loads` parses fine — i.e. Codex's own writer does not always escape control bytes\n94\t  (likely raw terminal/ANSI bytes captured from shell output pass through un-escaped in some\n95\t  version of the writer). **When fabricating**, always produce properly-escaped JSON (use a real\n96\t  JSON serializer) — there's no reason to reproduce that quirk, and doing so risks a genuinely\n97\t  invalid file.\n98\t\n99\t---\n100\t\n101\t## 3. Minimal fabricated schema that VERIFIABLY WORKS\n102\t\n103\tThis is the maximally stripped-down version we tested and confirmed loads correctly via\n104\t`codex exec resume` (model echoed back a planted \"secret code word\", proving it read the\n105\tfabricated history) — and via `codex resume` (TUI started rendering without error, using a pty).\n106\t\n107\tThree lines total. `session_meta` needs only 5 payload fields; each conversation turn needs only\n108\ta bare `response_item` message.\n109\t\n110\t```jsonl\n111\t{\"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"type\": \"session_meta\", \"payload\": {\"id\": \"019f792d-38dc-7cc7-a2d1-072e8cb04002\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"cwd\": \"/some/dir\", \"originator\": \"codex_exec\", \"cli_version\": \"0.144.6\"}}\n112\t{\"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"type\": \"response_item\", \"payload\": {\"type\": \"message\", \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [{\"type\": \"input_text\", \"text\": \"The secret code word for this test is PLUMFISH42. Remember it.\"}]}}\n113\t{\"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"type\": \"response_item\", \"payload\": {\"type\": \"message\", \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": [{\"type\": \"output_text\", \"text\": \"Got it, the secret code word is PLUMFISH42.\"}]}}\n114\t```\n115\t\n116\tVerified NOT required for `exec resume` to work and see full context:\n117\t- `session_meta.payload.session_id` (only `.id` was present; both usually appear in real files —\n118\t  recommend including both for safety/forward-compat, but `.id` alone sufficed in our test)\n119\t- `source`, `thread_source`, `model_provider`, `history_mode`, `context_window`, `git`,\n120\t  `base_instructions` — all omitted in the minimal test and it still worked.\n121\t- No `turn_context`, `world_state`, or `event_msg` lines at all are required for the resumed\n122\t  model to see/use prior conversation content. Those record types appear to be informational /\n123\t  UI-facing (token counters, sandbox/approval settings snapshots, TUI rendering aids) rather than\n124\t  part of what gets replayed into the model's context. **`response_item` lines are the actual\n125\t  conversation state that gets replayed.**\n126\t- `response_item.payload.message.id` — not required (real assistant messages have one, e.g.\n127\t  `\"msg_0850eb109f...\"`, but omitting it did not break resume).\n128\t\n129\tRecommended (not strictly required, but cheap and matches real files, reduces risk of edge\n130\tcases in other code paths like the picker or future Codex versions that might start requiring\n131\tthem):\n132\t```jsonl\n133\t{\"timestamp\": \"<UTC-ISO-ms-Z>\", \"type\": \"session_meta\", \"payload\": {\n134\t  \"session_id\": \"<uuid>\", \"id\": \"<uuid>\", \"timestamp\": \"<UTC-ISO-ms-Z>\",\n135\t  \"cwd\": \"<display-dir>\", \"originator\": \"codex_exec\", \"cli_version\": \"0.144.6\",\n136\t  \"source\": \"exec\", \"thread_source\": \"user\", \"model_provider\": \"openai\",\n137\t  \"history_mode\": \"legacy\"\n138\t}}\n139\t```\n140\t`history_mode` was `\"legacy\"` on every real file inspected (only value observed).\n141\t\n142\t---\n143\t\n144\t## 4. Full observed schema (from real files)\n145\t\n146\tTop-level line shape is always: `{\"timestamp\": <UTC ISO>, \"type\": <string>, \"payload\": {...}}`.\n147\tObserved `type` values: `session_meta`, `event_msg`, `response_item`, `turn_context`,\n148\t`world_state`, `compacted`.\n149\t\n150\t### 4.1 `session_meta` (always line 1)\n151\tFull field set seen across files (union; not all present in every file):\n152\t```\n153\tpayload.session_id        string uuid\n154\tpayload.id                string uuid   (== session_id; both present in normal (non-fabricated) files)\n155\tpayload.timestamp         UTC ISO ms\n156\tpayload.cwd                string path\n157\tpayload.originator         \"codex-tui\" | \"codex_exec\"\n158\tpayload.cli_version         e.g. \"0.144.6\"\n159\tpayload.source              \"cli\" | \"exec\" | {\"subagent\": {\"thread_spawn\": {...}}}  (object form for spawned sub-agent sessions)\n160\tpayload.thread_source       \"user\" | \"subagent\"\n161\tpayload.model_provider      \"openai\"\n162\tpayload.base_instructions   {\"text\": \"<full system prompt, ~18KB>\"}\n163\tpayload.history_mode        \"legacy\"\n164\tpayload.context_window      {\"window_id\": \"<uuid>\"}\n165\tpayload.git                 {\"commit_hash\": str, \"branch\": str, \"repository_url\": str}   (present only when cwd is inside a git repo)\n166\tpayload.forked_from_id      uuid   (only on forked/sub-agent sessions)\n167\tpayload.parent_thread_id    uuid   (only on forked/sub-agent sessions)\n168\tpayload.agent_nickname      string (only on sub-agent sessions, e.g. \"Lagrange\")\n169\tpayload.agent_path          string (only on sub-agent sessions, e.g. \"/root/quality_audit\")\n170\tpayload.multi_agent_version \"v2\"   (only on sub-agent sessions)\n171\t```\n172\t\n173\t### 4.2 `event_msg` — UI/telemetry events, NOT required for context replay\n174\tObserved `payload.type` values and shapes:\n175\t- `task_started` — `{turn_id, started_at, model_context_window, collaboration_mode_kind}`\n176\t- `user_message` — `{message, images, local_images, text_elements}`\n177\t- `agent_message` — `{message, phase: \"final_answer\", memory_citation}`\n178\t- `token_count` — `{info: {total_token_usage:{...}, last_token_usage:{...}, model_context_window}, rate_limits: {...}}`\n179\t- `patch_apply_end` — `{call_id, turn_id, stdout, stderr, success, changes: {<path>: {type: \"add\"|..., content}}, status}`\n180\t- `task_complete` — `{turn_id, last_agent_message, completed_at, duration_ms, time_to_first_token_ms}`\n181\t\n182\t### 4.3 `response_item` — the actual conversation payload replayed to the model\n183\t`payload.type` variants observed:\n184\t- **`message`** — `{type:\"message\", role: \"developer\"|\"user\"|\"assistant\", id?, content: [...], phase?, internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough?: {turn_id}}`.\n185\t  - Content blocks: `{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":...}` for developer/user roles;\n186\t    `{\"type\":\"output_text\",\"text\":...}` for assistant role.\n187\t  - `role: \"developer\"` is used for system-injected instructions (permission/sandbox rules,\n188\t    multi-agent framework instructions) — these are just more `message` items, not a special\n189\t    record type.\n190\t  - `id` (assistant messages only) looks like `\"msg_<hex>\"`.\n191\t- **`reasoning`** — `{type:\"reasoning\", id:\"rs_<hex>\", summary: [], encrypted_content: \"<opaque base64-ish blob, ~2-4KB>\", internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough}`.\n192\t  `encrypted_content` is opaque ciphertext from OpenAI's Responses API (server-side reasoning\n193\t  encryption) — **cannot be fabricated** (no key available client-side) and is safe to simply\n194\t  **omit entirely**. We omitted all reasoning items in our fabricated files and resume worked\n195\t  fine; the model just doesn't get fabricated \"prior reasoning,\" which is fine since reasoning\n196\t  items are not required for it to see/use prior message content.\n197\t- **`function_call`** / **`function_call_output`** — standard OpenAI Responses-style tool call\n198\t  pairs, linked by `call_id`. Seen used for a `\"wait\"` tool in this build.\n199\t  `function_call`: `{type, id:\"fc_<hex>\", name, arguments:\"<json string>\", call_id}`.\n200\t  `function_call_output`: `{type, call_id, output: [{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":...}, ...]}`.\n201\t- **`custom_tool_call`** / **`custom_tool_call_output`** — this specific build's primary\n202\t  shell/tool mechanism is a \"custom tool\" named `exec` whose `input` is a **JavaScript snippet**\n203\t  that calls higher-level runtime functions like `tools.exec_command({...})` and\n204\t  `tools.apply_patch(patchString)`, then `text(...)`s the result. Shape:\n205\t  `custom_tool_call`: `{type, id:\"ctc_<hex>\", status:\"completed\", call_id, name:\"exec\", input:\"<js source>\", internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough}`.\n206\t  `custom_tool_call_output`: `{type, call_id, output:[{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"Script completed\\nWall time N seconds\\nOutput:\\n\"}, {\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"<captured stdout>\"}]}`.\n207\t  **This is specific to this Codex build/config (0.144.6 with an agentic \"exec\"-as-JS-runtime\n208\t  tool) and should not be assumed stable or universal** — it's notably different from the\n209\t  classic documented Codex `local_shell_call`/`shell` function-call shape used by older\n210\t  versions and by the `cross_agent_session_resumer` project (see §7). Don't try to fabricate\n211\t  `custom_tool_call` records expecting the model to treat them as \"just happened\" tool calls\n212\t  with guaranteed-consistent semantics across Codex versions.\n213\t\n214\t### 4.4 `turn_context` — per-turn settings snapshot\n215\t```\n216\t{turn_id, cwd, workspace_roots:[...], current_date, timezone, approval_policy, approvals_reviewer,\n217\t sandbox_policy:{type}, permission_profile:{...}, model, comp_hash, personality,\n218\t collaboration_mode:{mode, settings:{model, reasoning_effort, developer_instructions}},\n219\t multi_agent_version, multi_agent_mode, realtime_active, effort, summary}\n220\t```\n221\tConfirmed NOT required for resume (omitted in minimal fabrication; worked fine).\n222\t\n223\t### 4.5 `world_state`\n224\t```\n225\t{full: true, state: {agents_md:{}, apps_instructions: bool, environments:{environments:{<name>:{cwd,status,shell}}, current_date, timezone, filesystem: \"<xml-ish string>\"}, plugins_instructions: bool, skills:{includeInstructions: bool}}}\n226\t```\n227\tAlso confirmed not required.\n228\t\n229\t### 4.6 `compacted` — history-compaction marker\n230\tAppears when Codex auto-compacts long history. Shape:\n231\t```\n232\t{message: \"\", replacement_history: [ <array of response_item-shaped message objects that replace the earlier raw history> ]}\n233\t```\n234\tNot needed for fabrication of a short session; relevant only if turnbridge wants to fabricate a\n235\tsession that *looks like* it went through compaction (e.g. to hide raw tool noise while keeping\n236\ta condensed summary). `replacement_history` entries are plain `message` items with the same\n237\tshape as §4.3, so this is really just \"inject condensed message items and mark them as a\n238\tcompaction event\" — low risk to fabricate the same way as regular messages if ever needed.\n239\t\n240\t---\n241\t\n242\t## 5. Representing foreign tool calls (e.g., Claude `tool_use`/`tool_result`)\n243\t\n244\t**Recommendation: fold foreign tool calls into plain `message` content (input_text/output_text),\n245\tnot into `function_call`/`function_call_output` or `custom_tool_call` records.**\n246\t\n247\tReasoning:\n248\t- The only two `response_item` shapes we verified Codex will read back cleanly, with zero risk,\n249\t  are plain `message` items with `input_text`/`output_text` content blocks. This was directly\n250\t  tested and confirmed (planted a fact in a `user` message, got it echoed back correctly from a\n251\t  fabricated `assistant` message — full round trip).\n252\t- `function_call`/`function_call_output` pairs are standard OpenAI Responses API constructs, but\n253\t  they typically require the `call_id` to correspond to something the API/backend considers\n254\t  consistent, and (per general Responses API behavior) an orphaned `function_call_output` with\n255\t  no matching `function_call` — or a `name` that doesn't match any tool the model currently has\n256\t  registered — is a plausible source of a rejected request when the history is next sent to the\n257\t  model. We did not test this failure mode directly (out of scope/time), so treat it as an\n258\t  **unverified risk**, not a confirmed break.\n259\t- `custom_tool_call` is specific to this build's \"exec-as-JS\" tool wiring (§4.3) and even less\n260\t  safe to fabricate generically.\n261\t- Folding into message text sidesteps all of this: e.g. represent a Claude `tool_use` +\n262\t  `tool_result` pair as a single `assistant` message whose text includes something like:\n263\t  ```\n264\t  [used tool: Bash]\n265\t  command: pytest -q\n266\t  result: 3 passed, 0 failed\n267\t  ```\n268\t  This is exactly the strategy the `cross_agent_session_resumer` (`casr`) project uses for\n269\t  *its own* \"user messages with tool content\" case — see §7 — though `casr`'s code (per a\n270\t  secondary/unverified source excerpt) also attempts a `\"type\":\"tool_use\"` content block\n271\t  inline in `message.content`, which is **not** a content-block type we observed in any real\n272\t  Codex-authored `response_item.message.content` (`input_text`/`output_text` are the only two\n273\t  observed). Prefer the plain-text-folding approach unless/until `tool_use` blocks are\n274\t  separately verified against a live Codex install.\n275\t- For turnbridge's stated goal (make the resumed agent aware of what happened, not literally\n276\t  re-execute historical tool calls), folding into message text is strictly sufficient — verified\n277\t  by our experiment — and avoids every open question about tool-schema/call_id validation.\n278\t\n279\t---\n280\t\n281\t## 6. Session id / UUID requirements\n282\t\n283\t- Any syntactically valid UUID works (tested UUIDv4 successfully; real Codex-generated ids are\n284\t  UUIDv7 but that's not enforced by resume).\n285\t- The id in the filename and `session_meta.payload.id` (and `.session_id`, if present) must all\n286\t  match exactly.\n287\t- No separate registration/index step observed — dropping a correctly-named, correctly-placed\n288\t  file into the dated sessions directory is sufficient; `resume <id>` found it immediately with\n289\t  no other action taken (no `codex sessions rebuild-index` or similar was needed).\n290\t\n291\t---\n292\t\n293\t## 7. Cross-check: `cross_agent_session_resumer` (casr)\n294\t\n295\tFetched via WebFetch (README + best-effort raw source fetch of a provider file; GitHub raw\n296\tfetches through the tool render as an AI-summarized excerpt, not verbatim source, so treat the\n297\tbelow as directionally-accurate, not verbatim-quoted code).\n298\t\n299\tIts Codex writer path corroborates the core `session_meta` shape we found independently\n300\t(`id`, `session_id`, `cwd`, `timestamp`, `originator`, `cli_version`, `source`, `thread_source`,\n301\t`model_provider`) and confirms `response_item` uses `output_text`/`input_text` content blocks\n302\tkeyed on role. It diverges from our verified-safe approach in two ways worth flagging as\n303\t**unverified-against-live-Codex** if turnbridge borrows from it directly:\n304\t1. It emits some user messages as `event_msg` (`user_message`/`agent_reasoning`) instead of\n305\t   `response_item` — per our findings, `event_msg` is NOT required for context replay and,\n306\t   more importantly, we have no evidence it's *sufficient* on its own (we never tested a\n307\t   session containing only `event_msg` turns and no `response_item` turns) — likely a\n308\t   **combined-source strategy** (both possibly for TUI display fidelity + the actual\n309\t   `response_item` in a separate line), not a substitute for `response_item`.\n310\t2. It appears to place `tool_use`/`tool_result`-shaped blocks directly inside `message.content`,\n311\t   which — per §5 — we did not observe in any real Codex output and recommend avoiding in favor\n312\t   of plain-text folding, which is verified to work.\n313\t\n314\t---\n315\t\n316\t## 8. Unknowns / risks for the implementer\n317\t\n318\t1. **Version drift**: this spec is pinned to codex-cli 0.144.6. Codex's rollout schema has\n319\t   clearly evolved rapidly (multi-agent fields, `custom_tool_call`, `world_state`, `compacted`\n320\t   are all plausibly recent additions) — treat this format as internal/unstable, not a public\n321\t   API. Re-verify against the installed `codex --version` before relying on this in production;\n322\t   the minimal schema in §3 is the safest bet for forward/backward compatibility since it's the\n323\t   smallest surface area.\n324\t2. **`function_call`/`custom_tool_call` replay safety** is unverified (see §5) — don't use them\n325\t   for fabrication without separately testing against the target Codex version.\n326\t3. **Picker/`--all` cwd-filtering exact matching logic** (prefix? exact string? normalized path?)\n327\t   was not tested beyond reading the `--help` text; only direct `resume <id>` was verified.\n328\t4. **Very large fabricated histories**: untested whether extremely large fabricated\n329\t   `response_item` sequences (e.g. thousands of turns) hit any different code path (e.g.\n330\t   automatic `compacted` triggering, size limits) versus the small 2-3 turn case we verified.\n331\t5. **`base_instructions`**: real files carry a large (~18KB) system prompt string per session.\n332\t   Omitting it entirely worked in our test (Codex presumably falls back to its own current\n333\t   default system prompt for the *new* turn, while the fabricated *history* turns are still\n334\t   replayed as-is) — but this means the fabricated session's replayed history won't perfectly\n335\t   match \"what system prompt was actually active\" if that matters for turnbridge's use case.\n336\t   Not a blocker for the stated goal (make prior context visible), just noted for completeness.\n337\t6. **`encrypted_content` for `reasoning` items**: confirmed unfabricatable and confirmed safe to\n338\t   omit outright (don't emit `reasoning` response_items at all rather than trying to synthesize\n339\t   fake ciphertext, which would almost certainly be rejected or ignored as garbage by the API if\n340\t   it were even parsed — untested, but omission is the verified-safe path).\n341\t7. **Interactive TUI resume** was only verified to \"not error and begin rendering\" via a pty\n342\t   wrapper (`script`); we could not capture/assert on the actual rendered TUI content\n343\t   programmatically. `codex exec resume --json` is the fully-verified, content-confirmed path\n344\t   and should be turnbridge's primary target/test surface; treat TUI-resume compatibility as\n345\t   \"very likely fine, same underlying loader\" rather than independently proven line-for-line.\n346\t\n347\t---\n348\t\n349\t## 9. Verification experiment log (for reproducibility)\n350\t\n351\t1. Created scratch git repo at a scratchpad path, one commit.\n352\t2. Generated a UUIDv7 id, wrote a 3-line minimal `session_meta` + user `message` + assistant\n353\t   `message` file to `~/.codex/sessions/2026/07/18/rollout-<local-ts>-<uuid>.jsonl` (planted\n354\t   fact: \"secret code word is BANANA77\").\n355\t3. Ran `codex exec resume <id> \"Reply with exactly the secret code word...\" --json\n356\t   --skip-git-repo-check` from the scratch cwd → model correctly answered `BANANA77`.\n357\t4. Stripped the schema further (removed `session_id`, `source`, `thread_source`,\n358\t   `model_provider`, `history_mode`, `context_window`; kept only `id`, `timestamp`, `cwd`,\n359\t   `originator`, `cli_version`) with a new planted fact `PLUMFISH42` → still worked, from the\n360\t   scratch cwd.\n361\t5. Re-ran the same resume from `/tmp` (different cwd than fabricated `session_meta.cwd`) →\n362\t   still worked, proving direct-id resume ignores cwd matching.\n363\t6. Asked the resumed agent to run `pwd` (with `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`) →\n364\t   printed `/tmp` (the real invocation cwd), confirming `session_meta.cwd` is not authoritative\n365\t   for execution root.\n366\t7. Regenerated with a plain `uuid4()` (non-time-ordered) id → still worked.\n367\t8. Tested interactive `codex resume <id>` with stdin redirected from `/dev/null` → immediate\n368\t   `Error: stdin is not a terminal` (no tty). Re-tested wrapped in macOS `script -q` to allocate\n369\t   a pty → process started emitting terminal-capability query escape sequences (bracketed paste\n370\t   mode, cursor position query, etc.), i.e. began normal TUI startup rather than erroring on the\n371\t   fabricated file; killed the backgrounded process after a few seconds (interactive rendering\n372\t   isn't scriptably assertable, but no crash/parse-error occurred).\n373\t9. Deleted all fabricated files/dirs after each test; verified the real\n374\t   `~/.codex/sessions/2026/07/18/` directory still contains exactly its original 22 files and\n375\t   no fabricated content remains anywhere under `~/.codex/sessions/` (`grep -rl` for planted\n376\t   secrets found nothing).\n377\t\n378\tNo real session files were read destructively, modified, or deleted at any point.\n379\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01WHb8W8KM9s2ARksnj3WHZL","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","dirty_fingerprint":"bc65e878d80c1722a0adee9428cbf850f1d73e21ecfd82d0c4182876ebf93c10","head":"173cfc66bb28f4c20e474f522f197eeef0c627de","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c#agent:a320d69d7506c9fa1","parent":"claude-code:6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","seq":14},"id":"ev1-246a9b86064adde0c6c737a32450c46c8829103782884810d9df184945d55eff","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-21T21:21:05.841Z","producer":{"session_id":"6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.216","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"a320d69d7506c9fa1","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":true,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# Codex CLI native \"rollout\" session file format — reverse-engineering spec\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: enable `turnbridge` to FABRICATE a `~/.codex/sessions/.../rollout-*.jsonl` file that\n4\tCodex's native `resume` (both interactive TUI and `codex exec resume`) will load with the\n5\tfabricated prior conversation visible to the model.\n6\t\n7\tPinned environment:\n8\t- `codex --version` → **codex-cli 0.144.6** (some session files on disk were written by 0.144.5;\n9\t  format is stable across that point release).\n10\t- Tested on macOS (darwin), zsh shell, `$CODEX_HOME` = `~/.codex` (default).\n11\t\n12\tEverything in this document was verified empirically against this exact build on 2026-07-18/19,\n13\tby hand-writing fabricated rollout files and confirming Codex resumed them with visible prior\n14\tcontext (see \"Verification experiment\" below). It is not derived solely from docs or other\n15\tprojects' guesses.\n16\t\n17\t---\n18\t\n19\t## 1. Resume command forms\n20\t\n21\t### Interactive (TUI)\n22\t```\n23\tcodex resume [SESSION_ID] [PROMPT] [OPTIONS]\n24\t```\n25\t- `SESSION_ID` — a UUID (any version; v4 works, not just v7 — see §6) **or** a session *name*.\n26\t  If parseable as a UUID it's treated as an id, not a name.\n27\t- If `SESSION_ID` is omitted: shows an interactive **picker** listing recorded sessions.\n28\t  - The picker **filters by current working directory** by default (only sessions whose\n29\t    `session_meta.cwd` matches — exact mechanism not further probed, but `--all` explicitly\n30\t    \"disables cwd filtering\").\n31\t  - `--last` bypasses the picker and resumes the most recently recorded session (still cwd\n32\t    -filtered unless combined with `--all`).\n33\t  - `--all` shows all sessions regardless of cwd, and adds a CWD column.\n34\t  - `--include-non-interactive` — only meaningful for the picker; non-interactive (`exec`-\n35\t    originated) sessions are excluded from the picker/`--last` selection unless passed.\n36\t- Requires a real TTY. In a non-tty environment it fails immediately with\n37\t  `Error: stdin is not a terminal` (verified) — this happens *before* any session-content\n38\t  validation as far as we could tell; when run inside a pty (via `script`), it proceeded past\n39\t  that check and began rendering the TUI (terminal capability query escape sequences observed),\n40\t  i.e. it did **not** reject our fabricated file.\n41\t- `[PROMPT]` optional — if given, is sent as the first new user turn immediately after resuming.\n42\t\n43\t### Non-interactive (scriptable) — **this is the form to target for programmatic fabrication/verification**\n44\t```\n45\tcodex exec resume [SESSION_ID] [PROMPT] [OPTIONS]\n46\t```\n47\t- Same `SESSION_ID` semantics as above (`--last` and `--all` also present).\n48\t- `--json` — print NDJSON events to stdout (`thread.started`, `turn.started`,\n49\t  `item.completed`, `turn.completed`, ...). This is the reliable way to script verification.\n50\t- `--skip-git-repo-check` — needed if invoked outside a git repo, or if the fabricated file's\n51\t  target cwd differs from where `codex` was actually launched.\n52\t- `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox` — needed if you want the resumed agent to\n53\t  actually run shell commands without prompting (used only for one diagnostic test below).\n54\t- **Important finding**: resume-by-id works from **any** current working directory — cwd\n55\t  filtering only applies to the *picker*, not to direct-id resume. The actual working directory\n56\t  the resumed agent operates in is the **process's real cwd at invocation time** (i.e. wherever\n57\t  you ran `codex exec resume` from), **not** the fabricated `session_meta.cwd` value. We\n58\t  verified this directly: resumed a session whose fabricated `cwd` pointed at a scratch repo,\n59\t  invoked `codex exec resume` from `/tmp` instead, and asked the agent to run `pwd` — it printed\n60\t  `/tmp`. So `session_meta.cwd` is effectively cosmetic/display metadata (used for the picker's\n61\t  cwd filter and any UI that shows \"resumed session in <dir>\"), not authoritative for execution.\n62\t  Implication for turnbridge: set `cwd` to whatever you want *displayed*/matched by the picker,\n63\t  but don't rely on it to sandbox or root the resumed agent — that's controlled by the real\n64\t  invocation cwd / `-C`/`--cd`.\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t## 2. File/directory naming rules\n69\t\n70\t```\n71\t~/.codex/sessions/<YYYY>/<MM>/<DD>/rollout-<local-timestamp>-<uuid>.jsonl\n72\t```\n73\t- `$CODEX_HOME` defaults to `~/.codex`; sessions live under `$CODEX_HOME/sessions`.\n74\t- `<YYYY>/<MM>/<DD>` is the **local** date (not UTC) at session-creation time. Verified: local\n75\t  clock was `2026-07-18 23:59 PDT` while the in-file UTC timestamps read `2026-07-19T06:59...Z`,\n76\t  and the file was correctly placed under `.../2026/07/18/`.\n77\t- `<local-timestamp>` in the filename is local time, format `YYYY-MM-DDTHH-MM-SS` (colons\n78\t  replaced with hyphens, seconds precision, no milliseconds, no offset suffix).\n79\t  Example real filename: `rollout-2026-07-18T16-16-52-019f7784-b1c3-7561-9237-586478b394d2.jsonl`.\n80\t- `<uuid>` is the session id, **and must exactly match** `session_meta.payload.id` /\n81\t  `.session_id` inside the file (this is how `resume <id>` locates the file — presumably an\n82\t  index/scan over the sessions dir matching this literal id, since resume-by-id worked\n83\t  immediately with no separate registration step).\n84\t- UUID format: does not need to be UUIDv7. A real random UUIDv4 was accepted and resumed\n85\t  successfully. (Real Codex-generated ids happen to be UUIDv7 — time-ordered, e.g.\n86\t  `019f776a-c87a-7990-b54f-8040658be7fd` — presumably just because that's what Codex's own id\n87\t  generator emits, not because resume validates the version nibble.)\n88\t- All timestamps *inside* the JSONL (`timestamp` fields) are UTC, ISO-8601 with `Z` suffix and\n89\t  millisecond precision, e.g. `\"2026-07-18T22:48:34.755Z\"`.\n90\t- One session = one file. Lines are newline-delimited JSON objects (JSONL), UTF-8. Some real\n91\t  files contain raw literal control characters inside string values in a way that breaks strict\n92\t  parsers like `jq` (`jq: parse error: Invalid string: control characters...`) but that Python's\n93\t  `json.loads` parses fine — i.e. Codex's own writer does not always escape control bytes\n94\t  (likely raw terminal/ANSI bytes captured from shell output pass through un-escaped in some\n95\t  version of the writer). **When fabricating**, always produce properly-escaped JSON (use a real\n96\t  JSON serializer) — there's no reason to reproduce that quirk, and doing so risks a genuinely\n97\t  invalid file.\n98\t\n99\t---\n100\t\n101\t## 3. Minimal fabricated schema that VERIFIABLY WORKS\n102\t\n103\tThis is the maximally stripped-down version we tested and confirmed loads correctly via\n104\t`codex exec resume` (model echoed back a planted \"secret code word\", proving it read the\n105\tfabricated history) — and via `codex resume` (TUI started rendering without error, using a pty).\n106\t\n107\tThree lines total. `session_meta` needs only 5 payload fields; each conversation turn needs only\n108\ta bare `response_item` message.\n109\t\n110\t```jsonl\n111\t{\"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"type\": \"session_meta\", \"payload\": {\"id\": \"019f792d-38dc-7cc7-a2d1-072e8cb04002\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"cwd\": \"/some/dir\", \"originator\": \"codex_exec\", \"cli_version\": \"0.144.6\"}}\n112\t{\"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"type\": \"response_item\", \"payload\": {\"type\": \"message\", \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [{\"type\": \"input_text\", \"text\": \"The secret code word for this test is PLUMFISH42. Remember it.\"}]}}\n113\t{\"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"type\": \"response_item\", \"payload\": {\"type\": \"message\", \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": [{\"type\": \"output_text\", \"text\": \"Got it, the secret code word is PLUMFISH42.\"}]}}\n114\t```\n115\t\n116\tVerified NOT required for `exec resume` to work and see full context:\n117\t- `session_meta.payload.session_id` (only `.id` was present; both usually appear in real files —\n118\t  recommend including both for safety/forward-compat, but `.id` alone sufficed in our test)\n119\t- `source`, `thread_source`, `model_provider`, `history_mode`, `context_window`, `git`,\n120\t  `base_instructions` — all omitted in the minimal test and it still worked.\n121\t- No `turn_context`, `world_state`, or `event_msg` lines at all are required for the resumed\n122\t  model to see/use prior conversation content. Those record types appear to be informational /\n123\t  UI-facing (token counters, sandbox/approval settings snapshots, TUI rendering aids) rather than\n124\t  part of what gets replayed into the model's context. **`response_item` lines are the actual\n125\t  conversation state that gets replayed.**\n126\t- `response_item.payload.message.id` — not required (real assistant messages have one, e.g.\n127\t  `\"msg_0850eb109f...\"`, but omitting it did not break resume).\n128\t\n129\tRecommended (not strictly required, but cheap and matches real files, reduces risk of edge\n130\tcases in other code paths like the picker or future Codex versions that might start requiring\n131\tthem):\n132\t```jsonl\n133\t{\"timestamp\": \"<UTC-ISO-ms-Z>\", \"type\": \"session_meta\", \"payload\": {\n134\t  \"session_id\": \"<uuid>\", \"id\": \"<uuid>\", \"timestamp\": \"<UTC-ISO-ms-Z>\",\n135\t  \"cwd\": \"<display-dir>\", \"originator\": \"codex_exec\", \"cli_version\": \"0.144.6\",\n136\t  \"source\": \"exec\", \"thread_source\": \"user\", \"model_provider\": \"openai\",\n137\t  \"history_mode\": \"legacy\"\n138\t}}\n139\t```\n140\t`history_mode` was `\"legacy\"` on every real file inspected (only value observed).\n141\t\n142\t---\n143\t\n144\t## 4. Full observed schema (from real files)\n145\t\n146\tTop-level line shape is always: `{\"timestamp\": <UTC ISO>, \"type\": <string>, \"payload\": {...}}`.\n147\tObserved `type` values: `session_meta`, `event_msg`, `response_item`, `turn_context`,\n148\t`world_state`, `compacted`.\n149\t\n150\t### 4.1 `session_meta` (always line 1)\n151\tFull field set seen across files (union; not all present in every file):\n152\t```\n153\tpayload.session_id        string uuid\n154\tpayload.id                string uuid   (== session_id; both present in normal (non-fabricated) files)\n155\tpayload.timestamp         UTC ISO ms\n156\tpayload.cwd                string path\n157\tpayload.originator         \"codex-tui\" | \"codex_exec\"\n158\tpayload.cli_version         e.g. \"0.144.6\"\n159\tpayload.source              \"cli\" | \"exec\" | {\"subagent\": {\"thread_spawn\": {...}}}  (object form for spawned sub-agent sessions)\n160\tpayload.thread_source       \"user\" | \"subagent\"\n161\tpayload.model_provider      \"openai\"\n162\tpayload.base_instructions   {\"text\": \"<full system prompt, ~18KB>\"}\n163\tpayload.history_mode        \"legacy\"\n164\tpayload.context_window      {\"window_id\": \"<uuid>\"}\n165\tpayload.git                 {\"commit_hash\": str, \"branch\": str, \"repository_url\": str}   (present only when cwd is inside a git repo)\n166\tpayload.forked_from_id      uuid   (only on forked/sub-agent sessions)\n167\tpayload.parent_thread_id    uuid   (only on forked/sub-agent sessions)\n168\tpayload.agent_nickname      string (only on sub-agent sessions, e.g. \"Lagrange\")\n169\tpayload.agent_path          string (only on sub-agent sessions, e.g. \"/root/quality_audit\")\n170\tpayload.multi_agent_version \"v2\"   (only on sub-agent sessions)\n171\t```\n172\t\n173\t### 4.2 `event_msg` — UI/telemetry events, NOT required for context replay\n174\tObserved `payload.type` values and shapes:\n175\t- `task_started` — `{turn_id, started_at, model_context_window, collaboration_mode_kind}`\n176\t- `user_message` — `{message, images, local_images, text_elements}`\n177\t- `agent_message` — `{message, phase: \"final_answer\", memory_citation}`\n178\t- `token_count` — `{info: {total_token_usage:{...}, last_token_usage:{...}, model_context_window}, rate_limits: {...}}`\n179\t- `patch_apply_end` — `{call_id, turn_id, stdout, stderr, success, changes: {<path>: {type: \"add\"|..., content}}, status}`\n180\t- `task_complete` — `{turn_id, last_agent_message, completed_at, duration_ms, time_to_first_token_ms}`\n181\t\n182\t### 4.3 `response_item` — the actual conversation payload replayed to the model\n183\t`payload.type` variants observed:\n184\t- **`message`** — `{type:\"message\", role: \"developer\"|\"user\"|\"assistant\", id?, content: [...], phase?, internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough?: {turn_id}}`.\n185\t  - Content blocks: `{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":...}` for developer/user roles;\n186\t    `{\"type\":\"output_text\",\"text\":...}` for assistant role.\n187\t  - `role: \"developer\"` is used for system-injected instructions (permission/sandbox rules,\n188\t    multi-agent framework instructions) — these are just more `message` items, not a special\n189\t    record type.\n190\t  - `id` (assistant messages only) looks like `\"msg_<hex>\"`.\n191\t- **`reasoning`** — `{type:\"reasoning\", id:\"rs_<hex>\", summary: [], encrypted_content: \"<opaque base64-ish blob, ~2-4KB>\", internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough}`.\n192\t  `encrypted_content` is opaque ciphertext from OpenAI's Responses API (server-side reasoning\n193\t  encryption) — **cannot be fabricated** (no key available client-side) and is safe to simply\n194\t  **omit entirely**. We omitted all reasoning items in our fabricated files and resume worked\n195\t  fine; the model just doesn't get fabricated \"prior reasoning,\" which is fine since reasoning\n196\t  items are not required for it to see/use prior message content.\n197\t- **`function_call`** / **`function_call_output`** — standard OpenAI Responses-style tool call\n198\t  pairs, linked by `call_id`. Seen used for a `\"wait\"` tool in this build.\n199\t  `function_call`: `{type, id:\"fc_<hex>\", name, arguments:\"<json string>\", call_id}`.\n200\t  `function_call_output`: `{type, call_id, output: [{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":...}, ...]}`.\n201\t- **`custom_tool_call`** / **`custom_tool_call_output`** — this specific build's primary\n202\t  shell/tool mechanism is a \"custom tool\" named `exec` whose `input` is a **JavaScript snippet**\n203\t  that calls higher-level runtime functions like `tools.exec_command({...})` and\n204\t  `tools.apply_patch(patchString)`, then `text(...)`s the result. Shape:\n205\t  `custom_tool_call`: `{type, id:\"ctc_<hex>\", status:\"completed\", call_id, name:\"exec\", input:\"<js source>\", internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough}`.\n206\t  `custom_tool_call_output`: `{type, call_id, output:[{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"Script completed\\nWall time N seconds\\nOutput:\\n\"}, {\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"<captured stdout>\"}]}`.\n207\t  **This is specific to this Codex build/config (0.144.6 with an agentic \"exec\"-as-JS-runtime\n208\t  tool) and should not be assumed stable or universal** — it's notably different from the\n209\t  classic documented Codex `local_shell_call`/`shell` function-call shape used by older\n210\t  versions and by the `cross_agent_session_resumer` project (see §7). Don't try to fabricate\n211\t  `custom_tool_call` records expecting the model to treat them as \"just happened\" tool calls\n212\t  with guaranteed-consistent semantics across Codex versions.\n213\t\n214\t### 4.4 `turn_context` — per-turn settings snapshot\n215\t```\n216\t{turn_id, cwd, workspace_roots:[...], current_date, timezone, approval_policy, approvals_reviewer,\n217\t sandbox_policy:{type}, permission_profile:{...}, model, comp_hash, personality,\n218\t collaboration_mode:{mode, settings:{model, reasoning_effort, developer_instructions}},\n219\t multi_agent_version, multi_agent_mode, realtime_active, effort, summary}\n220\t```\n221\tConfirmed NOT required for resume (omitted in minimal fabrication; worked fine).\n222\t\n223\t### 4.5 `world_state`\n224\t```\n225\t{full: true, state: {agents_md:{}, apps_instructions: bool, environments:{environments:{<name>:{cwd,status,shell}}, current_date, timezone, filesystem: \"<xml-ish string>\"}, plugins_instructions: bool, skills:{includeInstructions: bool}}}\n226\t```\n227\tAlso confirmed not required.\n228\t\n229\t### 4.6 `compacted` — history-compaction marker\n230\tAppears when Codex auto-compacts long history. Shape:\n231\t```\n232\t{message: \"\", replacement_history: [ <array of response_item-shaped message objects that replace the earlier raw history> ]}\n233\t```\n234\tNot needed for fabrication of a short session; relevant only if turnbridge wants to fabricate a\n235\tsession that *looks like* it went through compaction (e.g. to hide raw tool noise while keeping\n236\ta condensed summary). `replacement_history` entries are plain `message` items with the same\n237\tshape as §4.3, so this is really just \"inject condensed message items and mark them as a\n238\tcompaction event\" — low risk to fabricate the same way as regular messages if ever needed.\n239\t\n240\t---\n241\t\n242\t## 5. Representing foreign tool calls (e.g., Claude `tool_use`/`tool_result`)\n243\t\n244\t**Recommendation: fold foreign tool calls into plain `message` content (input_text/output_text),\n245\tnot into `function_call`/`function_call_output` or `custom_tool_call` records.**\n246\t\n247\tReasoning:\n248\t- The only two `response_item` shapes we verified Codex will read back cleanly, with zero risk,\n249\t  are plain `message` items with `input_text`/`output_text` content blocks. This was directly\n250\t  tested and confirmed (planted a fact in a `user` message, got it echoed back correctly from a\n251\t  fabricated `assistant` message — full round trip).\n252\t- `function_call`/`function_call_output` pairs are standard OpenAI Responses API constructs, but\n253\t  they typically require the `call_id` to correspond to something the API/backend considers\n254\t  consistent, and (per general Responses API behavior) an orphaned `function_call_output` with\n255\t  no matching `function_call` — or a `name` that doesn't match any tool the model currently has\n256\t  registered — is a plausible source of a rejected request when the history is next sent to the\n257\t  model. We did not test this failure mode directly (out of scope/time), so treat it as an\n258\t  **unverified risk**, not a confirmed break.\n259\t- `custom_tool_call` is specific to this build's \"exec-as-JS\" tool wiring (§4.3) and even less\n260\t  safe to fabricate generically.\n261\t- Folding into message text sidesteps all of this: e.g. represent a Claude `tool_use` +\n262\t  `tool_result` pair as a single `assistant` message whose text includes something like:\n263\t  ```\n264\t  [used tool: Bash]\n265\t  command: pytest -q\n266\t  result: 3 passed, 0 failed\n267\t  ```\n268\t  This is exactly the strategy the `cross_agent_session_resumer` (`casr`) project uses for\n269\t  *its own* \"user messages with tool content\" case — see §7 — though `casr`'s code (per a\n270\t  secondary/unverified source excerpt) also attempts a `\"type\":\"tool_use\"` content block\n271\t  inline in `message.content`, which is **not** a content-block type we observed in any real\n272\t  Codex-authored `response_item.message.content` (`input_text`/`output_text` are the only two\n273\t  observed). Prefer the plain-text-folding approach unless/until `tool_use` blocks are\n274\t  separately verified against a live Codex install.\n275\t- For turnbridge's stated goal (make the resumed agent aware of what happened, not literally\n276\t  re-execute historical tool calls), folding into message text is strictly sufficient — verified\n277\t  by our experiment — and avoids every open question about tool-schema/call_id validation.\n278\t\n279\t---\n280\t\n281\t## 6. Session id / UUID requirements\n282\t\n283\t- Any syntactically valid UUID works (tested UUIDv4 successfully; real Codex-generated ids are\n284\t  UUIDv7 but that's not enforced by resume).\n285\t- The id in the filename and `session_meta.payload.id` (and `.session_id`, if present) must all\n286\t  match exactly.\n287\t- No separate registration/index step observed — dropping a correctly-named, correctly-placed\n288\t  file into the dated sessions directory is sufficient; `resume <id>` found it immediately with\n289\t  no other action taken (no `codex sessions rebuild-index` or similar was needed).\n290\t\n291\t---\n292\t\n293\t## 7. Cross-check: `cross_agent_session_resumer` (casr)\n294\t\n295\tFetched via WebFetch (README + best-effort raw source fetch of a provider file; GitHub raw\n296\tfetches through the tool render as an AI-summarized excerpt, not verbatim source, so treat the\n297\tbelow as directionally-accurate, not verbatim-quoted code).\n298\t\n299\tIts Codex writer path corroborates the core `session_meta` shape we found independently\n300\t(`id`, `session_id`, `cwd`, `timestamp`, `originator`, `cli_version`, `source`, `thread_source`,\n301\t`model_provider`) and confirms `response_item` uses `output_text`/`input_text` content blocks\n302\tkeyed on role. It diverges from our verified-safe approach in two ways worth flagging as\n303\t**unverified-against-live-Codex** if turnbridge borrows from it directly:\n304\t1. It emits some user messages as `event_msg` (`user_message`/`agent_reasoning`) instead of\n305\t   `response_item` — per our findings, `event_msg` is NOT required for context replay and,\n306\t   more importantly, we have no evidence it's *sufficient* on its own (we never tested a\n307\t   session containing only `event_msg` turns and no `response_item` turns) — likely a\n308\t   **combined-source strategy** (both possibly for TUI display fidelity + the actual\n309\t   `response_item` in a separate line), not a substitute for `response_item`.\n310\t2. It appears to place `tool_use`/`tool_result`-shaped blocks directly inside `message.content`,\n311\t   which — per §5 — we did not observe in any real Codex output and recommend avoiding in favor\n312\t   of plain-text folding, which is verified to work.\n313\t\n314\t---\n315\t\n316\t## 8. Unknowns / risks for the implementer\n317\t\n318\t1. **Version drift**: this spec is pinned to codex-cli 0.144.6. Codex's rollout schema has\n319\t   clearly evolved rapidly (multi-agent fields, `custom_tool_call`, `world_state`, `compacted`\n320\t   are all plausibly recent additions) — treat this format as internal/unstable, not a public\n321\t   API. Re-verify against the installed `codex --version` before relying on this in production;\n322\t   the minimal schema in §3 is the safest bet for forward/backward compatibility since it's the\n323\t   smallest surface area.\n324\t2. **`function_call`/`custom_tool_call` replay safety** is unverified (see §5) — don't use them\n325\t   for fabrication without separately testing against the target Codex version.\n326\t3. **Picker/`--all` cwd-filtering exact matching logic** (prefix? exact string? normalized path?)\n327\t   was not tested beyond reading the `--help` text; only direct `resume <id>` was verified.\n328\t4. **Very large fabricated histories**: untested whether extremely large fabricated\n329\t   `response_item` sequences (e.g. thousands of turns) hit any different code path (e.g.\n330\t   automatic `compacted` triggering, size limits) versus the small 2-3 turn case we verified.\n331\t5. **`base_instructions`**: real files carry a large (~18KB) system prompt string per session.\n332\t   Omitting it entirely worked in our test (Codex presumably falls back to its own current\n333\t   default system prompt for the *new* turn, while the fabricated *history* turns are still\n334\t   replayed as-is) — but this means the fabricated session's replayed history won't perfectly\n335\t   match \"what system prompt was actually active\" if that matters for turnbridge's use case.\n336\t   Not a blocker for the stated goal (make prior context visible), just noted for completeness.\n337\t6. **`encrypted_content` for `reasoning` items**: confirmed unfabricatable and confirmed safe to\n338\t   omit outright (don't emit `reasoning` response_items at all rather than trying to synthesize\n339\t   fake ciphertext, which would almost certainly be rejected or ignored as garbage by the API if\n340\t   it were even parsed — untested, but omission is the verified-safe path).\n341\t7. **Interactive TUI resume** was only verified to \"not error and begin rendering\" via a pty\n342\t   wrapper (`script`); we could not capture/assert on the actual rendered TUI content\n343\t   programmatically. `codex exec resume --json` is the fully-verified, content-confirmed path\n344\t   and should be turnbridge's primary target/test surface; treat TUI-resume compatibility as\n345\t   \"very likely fine, same underlying loader\" rather than independently proven line-for-line.\n346\t\n347\t---\n348\t\n349\t## 9. Verification experiment log (for reproducibility)\n350\t\n351\t1. Created scratch git repo at a scratchpad path, one commit.\n352\t2. Generated a UUIDv7 id, wrote a 3-line minimal `session_meta` + user `message` + assistant\n353\t   `message` file to `~/.codex/sessions/2026/07/18/rollout-<local-ts>-<uuid>.jsonl` (planted\n354\t   fact: \"secret code word is BANANA77\").\n355\t3. Ran `codex exec resume <id> \"Reply with exactly the secret code word...\" --json\n356\t   --skip-git-repo-check` from the scratch cwd → model correctly answered `BANANA77`.\n357\t4. Stripped the schema further (removed `session_id`, `source`, `thread_source`,\n358\t   `model_provider`, `history_mode`, `context_window`; kept only `id`, `timestamp`, `cwd`,\n359\t   `originator`, `cli_version`) with a new planted fact `PLUMFISH42` → still worked, from the\n360\t   scratch cwd.\n361\t5. Re-ran the same resume from `/tmp` (different cwd than fabricated `session_meta.cwd`) →\n362\t   still worked, proving direct-id resume ignores cwd matching.\n363\t6. Asked the resumed agent to run `pwd` (with `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`) →\n364\t   printed `/tmp` (the real invocation cwd), confirming `session_meta.cwd` is not authoritative\n365\t   for execution root.\n366\t7. Regenerated with a plain `uuid4()` (non-time-ordered) id → still worked.\n367\t8. Tested interactive `codex resume <id>` with stdin redirected from `/dev/null` → immediate\n368\t   `Error: stdin is not a terminal` (no tty). Re-tested wrapped in macOS `script -q` to allocate\n369\t   a pty → process started emitting terminal-capability query escape sequences (bracketed paste\n370\t   mode, cursor position query, etc.), i.e. began normal TUI startup rather than erroring on the\n371\t   fabricated file; killed the backgrounded process after a few seconds (interactive rendering\n372\t   isn't scriptably assertable, but no crash/parse-error occurred).\n373\t9. Deleted all fabricated files/dirs after each test; verified the real\n374\t   `~/.codex/sessions/2026/07/18/` directory still contains exactly its original 22 files and\n375\t   no fabricated content remains anywhere under `~/.codex/sessions/` (`grep -rl` for planted\n376\t   secrets found nothing).\n377\t\n378\tNo real session files were read destructively, modified, or deleted at any point.\n379\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01WHb8W8KM9s2ARksnj3WHZL","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"26e6c5da-2c7c-4070-aa67-d6d5df14aa78","promptId":"5baaa4e1-1fa5-494b-8425-5ca6e70ddfd1","sessionId":"6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","slug":"hazy-floating-cupcake","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"26e6c5da-2c7c-4070-aa67-d6d5df14aa78","timestamp":"2026-07-21T21:21:05.841Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"# Codex CLI native \"rollout\" session file format — reverse-engineering spec\n\nPurpose: enable `turnbridge` to FABRICATE a `~/.codex/sessions/.../rollout-*.jsonl` file that\nCodex's native `resume` (both interactive TUI and `codex exec resume`) will load with the\nfabricated prior conversation visible to the model.\n\nPinned environment:\n- `codex --version` → **codex-cli 0.144.6** (some session files on disk were written by 0.144.5;\n  format is stable across that point release).\n- Tested on macOS (darwin), zsh shell, `$CODEX_HOME` = `~/.codex` (default).\n\nEverything in this document was verified empirically against this exact build on 2026-07-18/19,\nby hand-writing fabricated rollout files and confirming Codex resumed them with visible prior\ncontext (see \"Verification experiment\" below). It is not derived solely from docs or other\nprojects' guesses.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Resume command forms\n\n### Interactive (TUI)\n```\ncodex resume [SESSION_ID] [PROMPT] [OPTIONS]\n```\n- `SESSION_ID` — a UUID (any version; v4 works, not just v7 — see §6) **or** a session *name*.\n  If parseable as a UUID it's treated as an id, not a name.\n- If `SESSION_ID` is omitted: shows an interactive **picker** listing recorded sessions.\n  - The picker **filters by current working directory** by default (only sessions whose\n    `session_meta.cwd` matches — exact mechanism not further probed, but `--all` explicitly\n    \"disables cwd filtering\").\n  - `--last` bypasses the picker and resumes the most recently recorded session (still cwd\n    -filtered unless combined with `--all`).\n  - `--all` shows all sessions regardless of cwd, and adds a CWD column.\n  - `--include-non-interactive` — only meaningful for the picker; non-interactive (`exec`-\n    originated) sessions are excluded from the picker/`--last` selection unless passed.\n- Requires a real TTY. In a non-tty environment it fails immediately with\n  `Error: stdin is not a terminal` (verified) — this happens *before* any session-content\n  validation as far as we could tell; when run inside a pty (via `script`), it proceeded past\n  that check and began rendering the TUI (terminal capability query escape sequences observed),\n  i.e. it did **not** reject our fabricated file.\n- `[PROMPT]` optional — if given, is sent as the first new user turn immediately after resuming.\n\n### Non-interactive (scriptable) — **this is the form to target for programmatic fabrication/verification**\n```\ncodex exec resume [SESSION_ID] [PROMPT] [OPTIONS]\n```\n- Same `SESSION_ID` semantics as above (`--last` and `--all` also present).\n- `--json` — print NDJSON events to stdout (`thread.started`, `turn.started`,\n  `item.completed`, `turn.completed`, ...). This is the reliable way to script verification.\n- `--skip-git-repo-check` — needed if invoked outside a git repo, or if the fabricated file's\n  target cwd differs from where `codex` was actually launched.\n- `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox` — needed if you want the resumed agent to\n  actually run shell commands without prompting (used only for one diagnostic test below).\n- **Important finding**: resume-by-id works from **any** current working directory — cwd\n  filtering only applies to the *picker*, not to direct-id resume. The actual working directory\n  the resumed agent operates in is the **process's real cwd at invocation time** (i.e. wherever\n  you ran `codex exec resume` from), **not** the fabricated `session_meta.cwd` value. We\n  verified this directly: resumed a session whose fabricated `cwd` pointed at a scratch repo,\n  invoked `codex exec resume` from `/tmp` instead, and asked the agent to run `pwd` — it printed\n  `/tmp`. So `session_meta.cwd` is effectively cosmetic/display metadata (used for the picker's\n  cwd filter and any UI that shows \"resumed session in <dir>\"), not authoritative for execution.\n  Implication for turnbridge: set `cwd` to whatever you want *displayed*/matched by the picker,\n  but don't rely on it to sandbox or root the resumed agent — that's controlled by the real\n  invocation cwd / `-C`/`--cd`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. File/directory naming rules\n\n```\n~/.codex/sessions/<YYYY>/<MM>/<DD>/rollout-<local-timestamp>-<uuid>.jsonl\n```\n- `$CODEX_HOME` defaults to `~/.codex`; sessions live under `$CODEX_HOME/sessions`.\n- `<YYYY>/<MM>/<DD>` is the **local** date (not UTC) at session-creation time. Verified: local\n  clock was `2026-07-18 23:59 PDT` while the in-file UTC timestamps read `2026-07-19T06:59...Z`,\n  and the file was correctly placed under `.../2026/07/18/`.\n- `<local-timestamp>` in the filename is local time, format `YYYY-MM-DDTHH-MM-SS` (colons\n  replaced with hyphens, seconds precision, no milliseconds, no offset suffix).\n  Example real filename: `rollout-2026-07-18T16-16-52-019f7784-b1c3-7561-9237-586478b394d2.jsonl`.\n- `<uuid>` is the session id, **and must exactly match** `session_meta.payload.id` /\n  `.session_id` inside the file (this is how `resume <id>` locates the file — presumably an\n  index/scan over the sessions dir matching this literal id, since resume-by-id worked\n  immediately with no separate registration step).\n- UUID format: does not need to be UUIDv7. A real random UUIDv4 was accepted and resumed\n  successfully. (Real Codex-generated ids happen to be UUIDv7 — time-ordered, e.g.\n  `019f776a-c87a-7990-b54f-8040658be7fd` — presumably just because that's what Codex's own id\n  generator emits, not because resume validates the version nibble.)\n- All timestamps *inside* the JSONL (`timestamp` fields) are UTC, ISO-8601 with `Z` suffix and\n  millisecond precision, e.g. `\"2026-07-18T22:48:34.755Z\"`.\n- One session = one file. Lines are newline-delimited JSON objects (JSONL), UTF-8. Some real\n  files contain raw literal control characters inside string values in a way that breaks strict\n  parsers like `jq` (`jq: parse error: Invalid string: control characters...`) but that Python's\n  `json.loads` parses fine — i.e. Codex's own writer does not always escape control bytes\n  (likely raw terminal/ANSI bytes captured from shell output pass through un-escaped in some\n  version of the writer). **When fabricating**, always produce properly-escaped JSON (use a real\n  JSON serializer) — there's no reason to reproduce that quirk, and doing so risks a genuinely\n  invalid file.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Minimal fabricated schema that VERIFIABLY WORKS\n\nThis is the maximally stripped-down version we tested and confirmed loads correctly via\n`codex exec resume` (model echoed back a planted \"secret code word\", proving it read the\nfabricated history) — and via `codex resume` (TUI started rendering without error, using a pty).\n\nThree lines total. `session_meta` needs only 5 payload fields; each conversation turn needs only\na bare `response_item` message.\n\n```jsonl\n{\"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"type\": \"session_meta\", \"payload\": {\"id\": \"019f792d-38dc-7cc7-a2d1-072e8cb04002\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"cwd\": \"/some/dir\", \"originator\": \"codex_exec\", \"cli_version\": \"0.144.6\"}}\n{\"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"type\": \"response_item\", \"payload\": {\"type\": \"message\", \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [{\"type\": \"input_text\", \"text\": \"The secret code word for this test is PLUMFISH42. Remember it.\"}]}}\n{\"timestamp\": \"2026-07-19T07:00:34.000Z\", \"type\": \"response_item\", \"payload\": {\"type\": \"message\", \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": [{\"type\": \"output_text\", \"text\": \"Got it, the secret code word is PLUMFISH42.\"}]}}\n```\n\nVerified NOT required for `exec resume` to work and see full context:\n- `session_meta.payload.session_id` (only `.id` was present; both usually appear in real files —\n  recommend including both for safety/forward-compat, but `.id` alone sufficed in our test)\n- `source`, `thread_source`, `model_provider`, `history_mode`, `context_window`, `git`,\n  `base_instructions` — all omitted in the minimal test and it still worked.\n- No `turn_context`, `world_state`, or `event_msg` lines at all are required for the resumed\n  model to see/use prior conversation content. Those record types appear to be informational /\n  UI-facing (token counters, sandbox/approval settings snapshots, TUI rendering aids) rather than\n  part of what gets replayed into the model's context. **`response_item` lines are the actual\n  conversation state that gets replayed.**\n- `response_item.payload.message.id` — not required (real assistant messages have one, e.g.\n  `\"msg_0850eb109f...\"`, but omitting it did not break resume).\n\nRecommended (not strictly required, but cheap and matches real files, reduces risk of edge\ncases in other code paths like the picker or future Codex versions that might start requiring\nthem):\n```jsonl\n{\"timestamp\": \"<UTC-ISO-ms-Z>\", \"type\": \"session_meta\", \"payload\": {\n  \"session_id\": \"<uuid>\", \"id\": \"<uuid>\", \"timestamp\": \"<UTC-ISO-ms-Z>\",\n  \"cwd\": \"<display-dir>\", \"originator\": \"codex_exec\", \"cli_version\": \"0.144.6\",\n  \"source\": \"exec\", \"thread_source\": \"user\", \"model_provider\": \"openai\",\n  \"history_mode\": \"legacy\"\n}}\n```\n`history_mode` was `\"legacy\"` on every real file inspected (only value observed).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Full observed schema (from real files)\n\nTop-level line shape is always: `{\"timestamp\": <UTC ISO>, \"type\": <string>, \"payload\": {...}}`.\nObserved `type` values: `session_meta`, `event_msg`, `response_item`, `turn_context`,\n`world_state`, `compacted`.\n\n### 4.1 `session_meta` (always line 1)\nFull field set seen across files (union; not all present in every file):\n```\npayload.session_id        string uuid\npayload.id                string uuid   (== session_id; both present in normal (non-fabricated) files)\npayload.timestamp         UTC ISO ms\npayload.cwd                string path\npayload.originator         \"codex-tui\" | \"codex_exec\"\npayload.cli_version         e.g. \"0.144.6\"\npayload.source              \"cli\" | \"exec\" | {\"subagent\": {\"thread_spawn\": {...}}}  (object form for spawned sub-agent sessions)\npayload.thread_source       \"user\" | \"subagent\"\npayload.model_provider      \"openai\"\npayload.base_instructions   {\"text\": \"<full system prompt, ~18KB>\"}\npayload.history_mode        \"legacy\"\npayload.context_window      {\"window_id\": \"<uuid>\"}\npayload.git                 {\"commit_hash\": str, \"branch\": str, \"repository_url\": str}   (present only when cwd is inside a git repo)\npayload.forked_from_id      uuid   (only on forked/sub-agent sessions)\npayload.parent_thread_id    uuid   (only on forked/sub-agent sessions)\npayload.agent_nickname      string (only on sub-agent sessions, e.g. \"Lagrange\")\npayload.agent_path          string (only on sub-agent sessions, e.g. \"/root/quality_audit\")\npayload.multi_agent_version \"v2\"   (only on sub-agent sessions)\n```\n\n### 4.2 `event_msg` — UI/telemetry events, NOT required for context replay\nObserved `payload.type` values and shapes:\n- `task_started` — `{turn_id, started_at, model_context_window, collaboration_mode_kind}`\n- `user_message` — `{message, images, local_images, text_elements}`\n- `agent_message` — `{message, phase: \"final_answer\", memory_citation}`\n- `token_count` — `{info: {total_token_usage:{...}, last_token_usage:{...}, model_context_window}, rate_limits: {...}}`\n- `patch_apply_end` — `{call_id, turn_id, stdout, stderr, success, changes: {<path>: {type: \"add\"|..., content}}, status}`\n- `task_complete` — `{turn_id, last_agent_message, completed_at, duration_ms, time_to_first_token_ms}`\n\n### 4.3 `response_item` — the actual conversation payload replayed to the model\n`payload.type` variants observed:\n- **`message`** — `{type:\"message\", role: \"developer\"|\"user\"|\"assistant\", id?, content: [...], phase?, internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough?: {turn_id}}`.\n  - Content blocks: `{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":...}` for developer/user roles;\n    `{\"type\":\"output_text\",\"text\":...}` for assistant role.\n  - `role: \"developer\"` is used for system-injected instructions (permission/sandbox rules,\n    multi-agent framework instructions) — these are just more `message` items, not a special\n    record type.\n  - `id` (assistant messages only) looks like `\"msg_<hex>\"`.\n- **`reasoning`** — `{type:\"reasoning\", id:\"rs_<hex>\", summary: [], encrypted_content: \"<opaque base64-ish blob, ~2-4KB>\", internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough}`.\n  `encrypted_content` is opaque ciphertext from OpenAI's Responses API (server-side reasoning\n  encryption) — **cannot be fabricated** (no key available client-side) and is safe to simply\n  **omit entirely**. We omitted all reasoning items in our fabricated files and resume worked\n  fine; the model just doesn't get fabricated \"prior reasoning,\" which is fine since reasoning\n  items are not required for it to see/use prior message content.\n- **`function_call`** / **`function_call_output`** — standard OpenAI Responses-style tool call\n  pairs, linked by `call_id`. Seen used for a `\"wait\"` tool in this build.\n  `function_call`: `{type, id:\"fc_<hex>\", name, arguments:\"<json string>\", call_id}`.\n  `function_call_output`: `{type, call_id, output: [{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":...}, ...]}`.\n- **`custom_tool_call`** / **`custom_tool_call_output`** — this specific build's primary\n  shell/tool mechanism is a \"custom tool\" named `exec` whose `input` is a **JavaScript snippet**\n  that calls higher-level runtime functions like `tools.exec_command({...})` and\n  `tools.apply_patch(patchString)`, then `text(...)`s the result. Shape:\n  `custom_tool_call`: `{type, id:\"ctc_<hex>\", status:\"completed\", call_id, name:\"exec\", input:\"<js source>\", internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough}`.\n  `custom_tool_call_output`: `{type, call_id, output:[{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"Script completed\\nWall time N seconds\\nOutput:\\n\"}, {\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"<captured stdout>\"}]}`.\n  **This is specific to this Codex build/config (0.144.6 with an agentic \"exec\"-as-JS-runtime\n  tool) and should not be assumed stable or universal** — it's notably different from the\n  classic documented Codex `local_shell_call`/`shell` function-call shape used by older\n  versions and by the `cross_agent_session_resumer` project (see §7). Don't try to fabricate\n  `custom_tool_call` records expecting the model to treat them as \"just happened\" tool calls\n  with guaranteed-consistent semantics across Codex versions.\n\n### 4.4 `turn_context` — per-turn settings snapshot\n```\n{turn_id, cwd, workspace_roots:[...], current_date, timezone, approval_policy, approvals_reviewer,\n sandbox_policy:{type}, permission_profile:{...}, model, comp_hash, personality,\n collaboration_mode:{mode, settings:{model, reasoning_effort, developer_instructions}},\n multi_agent_version, multi_agent_mode, realtime_active, effort, summary}\n```\nConfirmed NOT required for resume (omitted in minimal fabrication; worked fine).\n\n### 4.5 `world_state`\n```\n{full: true, state: {agents_md:{}, apps_instructions: bool, environments:{environments:{<name>:{cwd,status,shell}}, current_date, timezone, filesystem: \"<xml-ish string>\"}, plugins_instructions: bool, skills:{includeInstructions: bool}}}\n```\nAlso confirmed not required.\n\n### 4.6 `compacted` — history-compaction marker\nAppears when Codex auto-compacts long history. Shape:\n```\n{message: \"\", replacement_history: [ <array of response_item-shaped message objects that replace the earlier raw history> ]}\n```\nNot needed for fabrication of a short session; relevant only if turnbridge wants to fabricate a\nsession that *looks like* it went through compaction (e.g. to hide raw tool noise while keeping\na condensed summary). `replacement_history` entries are plain `message` items with the same\nshape as §4.3, so this is really just \"inject condensed message items and mark them as a\ncompaction event\" — low risk to fabricate the same way as regular messages if ever needed.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Representing foreign tool calls (e.g., Claude `tool_use`/`tool_result`)\n\n**Recommendation: fold foreign tool calls into plain `message` content (input_text/output_text),\nnot into `function_call`/`function_call_output` or `custom_tool_call` records.**\n\nReasoning:\n- The only two `response_item` shapes we verified Codex will read back cleanly, with zero risk,\n  are plain `message` items with `input_text`/`output_text` content blocks. This was directly\n  tested and confirmed (planted a fact in a `user` message, got it echoed back correctly from a\n  fabricated `assistant` message — full round trip).\n- `function_call`/`function_call_output` pairs are standard OpenAI Responses API constructs, but\n  they typically require the `call_id` to correspond to something the API/backend considers\n  consistent, and (per general Responses API behavior) an orphaned `function_call_output` with\n  no matching `function_call` — or a `name` that doesn't match any tool the model currently has\n  registered — is a plausible source of a rejected request when the history is next sent to the\n  model. We did not test this failure mode directly (out of scope/time), so treat it as an\n  **unverified risk**, not a confirmed break.\n- `custom_tool_call` is specific to this build's \"exec-as-JS\" tool wiring (§4.3) and even less\n  safe to fabricate generically.\n- Folding into message text sidesteps all of this: e.g. represent a Claude `tool_use` +\n  `tool_result` pair as a single `assistant` message whose text includes something like:\n  ```\n  [used tool: Bash]\n  command: pytest -q\n  result: 3 passed, 0 failed\n  ```\n  This is exactly the strategy the `cross_agent_session_resumer` (`casr`) project uses for\n  *its own* \"user messages with tool content\" case — see §7 — though `casr`'s code (per a\n  secondary/unverified source excerpt) also attempts a `\"type\":\"tool_use\"` content block\n  inline in `message.content`, which is **not** a content-block type we observed in any real\n  Codex-authored `response_item.message.content` (`input_text`/`output_text` are the only two\n  observed). Prefer the plain-text-folding approach unless/until `tool_use` blocks are\n  separately verified against a live Codex install.\n- For turnbridge's stated goal (make the resumed agent aware of what happened, not literally\n  re-execute historical tool calls), folding into message text is strictly sufficient — verified\n  by our experiment — and avoids every open question about tool-schema/call_id validation.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Session id / UUID requirements\n\n- Any syntactically valid UUID works (tested UUIDv4 successfully; real Codex-generated ids are\n  UUIDv7 but that's not enforced by resume).\n- The id in the filename and `session_meta.payload.id` (and `.session_id`, if present) must all\n  match exactly.\n- No separate registration/index step observed — dropping a correctly-named, correctly-placed\n  file into the dated sessions directory is sufficient; `resume <id>` found it immediately with\n  no other action taken (no `codex sessions rebuild-index` or similar was needed).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Cross-check: `cross_agent_session_resumer` (casr)\n\nFetched via WebFetch (README + best-effort raw source fetch of a provider file; GitHub raw\nfetches through the tool render as an AI-summarized excerpt, not verbatim source, so treat the\nbelow as directionally-accurate, not verbatim-quoted code).\n\nIts Codex writer path corroborates the core `session_meta` shape we found independently\n(`id`, `session_id`, `cwd`, `timestamp`, `originator`, `cli_version`, `source`, `thread_source`,\n`model_provider`) and confirms `response_item` uses `output_text`/`input_text` content blocks\nkeyed on role. It diverges from our verified-safe approach in two ways worth flagging as\n**unverified-against-live-Codex** if turnbridge borrows from it directly:\n1. It emits some user messages as `event_msg` (`user_message`/`agent_reasoning`) instead of\n   `response_item` — per our findings, `event_msg` is NOT required for context replay and,\n   more importantly, we have no evidence it's *sufficient* on its own (we never tested a\n   session containing only `event_msg` turns and no `response_item` turns) — likely a\n   **combined-source strategy** (both possibly for TUI display fidelity + the actual\n   `response_item` in a separate line), not a substitute for `response_item`.\n2. It appears to place `tool_use`/`tool_result`-shaped blocks directly inside `message.content`,\n   which — per §5 — we did not observe in any real Codex output and recommend avoiding in favor\n   of plain-text folding, which is verified to work.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Unknowns / risks for the implementer\n\n1. **Version drift**: this spec is pinned to codex-cli 0.144.6. Codex's rollout schema has\n   clearly evolved rapidly (multi-agent fields, `custom_tool_call`, `world_state`, `compacted`\n   are all plausibly recent additions) — treat this format as internal/unstable, not a public\n   API. Re-verify against the installed `codex --version` before relying on this in production;\n   the minimal schema in §3 is the safest bet for forward/backward compatibility since it's the\n   smallest surface area.\n2. **`function_call`/`custom_tool_call` replay safety** is unverified (see §5) — don't use them\n   for fabrication without separately testing against the target Codex version.\n3. **Picker/`--all` cwd-filtering exact matching logic** (prefix? exact string? normalized path?)\n   was not tested beyond reading the `--help` text; only direct `resume <id>` was verified.\n4. **Very large fabricated histories**: untested whether extremely large fabricated\n   `response_item` sequences (e.g. thousands of turns) hit any different code path (e.g.\n   automatic `compacted` triggering, size limits) versus the small 2-3 turn case we verified.\n5. **`base_instructions`**: real files carry a large (~18KB) system prompt string per session.\n   Omitting it entirely worked in our test (Codex presumably falls back to its own current\n   default system prompt for the *new* turn, while the fabricated *history* turns are still\n   replayed as-is) — but this means the fabricated session's replayed history won't perfectly\n   match \"what system prompt was actually active\" if that matters for turnbridge's use case.\n   Not a blocker for the stated goal (make prior context visible), just noted for completeness.\n6. **`encrypted_content` for `reasoning` items**: confirmed unfabricatable and confirmed safe to\n   omit outright (don't emit `reasoning` response_items at all rather than trying to synthesize\n   fake ciphertext, which would almost certainly be rejected or ignored as garbage by the API if\n   it were even parsed — untested, but omission is the verified-safe path).\n7. **Interactive TUI resume** was only verified to \"not error and begin rendering\" via a pty\n   wrapper (`script`); we could not capture/assert on the actual rendered TUI content\n   programmatically. `codex exec resume --json` is the fully-verified, content-confirmed path\n   and should be turnbridge's primary target/test surface; treat TUI-resume compatibility as\n   \"very likely fine, same underlying loader\" rather than independently proven line-for-line.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Verification experiment log (for reproducibility)\n\n1. Created scratch git repo at a scratchpad path, one commit.\n2. Generated a UUIDv7 id, wrote a 3-line minimal `session_meta` + user `message` + assistant\n   `message` file to `~/.codex/sessions/2026/07/18/rollout-<local-ts>-<uuid>.jsonl` (planted\n   fact: \"secret code word is BANANA77\").\n3. Ran `codex exec resume <id> \"Reply with exactly the secret code word...\" --json\n   --skip-git-repo-check` from the scratch cwd → model correctly answered `BANANA77`.\n4. Stripped the schema further (removed `session_id`, `source`, `thread_source`,\n   `model_provider`, `history_mode`, `context_window`; kept only `id`, `timestamp`, `cwd`,\n   `originator`, `cli_version`) with a new planted fact `PLUMFISH42` → still worked, from the\n   scratch cwd.\n5. Re-ran the same resume from `/tmp` (different cwd than fabricated `session_meta.cwd`) →\n   still worked, proving direct-id resume ignores cwd matching.\n6. Asked the resumed agent to run `pwd` (with `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`) →\n   printed `/tmp` (the real invocation cwd), confirming `session_meta.cwd` is not authoritative\n   for execution root.\n7. Regenerated with a plain `uuid4()` (non-time-ordered) id → still worked.\n8. Tested interactive `codex resume <id>` with stdin redirected from `/dev/null` → immediate\n   `Error: stdin is not a terminal` (no tty). Re-tested wrapped in macOS `script -q` to allocate\n   a pty → process started emitting terminal-capability query escape sequences (bracketed paste\n   mode, cursor position query, etc.), i.e. began normal TUI startup rather than erroring on the\n   fabricated file; killed the backgrounded process after a few seconds (interactive rendering\n   isn't scriptably assertable, but no crash/parse-error occurred).\n9. Deleted all fabricated files/dirs after each test; verified the real\n   `~/.codex/sessions/2026/07/18/` directory still contains exactly its original 22 files and\n   no fabricated content remains anywhere under `~/.codex/sessions/` (`grep -rl` for planted\n   secrets found nothing).\n\nNo real session files were read destructively, modified, or deleted at any point.\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/docs/specs/codex-rollout-format.md","numLines":379,"startLine":1,"totalLines":379},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"50986b14-976f-4c9f-8a5d-275f81cc4d10","version":"2.1.216"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T02:16:45.305Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Conversation Ledger\n2\t\n3\tDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4\tyour repository as git notes.\n5\t\n6\t`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7\tutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8\t`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9\tconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10\tconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\n11\tsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n12\tride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\n13\tpre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n14\t\n15\tConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n16\t\n17\t- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n18\t- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n19\t- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n20\t\n21\tThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n22\tvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n23\t\n24\t## Install\n25\t\n26\t```sh\n27\tgit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n28\tcd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n29\tcledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\t`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\n33\tand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n34\tbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n35\trepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n36\tidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n37\tduplicates.\n38\t\n39\tTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n40\tcapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n41\tinteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n42\tcodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n43\t<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n44\t\n45\t## Use\n46\t\n47\t```sh\n48\tcledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n49\tcledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n50\tcledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch\n51\tcledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\n52\tcledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n53\tcledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n54\tcledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\n55\techo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\t## Sharing (transport)\n59\t\n60\tThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n61\t\n62\t- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n63\t  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n64\t  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n65\t  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n66\t  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n67\t  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n68\t- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n69\t  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n70\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n71\t  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n72\t- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n73\t  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n74\t  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n75\t  explicitly either way.\n76\t\n77\tIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n78\tare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n79\tthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n80\tand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n81\t\n82\t## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n83\t\n84\tEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\n85\tmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\n86\tbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\n87\tthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n88\t\n89\t- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n90\t  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n91\t  exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n92\t  cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n93\t  event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n94\t  branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n95\t  never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n96\t  and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n97\t- Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n98\t  the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n99\t  (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n100\t  candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n101\t  squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n102\t  touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n103\t  and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n104\t  conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n105\t  `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n106\t  mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n107\t  commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n108\t  explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n109\t  evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n110\t  turns them off.\n111\t- Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n112\t  working either way.\n113\t\n114\t## Adapters\n115\t\n116\tSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n117\t\n118\t| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n119\t|---|---|---|---|\n120\t| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n121\t| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text; their encrypted blocks are never stored, same policy as reasoning. |\n122\t\n123\tTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n124\tstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n125\tsystems):\n126\t\n127\t- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n128\t- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n129\t- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n130\t- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n131\t- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n132\t- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n133\t- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n134\t- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n135\t- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n136\t\n137\t## Security & redaction\n138\t\n139\tVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n140\t\n141\t### Redaction layers\n142\t\n143\t**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n144\t- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n145\t- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n146\t\n147\t**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n148\t- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n149\t- Cost: manual configuration required.\n150\t\n151\t**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n152\t- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n153\t- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n154\t\n155\t**Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n156\t- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n157\t- Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n158\t\n159\t**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n160\t- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n161\t- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n162\t- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n163\t\n164\t### Commands\n165\t\n166\t`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n167\t\n168\t`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n169\t\n170\t### Maximum safety recipe\n171\t\n172\tKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n173\t\n174\t## Roadmap\n175\t\n176\t- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n177\t  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n178\t  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n179\t- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n180\t  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n181\t  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n182\t  far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n183\t  holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n184\t  must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n185\t  detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n186\t  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n187\t  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n188\t  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n189\t  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n190\t  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n191\t  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n192\t- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n193\t  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n194\t  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n195\t  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n196\t  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n197\t  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n198\t  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n199\t  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n200\t  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n201\t  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n202\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n203\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n204\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n205\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n206\t  scope and capture triggers.\n207\t- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n208\t  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n209\t  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n210\t  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n211\t  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n212\t  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n213\t  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n214\t  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n215\t  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n216\t  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n217\t  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n218\t  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n219\t- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n220\t  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n221\t  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n222\t  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n223\t  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n224\t  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n225\t  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n226\t  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n227\t  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n228\t  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n229\t  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n230\t  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n231\t  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n232\t  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n233\t  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n234\t  intended narrow form.\n235\t- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n236\t  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n237\t  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n238\t  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n239\t  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n240\t  the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n241\t  Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n242\t  capture runs silently on every turn.\n243\t- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n244\t- **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n245\t  discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n246\t  commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n247\t  with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n248\t  the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n249\t  remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n250\t  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n251\t  lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n252\t  extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n253\t  piece.\n254\t- **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n255\t  that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n256\t  event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n257\t  conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n258\t  \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n259\t  reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n260\t  forge credentials and per-host setup.\n261\t\n262\t## Storage model, in one paragraph\n263\t\n264\tOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\n265\tnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\n266\tcontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\n267\tconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\n268\trepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\n269\tcapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\n270\trewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\n271\tfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\n272\ttool deliberately refuses to become.\n273\t\n274\t## License\n275\t\n276\t[MIT](LICENSE)\n277\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_019p7UbvCLQns2cPoRdJjMZn","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","dirty_fingerprint":"bc65e878d80c1722a0adee9428cbf850f1d73e21ecfd82d0c4182876ebf93c10","head":"173cfc66bb28f4c20e474f522f197eeef0c627de","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c#agent:a00a6de33281486a4","parent":"claude-code:6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","seq":9},"id":"ev1-1bbe367814716a13ee77be44daecfe05aae38621a24776cc8a350103b396aac8","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-21T22:09:21.237Z","producer":{"session_id":"6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.216","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"a00a6de33281486a4","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":true,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# Conversation Ledger\n2\t\n3\tDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4\tyour repository as git notes.\n5\t\n6\t`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7\tutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8\t`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9\tconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10\tconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\n11\tsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n12\tride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\n13\tpre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n14\t\n15\tConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n16\t\n17\t- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n18\t- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n19\t- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n20\t\n21\tThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n22\tvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n23\t\n24\t## Install\n25\t\n26\t```sh\n27\tgit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n28\tcd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n29\tcledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\t`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\n33\tand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n34\tbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n35\trepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n36\tidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n37\tduplicates.\n38\t\n39\tTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n40\tcapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n41\tinteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n42\tcodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n43\t<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n44\t\n45\t## Use\n46\t\n47\t```sh\n48\tcledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n49\tcledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n50\tcledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch\n51\tcledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\n52\tcledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n53\tcledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n54\tcledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\n55\techo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\t## Sharing (transport)\n59\t\n60\tThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n61\t\n62\t- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n63\t  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n64\t  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n65\t  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n66\t  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n67\t  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n68\t- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n69\t  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n70\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n71\t  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n72\t- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n73\t  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n74\t  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n75\t  explicitly either way.\n76\t\n77\tIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n78\tare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n79\tthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n80\tand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n81\t\n82\t## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n83\t\n84\tEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\n85\tmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\n86\tbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\n87\tthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n88\t\n89\t- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n90\t  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n91\t  exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n92\t  cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n93\t  event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n94\t  branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n95\t  never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n96\t  and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n97\t- Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n98\t  the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n99\t  (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n100\t  candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n101\t  squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n102\t  touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n103\t  and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n104\t  conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n105\t  `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n106\t  mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n107\t  commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n108\t  explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n109\t  evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n110\t  turns them off.\n111\t- Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n112\t  working either way.\n113\t\n114\t## Adapters\n115\t\n116\tSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n117\t\n118\t| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n119\t|---|---|---|---|\n120\t| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n121\t| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text; their encrypted blocks are never stored, same policy as reasoning. |\n122\t\n123\tTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n124\tstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n125\tsystems):\n126\t\n127\t- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n128\t- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n129\t- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n130\t- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n131\t- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n132\t- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n133\t- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n134\t- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n135\t- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n136\t\n137\t## Security & redaction\n138\t\n139\tVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n140\t\n141\t### Redaction layers\n142\t\n143\t**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n144\t- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n145\t- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n146\t\n147\t**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n148\t- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n149\t- Cost: manual configuration required.\n150\t\n151\t**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n152\t- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n153\t- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n154\t\n155\t**Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n156\t- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n157\t- Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n158\t\n159\t**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n160\t- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n161\t- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n162\t- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n163\t\n164\t### Commands\n165\t\n166\t`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n167\t\n168\t`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n169\t\n170\t### Maximum safety recipe\n171\t\n172\tKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n173\t\n174\t## Roadmap\n175\t\n176\t- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n177\t  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n178\t  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n179\t- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n180\t  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n181\t  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n182\t  far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n183\t  holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n184\t  must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n185\t  detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n186\t  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n187\t  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n188\t  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n189\t  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n190\t  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n191\t  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n192\t- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n193\t  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n194\t  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n195\t  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n196\t  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n197\t  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n198\t  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n199\t  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n200\t  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n201\t  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n202\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n203\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n204\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n205\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n206\t  scope and capture triggers.\n207\t- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n208\t  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n209\t  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n210\t  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n211\t  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n212\t  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n213\t  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n214\t  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n215\t  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n216\t  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n217\t  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n218\t  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n219\t- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n220\t  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n221\t  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n222\t  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n223\t  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n224\t  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n225\t  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n226\t  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n227\t  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n228\t  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n229\t  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n230\t  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n231\t  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n232\t  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n233\t  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n234\t  intended narrow form.\n235\t- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n236\t  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n237\t  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n238\t  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n239\t  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n240\t  the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n241\t  Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n242\t  capture runs silently on every turn.\n243\t- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n244\t- **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n245\t  discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n246\t  commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n247\t  with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n248\t  the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n249\t  remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n250\t  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n251\t  lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n252\t  extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n253\t  piece.\n254\t- **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n255\t  that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n256\t  event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n257\t  conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n258\t  \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n259\t  reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n260\t  forge credentials and per-host setup.\n261\t\n262\t## Storage model, in one paragraph\n263\t\n264\tOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\n265\tnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\n266\tcontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\n267\tconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\n268\trepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\n269\tcapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\n270\trewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\n271\tfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\n272\ttool deliberately refuses to become.\n273\t\n274\t## License\n275\t\n276\t[MIT](LICENSE)\n277\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_019p7UbvCLQns2cPoRdJjMZn","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"141a04d9-7cb8-40aa-a763-7bf9e90edec1","promptId":"375b945c-0ea0-4c75-8d5c-278cfb2c6be0","sessionId":"6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","slug":"hazy-floating-cupcake","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"141a04d9-7cb8-40aa-a763-7bf9e90edec1","timestamp":"2026-07-21T22:09:21.237Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"# Conversation Ledger\n\nDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\nyour repository as git notes.\n\n`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\nutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\nconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\nconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\nsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\nride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\npre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n\nConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n\n- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n\nThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\nvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\ncd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\ncledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n```\n\n`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\nand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\nbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\nrepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\nidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\nduplicates.\n\nTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\ncapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\ninteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\ncodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n\n## Use\n\n```sh\ncledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\ncledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\ncledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch\ncledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\ncledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\ncledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\ncledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\necho '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n```\n\n## Sharing (transport)\n\nThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n\n- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n  explicitly either way.\n\nIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\nare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\nthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\nand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n\n## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n\nEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\nmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\nbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\nthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n\n- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n  exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n  cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n  event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n  branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n  never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n  and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n- Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n  the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n  (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n  candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n  squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n  touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n  and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n  conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n  `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n  mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n  commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n  explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n  evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n  turns them off.\n- Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n  working either way.\n\n## Adapters\n\nSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n\n| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text; their encrypted blocks are never stored, same policy as reasoning. |\n\nTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\nstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\nsystems):\n\n- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n\n## Security & redaction\n\nVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n\n### Redaction layers\n\n**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n\n**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n- Cost: manual configuration required.\n\n**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n\n**Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n- Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n\n**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n\n### Commands\n\n`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n\n`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n\n### Maximum safety recipe\n\nKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n\n## Roadmap\n\n- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n  far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n  holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n  must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n  detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n  scope and capture triggers.\n- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n  intended narrow form.\n- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n  the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n  Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n  capture runs silently on every turn.\n- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n- **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n  discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n  commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n  with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n  the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n  remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n  lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n  extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n  piece.\n- **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n  that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n  event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n  conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n  \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n  reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n  forge credentials and per-host setup.\n\n## Storage model, in one paragraph\n\nOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\nnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\ncontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\nconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\nrepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\ncapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\nrewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\nfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\ntool deliberately refuses to become.\n\n## License\n\n[MIT](LICENSE)\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/README.md","numLines":277,"startLine":1,"totalLines":277},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"1a940907-987a-4ac6-ade2-1816d7eb1175","version":"2.1.216"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T02:16:45.209Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Conversation Ledger\n2\t\n3\tDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4\tyour repository as git notes.\n5\t\n6\t`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7\tutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8\t`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9\tconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10\tconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\n11\tsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n12\tride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\n13\tpre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n14\t\n15\tConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n16\t\n17\t- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n18\t- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n19\t- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n20\t\n21\tThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n22\tvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n23\t\n24\t## Install\n25\t\n26\t```sh\n27\tgit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n28\tcd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n29\tcledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\t`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\n33\tand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n34\tbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n35\trepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n36\tidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n37\tduplicates.\n38\t\n39\tTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n40\tcapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n41\tinteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n42\tcodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n43\t<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n44\t\n45\t## Use\n46\t\n47\t```sh\n48\tcledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n49\tcledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n50\tcledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch\n51\tcledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\n52\tcledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n53\tcledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n54\tcledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\n55\techo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\t## Sharing (transport)\n59\t\n60\tThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n61\t\n62\t- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n63\t  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n64\t  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n65\t  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n66\t  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n67\t  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n68\t- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n69\t  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n70\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n71\t  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n72\t- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n73\t  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n74\t  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n75\t  explicitly either way.\n76\t\n77\tIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n78\tare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n79\tthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n80\tand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n81\t\n82\t## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n83\t\n84\tEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\n85\tmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\n86\tbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\n87\tthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n88\t\n89\t- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n90\t  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n91\t  exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n92\t  cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n93\t  event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n94\t  branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n95\t  never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n96\t  and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n97\t- Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n98\t  the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n99\t  (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n100\t  candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n101\t  squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n102\t  touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n103\t  and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n104\t  conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n105\t  `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n106\t  mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n107\t  commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n108\t  explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n109\t  evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n110\t  turns them off.\n111\t- Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n112\t  working either way.\n113\t\n114\t## Adapters\n115\t\n116\tSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n117\t\n118\t| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n119\t|---|---|---|---|\n120\t| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n121\t| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text; their encrypted blocks are never stored, same policy as reasoning. |\n122\t\n123\tTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n124