d assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n160\t- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n161\t- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n162\t- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n163\t\n164\t### Commands\n165\t\n166\t`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n167\t\n168\t`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n169\t\n170\t### Maximum safety recipe\n171\t\n172\tKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n173\t\n174\t## Roadmap\n175\t\n176\t- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n177\t  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n178\t  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n179\t- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n180\t  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n181\t  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n182\t  far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n183\t  holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n184\t  must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n185\t  detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n186\t  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n187\t  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n188\t  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n189\t  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n190\t  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n191\t  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n192\t- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n193\t  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n194\t  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n195\t  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n196\t  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n197\t  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n198\t  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n199\t  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n200\t  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n201\t  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n202\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n203\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n204\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n205\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n206\t  scope and capture triggers.\n207\t- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n208\t  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n209\t  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n210\t  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n211\t  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n212\t  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n213\t  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n214\t  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n215\t  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n216\t  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n217\t  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n218\t  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n219\t- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n220\t  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n221\t  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n222\t  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n223\t  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n224\t  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n225\t  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n226\t  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n227\t  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n228\t  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n229\t  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n230\t  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n231\t  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n232\t  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n233\t  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n234\t  intended narrow form.\n235\t- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n236\t  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n237\t  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n238\t  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n239\t  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n240\t  the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n241\t  Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n242\t  capture runs silently on every turn.\n243\t- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n244\t- **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n245\t  discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n246\t  commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n247\t  with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n248\t  the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n249\t  remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n250\t  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n251\t  lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n252\t  extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n253\t  piece.\n254\t- **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n255\t  that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n256\t  event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n257\t  conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n258\t  \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n259\t  reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n260\t  forge credentials and per-host setup.\n261\t\n262\t## Storage model, in one paragraph\n263\t\n264\tOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\n265\tnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\n266\tcontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\n267\tconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\n268\trepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\n269\tcapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\n270\trewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\n271\tfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\n272\ttool deliberately refuses to become.\n273\t\n274\t## License\n275\t\n276\t[MIT](LICENSE)\n277\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CfVJfJskQj5LfVwp2m5AU4","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","dirty_fingerprint":"bc65e878d80c1722a0adee9428cbf850f1d73e21ecfd82d0c4182876ebf93c10","head":"173cfc66bb28f4c20e474f522f197eeef0c627de","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c#agent:a5c627b890fdc1883","parent":"claude-code:6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","seq":11},"id":"ev1-cfb7cf4752ed27fc3491e34ceca5edb4f90e2be557609d396ad5f306f3d65824","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-21T21:21:19.648Z","producer":{"session_id":"6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.216","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"a5c627b890fdc1883","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":true,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# Conversation Ledger\n2\t\n3\tDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4\tyour repository as git notes.\n5\t\n6\t`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7\tutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8\t`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9\tconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10\tconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\n11\tsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n12\tride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\n13\tpre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n14\t\n15\tConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n16\t\n17\t- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n18\t- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n19\t- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n20\t\n21\tThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n22\tvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n23\t\n24\t## Install\n25\t\n26\t```sh\n27\tgit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n28\tcd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n29\tcledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\t`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\n33\tand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n34\tbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n35\trepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n36\tidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n37\tduplicates.\n38\t\n39\tTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n40\tcapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n41\tinteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n42\tcodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n43\t<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n44\t\n45\t## Use\n46\t\n47\t```sh\n48\tcledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n49\tcledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n50\tcledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch\n51\tcledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\n52\tcledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n53\tcledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n54\tcledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\n55\techo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\t## Sharing (transport)\n59\t\n60\tThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n61\t\n62\t- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n63\t  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n64\t  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n65\t  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n66\t  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n67\t  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n68\t- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n69\t  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n70\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n71\t  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n72\t- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n73\t  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n74\t  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n75\t  explicitly either way.\n76\t\n77\tIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n78\tare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n79\tthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n80\tand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n81\t\n82\t## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n83\t\n84\tEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\n85\tmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\n86\tbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\n87\tthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n88\t\n89\t- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n90\t  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n91\t  exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n92\t  cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n93\t  event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n94\t  branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n95\t  never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n96\t  and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n97\t- Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n98\t  the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n99\t  (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n100\t  candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n101\t  squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n102\t  touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n103\t  and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n104\t  conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n105\t  `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n106\t  mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n107\t  commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n108\t  explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n109\t  evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n110\t  turns them off.\n111\t- Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n112\t  working either way.\n113\t\n114\t## Adapters\n115\t\n116\tSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n117\t\n118\t| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n119\t|---|---|---|---|\n120\t| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n121\t| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text; their encrypted blocks are never stored, same policy as reasoning. |\n122\t\n123\tTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n124\tstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n125\tsystems):\n126\t\n127\t- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n128\t- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n129\t- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n130\t- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n131\t- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n132\t- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n133\t- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n134\t- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n135\t- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n136\t\n137\t## Security & redaction\n138\t\n139\tVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n140\t\n141\t### Redaction layers\n142\t\n143\t**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n144\t- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n145\t- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n146\t\n147\t**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n148\t- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n149\t- Cost: manual configuration required.\n150\t\n151\t**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n152\t- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n153\t- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n154\t\n155\t**Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n156\t- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n157\t- Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n158\t\n159\t**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n160\t- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n161\t- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n162\t- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n163\t\n164\t### Commands\n165\t\n166\t`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n167\t\n168\t`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n169\t\n170\t### Maximum safety recipe\n171\t\n172\tKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n173\t\n174\t## Roadmap\n175\t\n176\t- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n177\t  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n178\t  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n179\t- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n180\t  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n181\t  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n182\t  far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n183\t  holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n184\t  must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n185\t  detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n186\t  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n187\t  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n188\t  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n189\t  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n190\t  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n191\t  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n192\t- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n193\t  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n194\t  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n195\t  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n196\t  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n197\t  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n198\t  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n199\t  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n200\t  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n201\t  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n202\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n203\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n204\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n205\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n206\t  scope and capture triggers.\n207\t- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n208\t  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n209\t  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n210\t  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n211\t  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n212\t  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n213\t  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n214\t  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n215\t  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n216\t  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n217\t  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n218\t  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n219\t- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n220\t  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n221\t  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n222\t  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n223\t  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n224\t  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n225\t  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n226\t  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n227\t  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n228\t  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n229\t  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n230\t  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n231\t  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n232\t  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n233\t  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n234\t  intended narrow form.\n235\t- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n236\t  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n237\t  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n238\t  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n239\t  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n240\t  the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n241\t  Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n242\t  capture runs silently on every turn.\n243\t- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n244\t- **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n245\t  discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n246\t  commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n247\t  with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n248\t  the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n249\t  remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n250\t  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n251\t  lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n252\t  extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n253\t  piece.\n254\t- **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n255\t  that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n256\t  event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n257\t  conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n258\t  \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n259\t  reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n260\t  forge credentials and per-host setup.\n261\t\n262\t## Storage model, in one paragraph\n263\t\n264\tOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\n265\tnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\n266\tcontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\n267\tconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\n268\trepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\n269\tcapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\n270\trewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\n271\tfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\n272\ttool deliberately refuses to become.\n273\t\n274\t## License\n275\t\n276\t[MIT](LICENSE)\n277\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CfVJfJskQj5LfVwp2m5AU4","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"36b352cb-19d7-4b48-aac6-ff25da49661c","promptId":"5baaa4e1-1fa5-494b-8425-5ca6e70ddfd1","sessionId":"6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","slug":"hazy-floating-cupcake","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"36b352cb-19d7-4b48-aac6-ff25da49661c","timestamp":"2026-07-21T21:21:19.648Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"# Conversation Ledger\n\nDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\nyour repository as git notes.\n\n`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\nutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\nconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\nconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\nsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\nride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\npre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n\nConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n\n- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n\nThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\nvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\ncd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\ncledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n```\n\n`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\nand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\nbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\nrepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\nidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\nduplicates.\n\nTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\ncapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\ninteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\ncodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n\n## Use\n\n```sh\ncledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\ncledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\ncledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch\ncledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\ncledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\ncledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\ncledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\necho '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n```\n\n## Sharing (transport)\n\nThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n\n- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n  explicitly either way.\n\nIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\nare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\nthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\nand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n\n## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n\nEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\nmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\nbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\nthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n\n- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n  exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n  cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n  event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n  branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n  never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n  and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n- Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n  the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n  (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n  candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n  squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n  touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n  and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n  conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n  `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n  mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n  commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n  explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n  evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n  turns them off.\n- Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n  working either way.\n\n## Adapters\n\nSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n\n| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text; their encrypted blocks are never stored, same policy as reasoning. |\n\nTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\nstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\nsystems):\n\n- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n\n## Security & redaction\n\nVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n\n### Redaction layers\n\n**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n\n**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n- Cost: manual configuration required.\n\n**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n\n**Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n- Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n\n**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n\n### Commands\n\n`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n\n`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n\n### Maximum safety recipe\n\nKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n\n## Roadmap\n\n- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n  far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n  holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n  must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n  detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n  scope and capture triggers.\n- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n  intended narrow form.\n- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n  the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n  Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n  capture runs silently on every turn.\n- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n- **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n  discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n  commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n  with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n  the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n  remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n  lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n  extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n  piece.\n- **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n  that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n  event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n  conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n  \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n  reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n  forge credentials and per-host setup.\n\n## Storage model, in one paragraph\n\nOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\nnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\ncontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\nconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\nrepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\ncapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\nrewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\nfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\ntool deliberately refuses to become.\n\n## License\n\n[MIT](LICENSE)\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/README.md","numLines":277,"startLine":1,"totalLines":277},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"b9a7c444-7bcb-483c-af70-5d16016e13ed","version":"2.1.216"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T02:16:45.527Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
