d?” Every answer should expose the underlying evidence and\n8\tdistinguish original requests from later changes of mind.\n9\t\n10\tThis repository is intentionally at the design stage. It has no released API yet.\n11\t\n12\tSee [product intent](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) and the [WIP technical design](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md).\n13\t\n14\t## License\n15\t\n16\t[MIT](LICENSE)\n17\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01QMwxHmFjZfzEpKaNBUNLKA","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"880cbe89-48c3-4de2-aebc-cb178c7faf44","promptId":"07367244-5cb8-4fc2-b615-0918c8d4acee","sessionId":"c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1c3052566c2e","slug":"generic-stirring-candy","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"880cbe89-48c3-4de2-aebc-cb178c7faf44","timestamp":"2026-07-19T06:35:36.398Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"# Intent Recall\n\nEvidence-backed retrieval of historical repository intent.\n\nIntent Recall will help humans and agents inspect recorded conversations,\ndecisions, specifications, code, and tests when answering: “what behavior was\nactually intended?” Every answer should expose the underlying evidence and\ndistinguish original requests from later changes of mind.\n\nThis repository is intentionally at the design stage. It has no released API yet.\n\nSee [product intent](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) and the [WIP technical design](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md).\n\n## License\n\n[MIT](LICENSE)\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/intent-recall/README.md","numLines":17,"startLine":1,"totalLines":17},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"66c513b1-ed6e-4a0e-81e4-2a0f1bf3752a","version":"2.1.215"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T02:16:46.202Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Making Sticky Note work with Copilot CLI\n2\t\n3\t*We built Sticky Note for Claude Code first. Getting it to work in\n4\tCopilot CLI meant dealing with a bunch of runtime differences nobody\n5\twarns you about.*\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## Where we started\n10\t\n11\tSticky Note was born as a Claude Code project. Claude Code has a rich hook\n12\tsystem: `SessionStart`, `SessionEnd`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `PreToolUse`,\n13\t`PostToolUse`. You drop JavaScript files into `.claude/hooks/`, wire them\n14\tup in `settings.json`, and they fire automatically at the right moments.\n15\t\n16\tThe hooks read stdin for context (tool inputs, transcripts, session IDs),\n17\twrite JSON to stdout to inject context back into the conversation, and\n18\tthat's it.\n19\t\n20\tThen we wanted Sticky Note to work in Copilot CLI too.\n21\t\n22\t---\n23\t\n24\t## ⚠️ Copilot CLI hook limitations\n25\t\n26\tBefore getting into solutions, here are the specific limitations we hit.\n27\tCopilot CLI's hook system is newer than Claude Code's, and several gaps\n28\tforced us into workarounds:\n29\t\n30\t1. **Per-turn lifecycle, not per-session.** Copilot CLI fires\n31\t   `sessionStart` and `sessionEnd` on *every user prompt*, not once per\n32\t   conversation. There is no concept of a persistent session boundary.\n33\t   This means any state management that assumes \"start once, end once\"\n34\t   breaks immediately — threads get prematurely closed, dedup tracking\n35\t   gets wiped, and transient files get cleaned up mid-conversation.\n36\t\n37\t   *How Claude Code does it:* `SessionStart` fires once when you open a\n38\t   session. `SessionEnd` fires once when you close it (or it times out).\n39\t   A session can span dozens of prompts and hundreds of tool calls, and\n40\t   the hooks only fire at the bookends. This makes state management\n41\t   straightforward — initialize on start, finalize on end.\n42\t\n43\t2. **Different output protocol with no shared spec.** Copilot CLI\n44\t   expects `{ \"additionalContext\": \"...\" }` on stdout to inject context.\n45\t   There's no shared protocol or content negotiation — and critically,\n46\t   **sending the wrong key produces no error**. Your hook runs, writes\n47\t   to stdout, exits successfully, and the context silently vanishes.\n48\t   This is the most dangerous kind of incompatibility because nothing\n49\t   tells you it's broken.\n50\t\n51\t   *How Claude Code does it:* Hooks write `{ \"output\": \"...\" }` to\n52\t   stdout to inject context into the conversation. The key name is\n53\t   documented, and while the failure mode is similarly silent, Claude\n54\t   Code's hook ecosystem is more established so the protocol is less\n55\t   likely to surprise you.\n56\t\n57\t3. **No transcript access.** Copilot CLI does not provide hooks with\n58\t   access to the conversation history. Hooks that need to analyze what\n59\t   happened during a session (extracting narratives, detecting failed\n60\t   approaches, summarizing work) have to reconstruct context from the\n61\t   audit trail instead of reading it directly.\n62\t\n63\t   *How Claude Code does it:* The hook input includes a\n64\t   `transcript_path` pointing to a JSONL file containing the full\n65\t   conversation — every user prompt, assistant response, tool call, and\n66\t   tool result. Our `session-end.js` hook reads this transcript to\n67\t   extract narratives, detect error patterns, identify failed approaches,\n68\t   and build a rich thread summary. It's the single most useful piece of\n69\t   data for understanding what happened in a session.\n70\t\n71\t4. **No native session identity.** Copilot CLI's per-turn model means\n72\t   session IDs may or may not persist between turns depending on the\n73\t   implementation. We had to add our own session ID persistence layer\n74\t   (a `.sticky-session` file) to maintain continuity across turns in\n75\t   the same conversation.\n76\t\n77\t   *How Claude Code does it:* A stable `session_id` is provided in the\n78\t   hook input payload and remains consistent across all hook invocations\n79\t   within a session. Hooks can use it directly without any persistence\n80\t   layer — `SessionStart` gets the same ID as `SessionEnd`, and every\n81\t   `PreToolUse` and `PostToolUse` in between.\n82\t\n83\t5. **Hook config lives in a different location and format.** Copilot CLI\n84\t   uses `.github/hooks/hooks.json`. There's no hook config inheritance or\n85\t   shared format, so you maintain two config files pointing at the same\n86\t   scripts.\n87\t\n88\t   *How Claude Code does it:* Hook configuration lives in\n89\t   `.claude/settings.json` in the project root, alongside the hook\n90\t   scripts in `.claude/hooks/`. Config and scripts are co-located, and\n91\t   the settings file supports matchers (tool name, file pattern) to\n92\t   selectively fire hooks — a feature Copilot CLI's config doesn't\n93\t   currently offer.\n94\t\n95\t6. **Cross-platform command differences.** Copilot CLI runs on Windows\n96\t   natively (PowerShell), whereas Claude Code primarily targets\n97\t   Unix-like environments. Every hook command needs both a `bash` and\n98\t   `powershell` variant, including different syntax for setting\n99\t   environment variables (`VAR=1 cmd` vs `$env:VAR='1'; cmd`).\n100\t\n101\t   *How Claude Code does it:* Hooks are specified as a single `command`\n102\t   string that runs in the system shell. Since Claude Code runs\n103\t   primarily on macOS and Linux, you typically only need the bash\n104\t   variant. Windows support isn't a primary concern for Claude Code's\n105\t   hook system.\n106\t\n107\t7. **Instruction file location differs.** Copilot CLI reads\n108\t   `.github/copilot-instructions.md`. Since the instruction file is the\n109\t   fallback when hooks aren't running at all, we need to maintain both —\n110\t   and the Copilot CLI version includes extra self-serve guidance that\n111\t   assumes hooks may not be present.\n112\t\n113\t   *How Claude Code does it:* Instructions live in `CLAUDE.md` at the\n114\t   repo root. Claude Code reads this file automatically at session start,\n115\t   and because Claude Code's hooks are reliable and well-established,\n116\t   the instructions can focus on conventions and guidelines rather than\n117\t   including fallback \"do this manually if hooks aren't working\" sections.\n118\t\n119\t 8. **Hook output is invisible to users.** This is the one that bit us\n120\t    hardest. Both runtimes let hooks inject context into the AI's working\n121\t    memory via stdout (`additionalContext` or `output`). But that context\n122\t    goes to the *model*, not the *user*. The AI decides whether to surface\n123\t    it. For background context (thread data, attribution), that's fine —\n124\t    the AI uses it silently. For urgent warnings (someone else is editing\n125\t    your files), it's a problem. The AI routinely absorbs the warning and\n126\t    says nothing.\n127\t\n128\t    We tried stronger language in the injected text (\"TELL THE USER\n129\t    IMMEDIATELY\"), prescribed exact banner formats, and added instructions\n130\t    to `copilot-instructions.md`. None of it was reliable — the model\n131\t    sometimes complied, sometimes didn't.\n132\t\n133\t    *How Claude Code does it:* Claude Code doesn't have this problem in\n134\t    the same way because its model tends to surface injected context more\n135\t    reliably. We still hit absorption issues occasionally, but less often.\n136\t\n137\t    The solution we found — using `preToolUse` deny responses as a\n138\t    user-visible message channel — is covered in Challenge 8 below.\n139\t\n140\tThese aren't bugs. They're what happens when two tools get built\n141\tindependently with different assumptions. But they mean that\n142\t\"write a hook once, run it everywhere\" is aspirational for now. Every\n143\thook in Sticky Note has Copilot CLI-specific branches to work\n144\taround these differences.\n145\t\n146\t---\n147\t\n148\t## Challenge 1: different hook config, same scripts\n149\t\n150\tClaude Code reads hooks from `.claude/settings.json`. Copilot CLI reads\n151\tthem from `.github/hooks/hooks.json`. Two config files, two formats, but\n152\twe didn't want to maintain two sets of hook scripts.\n153\t\n154\t**The solution:** One set of hook scripts lives in `.claude/hooks/`. The\n155\tCopilot CLI config in `.github/hooks/hooks.json` points at the *same*\n156\tscripts, but passes a `--copilot-cli` flag and sets a `COPILOT_CLI=1`\n157\tenvironment variable:\n158\t\n159\t```json\n160\t{\n161\t  \"hooks\": {\n162\t    \"sessionStart\": [\n163\t      {\n164\t        \"type\": \"command\",\n165\t        \"bash\": \"COPILOT_CLI=1 node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\",\n166\t        \"powershell\": \"$env:COPILOT_CLI='1'; node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\"\n167\t      }\n168\t    ]\n169\t  }\n170\t}\n171\t```\n172\t\n173\tEvery hook script checks for this flag early and branches behavior where\n174\tneeded:\n175\t\n176\t```javascript\n177\tfunction _isCopilotCli() {\n178\t  return process.argv.includes(\"--copilot-cli\") || !!process.env.COPILOT_CLI;\n179\t}\n180\t```\n181\t\n182\tThis meant we could keep a single codebase for all hooks while handling\n183\tthe runtime differences with simple conditionals.\n184\t\n185\t---\n186\t\n187\t## Challenge 2: different event names\n188\t\n189\tClaude Code and Copilot CLI use different naming conventions for the same\n190\tlifecycle events:\n191\t\n192\t| Lifecycle Event          | Claude Code         | Copilot CLI            |\n193\t|--------------------------|---------------------|------------------------|\n194\t| Session starts           | `SessionStart`      | `sessionStart`         |\n195\t| Session ends             | `SessionEnd`        | `sessionEnd`           |\n196\t| User submits prompt      | `UserPromptSubmit`  | `userPromptSubmitted`  |\n197\t| Before tool executes     | `PreToolUse`        | `preToolUse`           |\n198\t| After tool executes      | `PostToolUse`       | `postToolUse`          |\n199\t| Error occurs             | `PostToolUseFailure`| `errorOccurred`        |\n200\t\n201\tClaude Code uses PascalCase. Copilot CLI uses camelCase. The hook input\n202\tpayload includes a `hook_event_name` field, so we use the casing to\n203\tauto-detect which runtime we're in:\n204\t\n205\t```javascript\n206\tfunction detectTool(hookInput) {\n207\t  if (process.argv.includes(\"--copilot-cli\")) return \"copilot-cli\";\n208\t  if (process.env.COPILOT_CLI) return \"copilot-cli\";\n209\t\n210\t  if (hookInput) {\n211\t    const event = hookInput.hook_event_name || \"\";\n212\t    // PascalCase = Claude Code, camelCase = Copilot CLI\n213\t    if (event && event[0] === event[0].toUpperCase()\n214\t             && event[0] !== event[0].toLowerCase()) return \"claude-code\";\n215\t    if (event && event[0] === event[0].toLowerCase()\n216\t             && event[0] !== event[0].toUpperCase()) return \"copilot-cli\";\n217\t    // Only Claude Code provides transcript access\n218\t    if (\"transcript_path\" in hookInput) return \"claude-code\";\n219\t  }\n220\t\n221\t  return \"unknown\";\n222\t}\n223\t```\n224\t\n225\tThis four-layer check (explicit flag → env var → event name casing →\n226\ttranscript presence) means the right runtime gets detected even if one\n227\tsignal is missing. The extra `!== toLowerCase()`/`!== toUpperCase()`\n228\tguards prevent false positives on digits or symbols in the event name.\n229\t\n230\t---\n231\t\n232\t## Challenge 3: different output protocols\n233\t\n234\tThis was the subtlest difference. Both runtimes read JSON from stdout to\n235\tinject context into the conversation, but they expect **different keys**:\n236\t\n237\t- **Claude Code** expects `{ \"output\": \"text to inject\" }`\n238\t- **Copilot CLI** expects `{ \"additionalContext\": \"text to inject\" }`\n239\t\n240\tIf you send `output` to Copilot CLI, it silently ignores it. If you send\n241\t`additionalContext` to Claude Code, same thing. No error, just silence —\n242\tyour context injection simply doesn't work.\n243\t\n244\tEvery hook that injects context uses a wrapper:\n245\t\n246\t```javascript\n247\tfunction _emit(text) {\n248\t  if (_isCopilotCli()) {\n249\t    process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify({ additionalContext: text }) + \"\\n\");\n250\t  } else {\n251\t    process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify({ output: text }) + \"\\n\");\n252\t  }\n253\t}\n254\t```\n255\t\n256\tThis was one of those bugs that took forever to find because there was\n257\tno error. Things just silently didn't work until we checked the protocol\n258\tdifference.\n259\t\n260\t---\n261\t\n262\t## Challenge 4: per-turn vs per-session lifecycle\n263\t\n264\tThis is the big one.\n265\t\n266\tIn **Claude Code**, `SessionStart` fires once when you open a session and\n267\t`SessionEnd` fires once when you close it. A session is a long-lived\n268\tconversation — you might have 50 tool calls across 20 prompts, and the\n269\thooks fire at the bookends.\n270\t\n271\tIn **Copilot CLI**, `sessionStart` and `sessionEnd` fire **on every\n272\tturn**. Each user prompt is its own mini-session. This difference\n273\tbreaks a bunch of assumptions:\n274\t\n275\t### Thread status: open vs closed\n276\t\n277\tIn Claude Code, `session-end.js` closes the thread because the session\n278\tis truly over. In Copilot CLI, closing the thread on every turn would\n279\tmean it flips between open and closed dozens of times in a conversation.\n280\t\n281\t**Fix:** Copilot CLI threads stay `\"open\"`:\n282\t\n283\t```javascript\n284\tstatus: isCopilotCli ? \"open\" : \"closed\",\n285\tclosed_at: isCopilotCli ? null : now,\n286\t```\n287\t\n288\t### Dedup state\n289\t\n290\t`session-start.js` clears the injection dedup set\n291\t(`.sticky-injected`) at the start of each session so threads can be\n292\tre-injected next time. But in Copilot CLI, \"session start\" fires every\n293\tturn, so clearing the dedup set would re-inject the same threads on\n294\tevery single prompt.\n295\t\n296\t**Fix:** Skip the clear for Copilot CLI:\n297\t\n298\t```javascript\n299\t// Copilot CLI fires SessionStart per-turn; clearing would lose dedup state.\n300\tif (!isCopilotCli) {\n301\t  clearInjectedSet();\n302\t  clearActiveResumeThreadId();\n303\t}\n304\t```\n305\t\n306\t### Transient file cleanup\n307\t\n308\tAt session end, Claude Code cleans up transient files (`.sticky-session`,\n309\t`.sticky-head`, `.sticky-injected`, presence data). In Copilot CLI, doing\n310\tthis after every turn would destroy state that the next turn needs.\n311\t\n312\t**Fix:** Skip cleanup for Copilot CLI:\n313\t\n314\t```javascript\n315\t// Copilot CLI fires per-turn; clearing would break state across turns.\n316\tif (!isCopilotCli) {\n317\t  clearPresence(user);\n318\t  clearSessionFile();\n319\t  clearHeadFile();\n320\t  clearInjectedSet();\n321\t}\n322\t```\n323\t\n324\t### Status messages\n325\t\n326\tEven the human-facing output adapts:\n327\t\n328\t```javascript\n329\tconst statusLabel = isCopilotCli ? \"updated\" : \"closed\";\n330\tconst statusMsg = `[STICKY-NOTE] Session ${statusLabel} - thread ${\n331\t  isCopilotCli ? \"updated\" : \"created\"\n332\t}`;\n333\t```\n334\t\n335\tA Claude Code user sees \"Session closed — thread created.\" A Copilot CLI\n336\tuser sees \"Session updated — thread updated.\" Same hook, different\n337\twording that matches the actual lifecycle.\n338\t\n339\t---\n340\t\n341\t## Challenge 5: cross-platform commands\n342\t\n343\tCopilot CLI runs on Windows, macOS, and Linux. Claude Code primarily\n344\ttargets macOS and Linux. The hooks need to work everywhere.\n345\t\n346\tThe `hooks.json` config includes **both bash and PowerShell** variants\n347\tfor every hook:\n348\t\n349\t```json\n350\t{\n351\t  \"bash\": \"COPILOT_CLI=1 node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\",\n352\t  \"powershell\": \"$env:COPILOT_CLI='1'; node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\"\n353\t}\n354\t```\n355\t\n356\tSetting environment variables works differently (`VAR=1 command` in bash\n357\tvs `$env:VAR='1'; command` in PowerShell), so both forms are provided.\n358\tThe runtime picks the right one for the OS.\n359\t\n360\tInside the hooks themselves, all path handling uses `path.join()` and a\n361\t`normalizeSep()` helper that converts backslashes to forward slashes for\n362\tconsistent storage:\n363\t\n364\t```javascript\n365\tfunction normalizeSep(p) {\n366\t  return typeof p === \"string\" ? p.replace(/\\\\/g, \"/\") : p;\n367\t}\n368\t```\n369\t\n370\tThis ensures that file paths recorded on Windows can be matched against\n371\tfile paths recorded on macOS, and vice versa.\n372\t\n373\t---\n374\t\n375\t## Challenge 6: the instruction file split\n376\t\n377\tClaude Code reads AI instructions from `CLAUDE.md` at the repo root.\n378\tCopilot CLI reads from `.github/copilot-instructions.md`. Same purpose,\n379\tdifferent locations, different audiences.\n380\t\n381\tWe maintain both, but with a difference: the Copilot CLI instructions\n382\tinclude a **self-serve fallback** that Claude Code doesn't need.\n383\t\n384\tSince Copilot CLI's hook support was initially limited (and some\n385\tdeployments may not run hooks at all), the instructions tell the AI to\n386\tself-serve context directly:\n387\t\n388\t```markdown\n389\t### Proactive context injection (V2.5)\n390\t\n391\tCopilot CLI does not have lifecycle hooks, so **you must self-serve context**.\n392\t\n393\t#### 1. At session start, check for stuck/open threads\n394\tBefore doing any work, read `.sticky-note/sticky-note.json`...\n395\t\n396\t#### 2. Before editing a file, check for prior thread context\n397\tThe first time you edit or read a file in a session, run:\n398\t  npx sticky-note get-line-attribution --file <path>\n399\t```\n400\t\n401\tThis means Sticky Note works in Copilot CLI even when hooks aren't\n402\tconfigured. The AI reads the instructions and calls the CLI commands\n403\titself. Hooks just make it automatic.\n404\t\n405\t---\n406\t\n407\t## Challenge 7: cross-tool thread continuity\n408\t\n409\tA thread started in Claude Code should be resumable in Copilot CLI, and\n410\tvice versa. This required:\n411\t\n412\t1. **Tool-agnostic thread format:** Every thread records a `tool` field\n413\t   (`\"claude-code\"` or `\"copilot-cli\"`), but the thread structure itself\n414\t   is identical. No tool-specific fields.\n415\t\n416\t2. **Session ID compatibility:** Both tools generate UUIDs for session\n417\t   IDs. The `session-start.js` hook normalizes this — if the runtime\n418\t   doesn't provide a session ID, the hook generates one:\n419\t\n420\t   ```javascript\n421\t   if (sessionId === \"unknown\") {\n422\t     sessionId = crypto.randomUUID();\n423\t   }\n424\t   saveSessionId(sessionId);\n425\t   ```\n426\t\n427\t3. **Prompts stored for cross-tool resume:** Each thread stores the\n428\t   user's prompts (up to 20, truncated to 300 chars). When resuming a\n429\t   thread in a different tool, the prompts provide enough context to\n430\t   understand what happened:\n431\t\n432\t   ```json\n433\t   {\n434\t     \"prompts\": [\n435\t       \"fix the auth token refresh race condition\",\n436\t       \"that didn't work, try using a mutex instead\",\n437\t       \"run the tests again\"\n438\t     ]\n439\t   }\n440\t   ```\n441\t\n442\t4. **Smart resume works across tools:** The `resume-thread` command\n443\t   searches by text similarity against narratives and prompts — it\n444\t   doesn't filter by tool. \"Pick up where the last session left off\"\n445\t   works whether that session was Claude Code or Copilot CLI.\n446\t\n447\t---\n448\t\n449\t## Challenge 8: getting messages to the user\n450\t\n451\tThis one took us twelve releases to solve.\n452\t\n453\t### The problem\n454\t\n455\tOverlap detection landed in v2.6.0: when you start working and someone\n456\telse has an open thread touching the same files, you should know about\n457\tit. The detection logic was straightforward. Getting the warning in\n458\tfront of the user was not.\n459\t\n460\tHook output (`additionalContext` in Copilot CLI) goes to the AI model's\n461\tcontext window. The model *sees* it, but it doesn't necessarily *show*\n462\tit. For thread context and attribution data, that's the right behavior —\n463\tthe AI should absorb it silently and use it when relevant. But for\n464\t\"hey, Alice is actively editing the same files you're about to touch,\"\n465\tsilent absorption defeats the purpose.\n466\t\n467\t### What we tried (and what failed)\n468\t\n469\t**Attempt 1: sessionStart output.** Put the overlap warning in\n470\t`session-start.js` output. Copilot CLI silently dropped it. Turns out\n471\t`sessionStart` hook output isn't reliably surfaced at all.\n472\t\n473\t**Attempt 2: userPromptSubmitted output.** Moved the warning into\n474\t`inject-context.js` so it arrives with every prompt. The AI received it\n475\tbut treated it as background context. No mention to the user.\n476\t\n477\t**Attempt 3: stronger language.** Added directives like \"TELL THE USER\n478\tIMMEDIATELY\" and \"You MUST surface this warning\" to the injected text.\n479\tSometimes the AI complied. Sometimes it didn't. Not reliable enough for\n480\ta safety-critical warning.\n481\t\n482\t**Attempt 4: exact banner format.** Prescribed the exact text the AI\n483\tshould display, down to the emoji and formatting. Added matching rules\n484\tto both `CLAUDE.md` and `copilot-instructions.md`. Better compliance\n485\trate, but still not guaranteed.\n486\t\n487\t**Attempt 5: stderr.** Wrote a concise banner directly to `stderr` so\n488\tit appears in the user's terminal regardless of what the AI does:\n489\t\n490\t```javascript\n491\tprocess.stderr.write(\"\\n⚠️  OVERLAP DETECTED — someone else is touching your files:\\n\");\n492\tprocess.stderr.write(`   [STUCK] alice: src/auth.ts — token refresh race condition\\n`);\n493\t```\n494\t\n495\tThis works for humans reading the terminal, but AI assistants don't\n496\trelay stderr content. So the user sees a raw banner scrolling past in\n497\tthe hook output, potentially before the AI has even started responding.\n498\tBetter than nothing, but not great.\n499\t\n500\t### What actually works: preToolUse deny\n501\t\n502\tThe breakthrough was realizing that Copilot CLI's `preToolUse` hook can\n503\treturn a **deny response**:\n504\t\n505\t```javascript\n506\treturn {\n507\t  permissionDecision: \"deny\",\n508\t  permissionDecisionReason: \"⚠️ Overlap detected: alice is working on src/auth.ts...\"\n509\t};\n510\t```\n511\t\n512\tWhen a tool call is denied, the AI *has* to tell the user about it —\n513\tit needs to explain why the action failed. This is the one output\n514\tchannel where the model can't silently absorb the message, because the\n515\ttool call didn't succeed and the user needs to know why.\n516\t\n517\tThe pattern: on the first tool call of a session, if file overlaps\n518\texist, deny it with the warning as the reason. Copilot CLI auto-retries\n519\tthe tool call. On retry, the overlap check passes (already warned), and\n520\tthe tool proceeds normally. The user sees the warning, the work\n521\tcontinues, and the deny is invisible except for a brief pause.\n522\t\n523\t```javascript\n524\tfunction _checkOverlapsAndDeny() {\n525\t  if (!_isCopilotCli()) return null;\n526\t  if (isOverlapWarned()) return null;\n527\t\n528\t  // ... detect file overlaps with other users' open threads ...\n529\t\n530\t  if (warnings.length === 0) return null;\n531\t\n532\t  markOverlapWarned();\n533\t\n534\t  return {\n535\t    permissionDecision: \"deny\",\n536\t    permissionDecisionReason: formatOverlapWarning(warnings),\n537\t  };\n538\t}\n539\t```\n540\t\n541\t### The dedup problem\n542\t\n543\tOne deny per session. Sounds simple. It wasn't.\n544\t\n545\tCopilot CLI sessions share the filesystem. The `.sticky-session` file\n546\tthat tracks session identity gets reused across concurrent sessions on\n547\tthe same machine. So \"already warned for this session\" leaked between\n548\tsessions: warn Session A, Session B thinks it was already warned, skips\n549\tthe warning entirely.\n550\t\n551\tWe tried several approaches:\n552\t\n553\t**TTL-based dedup (v2.6.9):** If the warning was shown more than 60\n554\tseconds ago, reset and re-warn. The idea was that a retry comes within\n555\tseconds (so it passes), but a new session would be more than 60 seconds\n556\tlater. Broke because long-running sessions would get re-denied every 60\n557\tseconds.\n558\t\n559\t**Signal file (v2.6.10):** `inject-context.js` writes an\n560\t`.overlap-pending` file when overlaps are detected. `pre-tool-use.js`\n561\treads and deletes it atomically on the first tool call. No shared state,\n562\tno TTL. Broke because the signal file was shared across concurrent\n563\tsessions — Session A's pending file got consumed by Session B.\n564\t\n565\t**PID-keyed dedup (v2.6.11):** The fix that stuck. Copilot CLI sets a\n566\t`COPILOT_LOADER_PID` environment variable that's unique per session and\n567\tinherited by hook child processes. The warned state is stored as a JSON\n568\tobject keyed by PID:\n569\t\n570\t```javascript\n571\t// .sticky-note/.overlap-warned\n572\t{\n573\t  \"12345\": { \"warned_at\": \"2026-03-14T...\" },\n574\t  \"67890\": { \"warned_at\": \"2026-03-14T...\" }\n575\t}\n576\t```\n577\t\n578\tEach session gets its own deny state. A 1-hour TTL cleans up stale\n579\tentries from dead sessions. No cross-session interference.\n580\t\n581\t### Auto-closing orphaned threads\n582\t\n583\tThe per-turn lifecycle creates another problem: Copilot CLI threads\n584\tnever close. There's no reliable session-end signal, so threads stay\n585\t`\"open\"` indefinitely. This pollutes overlap detection — a thread from\n586\tyesterday looks active today.\n587\t\n588\tThe fix: `session-start.js` now runs `autoCloseCopilotCliThreads()`,\n589\twhich closes any `copilot-cli` thread that's been inactive for longer\n590\tthan a configurable threshold (default 24 hours):\n591\t\n592\t```javascript\n593\tif (thread.tool === \"copilot-cli\" && thread.status === \"open\") {\n594\t  const hoursInactive = (now - lastActivity) / (1000 * 60 * 60);\n595\t  if (hoursInactive >= autoCloseHours) {\n596\t    thread.status = \"closed\";\n597\t  }\n598\t}\n599\t```\n600\t\n601\tConfigurable via `copilot_cli_auto_close_hours` in\n602\t`sticky-note-config.json`.\n603\t\n604\t### The final architecture\n605\t\n606\tOverlap warnings now use a three-channel approach:\n607\t\n608\t1. **`additionalContext`** (inject-context.js) — full formatted warning\n609\t   with CRITICAL INSTRUCTION directive. The AI *might* surface it.\n610\t2. **stderr** (inject-context.js) — concise terminal banner. The user\n611\t   *will* see it scroll past in hook output.\n612\t3. **preToolUse deny** (pre-tool-use.js) — blocks the first tool call\n613\t   with the warning as the deny reason. The AI *must* report it.\n614\t\n615\tChannels 1 and 2 fire on every prompt where overlaps exist. Channel 3\n616\tfires once per session (PID-keyed). Together they guarantee the user\n617\tsees the warning through at least one path.\n618\t\n619\t---\n620\t\n621\t## The result\n622\t\n623\tThe full hook mapping, both tools running the same scripts:\n624\t\n625\t| Hook Script          | Claude Code Event     | Copilot CLI Event     |\n626\t|----------------------|-----------------------|-----------------------|\n627\t| `session-start.js`   | SessionStart          | sessionStart          |\n628\t| `session-end.js`     | SessionEnd            | sessionEnd            |\n629\t| `inject-context.js`  | UserPromptSubmit      | userPromptSubmitted   |\n630\t| `pre-tool-use.js`    | PreToolUse            | preToolUse            |\n631\t| `track-work.js`      | PostToolUse           | postToolUse           |\n632\t| `on-error.js`        | PostToolUseFailure    | errorOccurred         |\n633\t\n634\tSix hooks, one codebase, two runtimes. The `--copilot-cli` flag and\n635\t`COPILOT_CLI` env var flow through every hook, triggering the behavioral\n636\tdifferences where the two runtimes diverge:\n637\t\n638\t- Output protocol (`output` vs `additionalContext`)\n639\t- Thread lifecycle (`closed` vs `open`, with auto-close for stale threads)\n640\t- State cleanup (full cleanup vs preserve across turns)\n641\t- Status messaging (\"closed\" vs \"updated\")\n642\t- Dedup handling (clear per session vs PID-keyed persistence)\n643\t- User-facing warnings (injected context vs preToolUse deny + stderr)\n644\t- Thread garbage collection (manual gc vs auto-close after 24h inactivity)\n645\t\n646\tEverything else — the attribution engine, git blame pipeline, Git Notes,\n647\tthread scoring, resume search — works identically in both tools.\n648\t\n649\t---\n650\t\n651\t## What we'd do differently\n652\t\n653\tThe biggest lesson: **hook output is not user output.** We spent twelve\n654\treleases learning that injected context goes to the model, not the\n655\tuser, and the model decides what to surface. If you need guaranteed\n656\tuser visibility from a hook, the only reliable mechanism in Copilot CLI\n657\tis a `preToolUse` deny. We'd design around that constraint from day one\n658\tinstead of discovering it through trial and error.\n659\t\n660\tThe silent protocol mismatch (`output` vs `additionalContext`) wasted\n661\tmore debugging time than any other single issue. No errors, no warnings,\n662\tcontext just vanished. We log every emit call during development now.\n663\t\n664\tAssuming \"session\" means the same thing everywhere was wrong. Copilot\n665\tCLI's per-turn model means session IDs, dedup state, and cleanup logic\n666\tall need different semantics. If we were starting over, we'd design for\n667\tper-turn first and treat per-session as the special case.\n668\t\n669\tConcurrent sessions sharing filesystem state was a recurring headache.\n670\tThe `COPILOT_LOADER_PID` discovery solved the dedup problem, but we\n671\tcould have avoided three intermediate releases if we'd looked for a\n672\tper-session environment variable sooner.\n673\t\n674\tThe instruction file fallback turned out better than expected. Even when\n675\thooks aren't configured at all, the AI reads\n676\t`.github/copilot-instructions.md` and self-serves the same\n677\tfunctionality by calling CLI commands directly. We almost didn't build\n678\tthat, and it's saved us more than once.\n679\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01D475eJNgTQdgVyWqSroyRi","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","dirty_fingerprint":"bc65e878d80c1722a0adee9428cbf850f1d73e21ecfd82d0c4182876ebf93c10","head":"173cfc66bb28f4c20e474f522f197eeef0c627de","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1c3052566c2e#agent:a264be797d1ae6615","parent":"claude-code:c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1c3052566c2e","seq":59},"id":"ev1-fa421e6e32173c13a42e79e1b92dde9eefe08bcdb319e1a6d651bc5dc33a8e1b","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-19T06:36:15.102Z","producer":{"session_id":"c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1c3052566c2e","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.215","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"a264be797d1ae6615","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":true,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# Making Sticky Note work with Copilot CLI\n2\t\n3\t*We built Sticky Note for Claude Code first. Getting it to work in\n4\tCopilot CLI meant dealing with a bunch of runtime differences nobody\n5\twarns you about.*\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## Where we started\n10\t\n11\tSticky Note was born as a Claude Code project. Claude Code has a rich hook\n12\tsystem: `SessionStart`, `SessionEnd`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `PreToolUse`,\n13\t`PostToolUse`. You drop JavaScript files into `.claude/hooks/`, wire them\n14\tup in `settings.json`, and they fire automatically at the right moments.\n15\t\n16\tThe hooks read stdin for context (tool inputs, transcripts, session IDs),\n17\twrite JSON to stdout to inject context back into the conversation, and\n18\tthat's it.\n19\t\n20\tThen we wanted Sticky Note to work in Copilot CLI too.\n21\t\n22\t---\n23\t\n24\t## ⚠️ Copilot CLI hook limitations\n25\t\n26\tBefore getting into solutions, here are the specific limitations we hit.\n27\tCopilot CLI's hook system is newer than Claude Code's, and several gaps\n28\tforced us into workarounds:\n29\t\n30\t1. **Per-turn lifecycle, not per-session.** Copilot CLI fires\n31\t   `sessionStart` and `sessionEnd` on *every user prompt*, not once per\n32\t   conversation. There is no concept of a persistent session boundary.\n33\t   This means any state management that assumes \"start once, end once\"\n34\t   breaks immediately — threads get prematurely closed, dedup tracking\n35\t   gets wiped, and transient files get cleaned up mid-conversation.\n36\t\n37\t   *How Claude Code does it:* `SessionStart` fires once when you open a\n38\t   session. `SessionEnd` fires once when you close it (or it times out).\n39\t   A session can span dozens of prompts and hundreds of tool calls, and\n40\t   the hooks only fire at the bookends. This makes state management\n41\t   straightforward — initialize on start, finalize on end.\n42\t\n43\t2. **Different output protocol with no shared spec.** Copilot CLI\n44\t   expects `{ \"additionalContext\": \"...\" }` on stdout to inject context.\n45\t   There's no shared protocol or content negotiation — and critically,\n46\t   **sending the wrong key produces no error**. Your hook runs, writes\n47\t   to stdout, exits successfully, and the context silently vanishes.\n48\t   This is the most dangerous kind of incompatibility because nothing\n49\t   tells you it's broken.\n50\t\n51\t   *How Claude Code does it:* Hooks write `{ \"output\": \"...\" }` to\n52\t   stdout to inject context into the conversation. The key name is\n53\t   documented, and while the failure mode is similarly silent, Claude\n54\t   Code's hook ecosystem is more established so the protocol is less\n55\t   likely to surprise you.\n56\t\n57\t3. **No transcript access.** Copilot CLI does not provide hooks with\n58\t   access to the conversation history. Hooks that need to analyze what\n59\t   happened during a session (extracting narratives, detecting failed\n60\t   approaches, summarizing work) have to reconstruct context from the\n61\t   audit trail instead of reading it directly.\n62\t\n63\t   *How Claude Code does it:* The hook input includes a\n64\t   `transcript_path` pointing to a JSONL file containing the full\n65\t   conversation — every user prompt, assistant response, tool call, and\n66\t   tool result. Our `session-end.js` hook reads this transcript to\n67\t   extract narratives, detect error patterns, identify failed approaches,\n68\t   and build a rich thread summary. It's the single most useful piece of\n69\t   data for understanding what happened in a session.\n70\t\n71\t4. **No native session identity.** Copilot CLI's per-turn model means\n72\t   session IDs may or may not persist between turns depending on the\n73\t   implementation. We had to add our own session ID persistence layer\n74\t   (a `.sticky-session` file) to maintain continuity across turns in\n75\t   the same conversation.\n76\t\n77\t   *How Claude Code does it:* A stable `session_id` is provided in the\n78\t   hook input payload and remains consistent across all hook invocations\n79\t   within a session. Hooks can use it directly without any persistence\n80\t   layer — `SessionStart` gets the same ID as `SessionEnd`, and every\n81\t   `PreToolUse` and `PostToolUse` in between.\n82\t\n83\t5. **Hook config lives in a different location and format.** Copilot CLI\n84\t   uses `.github/hooks/hooks.json`. There's no hook config inheritance or\n85\t   shared format, so you maintain two config files pointing at the same\n86\t   scripts.\n87\t\n88\t   *How Claude Code does it:* Hook configuration lives in\n89\t   `.claude/settings.json` in the project root, alongside the hook\n90\t   scripts in `.claude/hooks/`. Config and scripts are co-located, and\n91\t   the settings file supports matchers (tool name, file pattern) to\n92\t   selectively fire hooks — a feature Copilot CLI's config doesn't\n93\t   currently offer.\n94\t\n95\t6. **Cross-platform command differences.** Copilot CLI runs on Windows\n96\t   natively (PowerShell), whereas Claude Code primarily targets\n97\t   Unix-like environments. Every hook command needs both a `bash` and\n98\t   `powershell` variant, including different syntax for setting\n99\t   environment variables (`VAR=1 cmd` vs `$env:VAR='1'; cmd`).\n100\t\n101\t   *How Claude Code does it:* Hooks are specified as a single `command`\n102\t   string that runs in the system shell. Since Claude Code runs\n103\t   primarily on macOS and Linux, you typically only need the bash\n104\t   variant. Windows support isn't a primary concern for Claude Code's\n105\t   hook system.\n106\t\n107\t7. **Instruction file location differs.** Copilot CLI reads\n108\t   `.github/copilot-instructions.md`. Since the instruction file is the\n109\t   fallback when hooks aren't running at all, we need to maintain both —\n110\t   and the Copilot CLI version includes extra self-serve guidance that\n111\t   assumes hooks may not be present.\n112\t\n113\t   *How Claude Code does it:* Instructions live in `CLAUDE.md` at the\n114\t   repo root. Claude Code reads this file automatically at session start,\n115\t   and because Claude Code's hooks are reliable and well-established,\n116\t   the instructions can focus on conventions and guidelines rather than\n117\t   including fallback \"do this manually if hooks aren't working\" sections.\n118\t\n119\t 8. **Hook output is invisible to users.** This is the one that bit us\n120\t    hardest. Both runtimes let hooks inject context into the AI's working\n121\t    memory via stdout (`additionalContext` or `output`). But that context\n122\t    goes to the *model*, not the *user*. The AI decides whether to surface\n123\t    it. For background context (thread data, attribution), that's fine —\n124\t    the AI uses it silently. For urgent warnings (someone else is editing\n125\t    your files), it's a problem. The AI routinely absorbs the warning and\n126\t    says nothing.\n127\t\n128\t    We tried stronger language in the injected text (\"TELL THE USER\n129\t    IMMEDIATELY\"), prescribed exact banner formats, and added instructions\n130\t    to `copilot-instructions.md`. None of it was reliable — the model\n131\t    sometimes complied, sometimes didn't.\n132\t\n133\t    *How Claude Code does it:* Claude Code doesn't have this problem in\n134\t    the same way because its model tends to surface injected context more\n135\t    reliably. We still hit absorption issues occasionally, but less often.\n136\t\n137\t    The solution we found — using `preToolUse` deny responses as a\n138\t    user-visible message channel — is covered in Challenge 8 below.\n139\t\n140\tThese aren't bugs. They're what happens when two tools get built\n141\tindependently with different assumptions. But they mean that\n142\t\"write a hook once, run it everywhere\" is aspirational for now. Every\n143\thook in Sticky Note has Copilot CLI-specific branches to work\n144\taround these differences.\n145\t\n146\t---\n147\t\n148\t## Challenge 1: different hook config, same scripts\n149\t\n150\tClaude Code reads hooks from `.claude/settings.json`. Copilot CLI reads\n151\tthem from `.github/hooks/hooks.json`. Two config files, two formats, but\n152\twe didn't want to maintain two sets of hook scripts.\n153\t\n154\t**The solution:** One set of hook scripts lives in `.claude/hooks/`. The\n155\tCopilot CLI config in `.github/hooks/hooks.json` points at the *same*\n156\tscripts, but passes a `--copilot-cli` flag and sets a `COPILOT_CLI=1`\n157\tenvironment variable:\n158\t\n159\t```json\n160\t{\n161\t  \"hooks\": {\n162\t    \"sessionStart\": [\n163\t      {\n164\t        \"type\": \"command\",\n165\t        \"bash\": \"COPILOT_CLI=1 node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\",\n166\t        \"powershell\": \"$env:COPILOT_CLI='1'; node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\"\n167\t      }\n168\t    ]\n169\t  }\n170\t}\n171\t```\n172\t\n173\tEvery hook script checks for this flag early and branches behavior where\n174\tneeded:\n175\t\n176\t```javascript\n177\tfunction _isCopilotCli() {\n178\t  return process.argv.includes(\"--copilot-cli\") || !!process.env.COPILOT_CLI;\n179\t}\n180\t```\n181\t\n182\tThis meant we could keep a single codebase for all hooks while handling\n183\tthe runtime differences with simple conditionals.\n184\t\n185\t---\n186\t\n187\t## Challenge 2: different event names\n188\t\n189\tClaude Code and Copilot CLI use different naming conventions for the same\n190\tlifecycle events:\n191\t\n192\t| Lifecycle Event          | Claude Code         | Copilot CLI            |\n193\t|--------------------------|---------------------|------------------------|\n194\t| Session starts           | `SessionStart`      | `sessionStart`         |\n195\t| Session ends             | `SessionEnd`        | `sessionEnd`           |\n196\t| User submits prompt      | `UserPromptSubmit`  | `userPromptSubmitted`  |\n197\t| Before tool executes     | `PreToolUse`        | `preToolUse`           |\n198\t| After tool executes      | `PostToolUse`       | `postToolUse`          |\n199\t| Error occurs             | `PostToolUseFailure`| `errorOccurred`        |\n200\t\n201\tClaude Code uses PascalCase. Copilot CLI uses camelCase. The hook input\n202\tpayload includes a `hook_event_name` field, so we use the casing to\n203\tauto-detect which runtime we're in:\n204\t\n205\t```javascript\n206\tfunction detectTool(hookInput) {\n207\t  if (process.argv.includes(\"--copilot-cli\")) return \"copilot-cli\";\n208\t  if (process.env.COPILOT_CLI) return \"copilot-cli\";\n209\t\n210\t  if (hookInput) {\n211\t    const event = hookInput.hook_event_name || \"\";\n212\t    // PascalCase = Claude Code, camelCase = Copilot CLI\n213\t    if (event && event[0] === event[0].toUpperCase()\n214\t             && event[0] !== event[0].toLowerCase()) return \"claude-code\";\n215\t    if (event && event[0] === event[0].toLowerCase()\n216\t             && event[0] !== event[0].toUpperCase()) return \"copilot-cli\";\n217\t    // Only Claude Code provides transcript access\n218\t    if (\"transcript_path\" in hookInput) return \"claude-code\";\n219\t  }\n220\t\n221\t  return \"unknown\";\n222\t}\n223\t```\n224\t\n225\tThis four-layer check (explicit flag → env var → event name casing →\n226\ttranscript presence) means the right runtime gets detected even if one\n227\tsignal is missing. The extra `!== toLowerCase()`/`!== toUpperCase()`\n228\tguards prevent false positives on digits or symbols in the event name.\n229\t\n230\t---\n231\t\n232\t## Challenge 3: different output protocols\n233\t\n234\tThis was the subtlest difference. Both runtimes read JSON from stdout to\n235\tinject context into the conversation, but they expect **different keys**:\n236\t\n237\t- **Claude Code** expects `{ \"output\": \"text to inject\" }`\n238\t- **Copilot CLI** expects `{ \"additionalContext\": \"text to inject\" }`\n239\t\n240\tIf you send `output` to Copilot CLI, it silently ignores it. If you send\n241\t`additionalContext` to Claude Code, same thing. No error, just silence —\n242\tyour context injection simply doesn't work.\n243\t\n244\tEvery hook that injects context uses a wrapper:\n245\t\n246\t```javascript\n247\tfunction _emit(text) {\n248\t  if (_isCopilotCli()) {\n249\t    process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify({ additionalContext: text }) + \"\\n\");\n250\t  } else {\n251\t    process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify({ output: text }) + \"\\n\");\n252\t  }\n253\t}\n254\t```\n255\t\n256\tThis was one of those bugs that took forever to find because there was\n257\tno error. Things just silently didn't work until we checked the protocol\n258\tdifference.\n259\t\n260\t---\n261\t\n262\t## Challenge 4: per-turn vs per-session lifecycle\n263\t\n264\tThis is the big one.\n265\t\n266\tIn **Claude Code**, `SessionStart` fires once when you open a session and\n267\t`SessionEnd` fires once when you close it. A session is a long-lived\n268\tconversation — you might have 50 tool calls across 20 prompts, and the\n269\thooks fire at the bookends.\n270\t\n271\tIn **Copilot CLI**, `sessionStart` and `sessionEnd` fire **on every\n272\tturn**. Each user prompt is its own mini-session. This difference\n273\tbreaks a bunch of assumptions:\n274\t\n275\t### Thread status: open vs closed\n276\t\n277\tIn Claude Code, `session-end.js` closes the thread because the session\n278\tis truly over. In Copilot CLI, closing the thread on every turn would\n279\tmean it flips between open and closed dozens of times in a conversation.\n280\t\n281\t**Fix:** Copilot CLI threads stay `\"open\"`:\n282\t\n283\t```javascript\n284\tstatus: isCopilotCli ? \"open\" : \"closed\",\n285\tclosed_at: isCopilotCli ? null : now,\n286\t```\n287\t\n288\t### Dedup state\n289\t\n290\t`session-start.js` clears the injection dedup set\n291\t(`.sticky-injected`) at the start of each session so threads can be\n292\tre-injected next time. But in Copilot CLI, \"session start\" fires every\n293\tturn, so clearing the dedup set would re-inject the same threads on\n294\tevery single prompt.\n295\t\n296\t**Fix:** Skip the clear for Copilot CLI:\n297\t\n298\t```javascript\n299\t// Copilot CLI fires SessionStart per-turn; clearing would lose dedup state.\n300\tif (!isCopilotCli) {\n301\t  clearInjectedSet();\n302\t  clearActiveResumeThreadId();\n303\t}\n304\t```\n305\t\n306\t### Transient file cleanup\n307\t\n308\tAt session end, Claude Code cleans up transient files (`.sticky-session`,\n309\t`.sticky-head`, `.sticky-injected`, presence data). In Copilot CLI, doing\n310\tthis after every turn would destroy state that the next turn needs.\n311\t\n312\t**Fix:** Skip cleanup for Copilot CLI:\n313\t\n314\t```javascript\n315\t// Copilot CLI fires per-turn; clearing would break state across turns.\n316\tif (!isCopilotCli) {\n317\t  clearPresence(user);\n318\t  clearSessionFile();\n319\t  clearHeadFile();\n320\t  clearInjectedSet();\n321\t}\n322\t```\n323\t\n324\t### Status messages\n325\t\n326\tEven the human-facing output adapts:\n327\t\n328\t```javascript\n329\tconst statusLabel = isCopilotCli ? \"updated\" : \"closed\";\n330\tconst statusMsg = `[STICKY-NOTE] Session ${statusLabel} - thread ${\n331\t  isCopilotCli ? \"updated\" : \"created\"\n332\t}`;\n333\t```\n334\t\n335\tA Claude Code user sees \"Session closed — thread created.\" A Copilot CLI\n336\tuser sees \"Session updated — thread updated.\" Same hook, different\n337\twording that matches the actual lifecycle.\n338\t\n339\t---\n340\t\n341\t## Challenge 5: cross-platform commands\n342\t\n343\tCopilot CLI runs on Windows, macOS, and Linux. Claude Code primarily\n344\ttargets macOS and Linux. The hooks need to work everywhere.\n345\t\n346\tThe `hooks.json` config includes **both bash and PowerShell** variants\n347\tfor every hook:\n348\t\n349\t```json\n350\t{\n351\t  \"bash\": \"COPILOT_CLI=1 node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\",\n352\t  \"powershell\": \"$env:COPILOT_CLI='1'; node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\"\n353\t}\n354\t```\n355\t\n356\tSetting environment variables works differently (`VAR=1 command` in bash\n357\tvs `$env:VAR='1'; command` in PowerShell), so both forms are provided.\n358\tThe runtime picks the right one for the OS.\n359\t\n360\tInside the hooks themselves, all path handling uses `path.join()` and a\n361\t`normalizeSep()` helper that converts backslashes to forward slashes for\n362\tconsistent storage:\n363\t\n364\t```javascript\n365\tfunction normalizeSep(p) {\n366\t  return typeof p === \"string\" ? p.replace(/\\\\/g, \"/\") : p;\n367\t}\n368\t```\n369\t\n370\tThis ensures that file paths recorded on Windows can be matched against\n371\tfile paths recorded on macOS, and vice versa.\n372\t\n373\t---\n374\t\n375\t## Challenge 6: the instruction file split\n376\t\n377\tClaude Code reads AI instructions from `CLAUDE.md` at the repo root.\n378\tCopilot CLI reads from `.github/copilot-instructions.md`. Same purpose,\n379\tdifferent locations, different audiences.\n380\t\n381\tWe maintain both, but with a difference: the Copilot CLI instructions\n382\tinclude a **self-serve fallback** that Claude Code doesn't need.\n383\t\n384\tSince Copilot CLI's hook support was initially limited (and some\n385\tdeployments may not run hooks at all), the instructions tell the AI to\n386\tself-serve context directly:\n387\t\n388\t```markdown\n389\t### Proactive context injection (V2.5)\n390\t\n391\tCopilot CLI does not have lifecycle hooks, so **you must self-serve context**.\n392\t\n393\t#### 1. At session start, check for stuck/open threads\n394\tBefore doing any work, read `.sticky-note/sticky-note.json`...\n395\t\n396\t#### 2. Before editing a file, check for prior thread context\n397\tThe first time you edit or read a file in a session, run:\n398\t  npx sticky-note get-line-attribution --file <path>\n399\t```\n400\t\n401\tThis means Sticky Note works in Copilot CLI even when hooks aren't\n402\tconfigured. The AI reads the instructions and calls the CLI commands\n403\titself. Hooks just make it automatic.\n404\t\n405\t---\n406\t\n407\t## Challenge 7: cross-tool thread continuity\n408\t\n409\tA thread started in Claude Code should be resumable in Copilot CLI, and\n410\tvice versa. This required:\n411\t\n412\t1. **Tool-agnostic thread format:** Every thread records a `tool` field\n413\t   (`\"claude-code\"` or `\"copilot-cli\"`), but the thread structure itself\n414\t   is identical. No tool-specific fields.\n415\t\n416\t2. **Session ID compatibility:** Both tools generate UUIDs for session\n417\t   IDs. The `session-start.js` hook normalizes this — if the runtime\n418\t   doesn't provide a session ID, the hook generates one:\n419\t\n420\t   ```javascript\n421\t   if (sessionId === \"unknown\") {\n422\t     sessionId = crypto.randomUUID();\n423\t   }\n424\t   saveSessionId(sessionId);\n425\t   ```\n426\t\n427\t3. **Prompts stored for cross-tool resume:** Each thread stores the\n428\t   user's prompts (up to 20, truncated to 300 chars). When resuming a\n429\t   thread in a different tool, the prompts provide enough context to\n430\t   understand what happened:\n431\t\n432\t   ```json\n433\t   {\n434\t     \"prompts\": [\n435\t       \"fix the auth token refresh race condition\",\n436\t       \"that didn't work, try using a mutex instead\",\n437\t       \"run the tests again\"\n438\t     ]\n439\t   }\n440\t   ```\n441\t\n442\t4. **Smart resume works across tools:** The `resume-thread` command\n443\t   searches by text similarity against narratives and prompts — it\n444\t   doesn't filter by tool. \"Pick up where the last session left off\"\n445\t   works whether that session was Claude Code or Copilot CLI.\n446\t\n447\t---\n448\t\n449\t## Challenge 8: getting messages to the user\n450\t\n451\tThis one took us twelve releases to solve.\n452\t\n453\t### The problem\n454\t\n455\tOverlap detection landed in v2.6.0: when you start working and someone\n456\telse has an open thread touching the same files, you should know about\n457\tit. The detection logic was straightforward. Getting the warning in\n458\tfront of the user was not.\n459\t\n460\tHook output (`additionalContext` in Copilot CLI) goes to the AI model's\n461\tcontext window. The model *sees* it, but it doesn't necessarily *show*\n462\tit. For thread context and attribution data, that's the right behavior —\n463\tthe AI should absorb it silently and use it when relevant. But for\n464\t\"hey, Alice is actively editing the same files you're about to touch,\"\n465\tsilent absorption defeats the purpose.\n466\t\n467\t### What we tried (and what failed)\n468\t\n469\t**Attempt 1: sessionStart output.** Put the overlap warning in\n470\t`session-start.js` output. Copilot CLI silently dropped it. Turns out\n471\t`sessionStart` hook output isn't reliably surfaced at all.\n472\t\n473\t**Attempt 2: userPromptSubmitted output.** Moved the warning into\n474\t`inject-context.js` so it arrives with every prompt. The AI received it\n475\tbut treated it as background context. No mention to the user.\n476\t\n477\t**Attempt 3: stronger language.** Added directives like \"TELL THE USER\n478\tIMMEDIATELY\" and \"You MUST surface this warning\" to the injected text.\n479\tSometimes the AI complied. Sometimes it didn't. Not reliable enough for\n480\ta safety-critical warning.\n481\t\n482\t**Attempt 4: exact banner format.** Prescribed the exact text the AI\n483\tshould display, down to the emoji and formatting. Added matching rules\n484\tto both `CLAUDE.md` and `copilot-instructions.md`. Better compliance\n485\trate, but still not guaranteed.\n486\t\n487\t**Attempt 5: stderr.** Wrote a concise banner directly to `stderr` so\n488\tit appears in the user's terminal regardless of what the AI does:\n489\t\n490\t```javascript\n491\tprocess.stderr.write(\"\\n⚠️  OVERLAP DETECTED — someone else is touching your files:\\n\");\n492\tprocess.stderr.write(`   [STUCK] alice: src/auth.ts — token refresh race condition\\n`);\n493\t```\n494\t\n495\tThis works for humans reading the terminal, but AI assistants don't\n496\trelay stderr content. So the user sees a raw banner scrolling past in\n497\tthe hook output, potentially before the AI has even started responding.\n498\tBetter than nothing, but not great.\n499\t\n500\t### What actually works: preToolUse deny\n501\t\n502\tThe breakthrough was realizing that Copilot CLI's `preToolUse` hook can\n503\treturn a **deny response**:\n504\t\n505\t```javascript\n506\treturn {\n507\t  permissionDecision: \"deny\",\n508\t  permissionDecisionReason: \"⚠️ Overlap detected: alice is working on src/auth.ts...\"\n509\t};\n510\t```\n511\t\n512\tWhen a tool call is denied, the AI *has* to tell the user about it —\n513\tit needs to explain why the action failed. This is the one output\n514\tchannel where the model can't silently absorb the message, because the\n515\ttool call didn't succeed and the user needs to know why.\n516\t\n517\tThe pattern: on the first tool call of a session, if file overlaps\n518\texist, deny it with the warning as the reason. Copilot CLI auto-retries\n519\tthe tool call. On retry, the overlap check passes (already warned), and\n520\tthe tool proceeds normally. The user sees the warning, the work\n521\tcontinues, and the deny is invisible except for a brief pause.\n522\t\n523\t```javascript\n524\tfunction _checkOverlapsAndDeny() {\n525\t  if (!_isCopilotCli()) return null;\n526\t  if (isOverlapWarned()) return null;\n527\t\n528\t  // ... detect file overlaps with other users' open threads ...\n529\t\n530\t  if (warnings.length === 0) return null;\n531\t\n532\t  markOverlapWarned();\n533\t\n534\t  return {\n535\t    permissionDecision: \"deny\",\n536\t    permissionDecisionReason: formatOverlapWarning(warnings),\n537\t  };\n538\t}\n539\t```\n540\t\n541\t### The dedup problem\n542\t\n543\tOne deny per session. Sounds simple. It wasn't.\n544\t\n545\tCopilot CLI sessions share the filesystem. The `.sticky-session` file\n546\tthat tracks session identity gets reused across concurrent sessions on\n547\tthe same machine. So \"already warned for this session\" leaked between\n548\tsessions: warn Session A, Session B thinks it was already warned, skips\n549\tthe warning entirely.\n550\t\n551\tWe tried several approaches:\n552\t\n553\t**TTL-based dedup (v2.6.9):** If the warning was shown more than 60\n554\tseconds ago, reset and re-warn. The idea was that a retry comes within\n555\tseconds (so it passes), but a new session would be more than 60 seconds\n556\tlater. Broke because long-running sessions would get re-denied every 60\n557\tseconds.\n558\t\n559\t**Signal file (v2.6.10):** `inject-context.js` writes an\n560\t`.overlap-pending` file when overlaps are detected. `pre-tool-use.js`\n561\treads and deletes it atomically on the first tool call. No shared state,\n562\tno TTL. Broke because the signal file was shared across concurrent\n563\tsessions — Session A's pending file got consumed by Session B.\n564\t\n565\t**PID-keyed dedup (v2.6.11):** The fix that stuck. Copilot CLI sets a\n566\t`COPILOT_LOADER_PID` environment variable that's unique per session and\n567\tinherited by hook child processes. The warned state is stored as a JSON\n568\tobject keyed by PID:\n569\t\n570\t```javascript\n571\t// .sticky-note/.overlap-warned\n572\t{\n573\t  \"12345\": { \"warned_at\": \"2026-03-14T...\" },\n574\t  \"67890\": { \"warned_at\": \"2026-03-14T...\" }\n575\t}\n576\t```\n577\t\n578\tEach session gets its own deny state. A 1-hour TTL cleans up stale\n579\tentries from dead sessions. No cross-session interference.\n580\t\n581\t### Auto-closing orphaned threads\n582\t\n583\tThe per-turn lifecycle creates another problem: Copilot CLI threads\n584\tnever close. There's no reliable session-end signal, so threads stay\n585\t`\"open\"` indefinitely. This pollutes overlap detection — a thread from\n586\tyesterday looks active today.\n587\t\n588\tThe fix: `session-start.js` now runs `autoCloseCopilotCliThreads()`,\n589\twhich closes any `copilot-cli` thread that's been inactive for longer\n590\tthan a configurable threshold (default 24 hours):\n591\t\n592\t```javascript\n593\tif (thread.tool === \"copilot-cli\" && thread.status === \"open\") {\n594\t  const hoursInactive = (now - lastActivity) / (1000 * 60 * 60);\n595\t  if (hoursInactive >= autoCloseHours) {\n596\t    thread.status = \"closed\";\n597\t  }\n598\t}\n599\t```\n600\t\n601\tConfigurable via `copilot_cli_auto_close_hours` in\n602\t`sticky-note-config.json`.\n603\t\n604\t### The final architecture\n605\t\n606\tOverlap warnings now use a three-channel approach:\n607\t\n608\t1. **`additionalContext`** (inject-context.js) — full formatted warning\n609\t   with CRITICAL INSTRUCTION directive. The AI *might* surface it.\n610\t2. **stderr** (inject-context.js) — concise terminal banner. The user\n611\t   *will* see it scroll past in hook output.\n612\t3. **preToolUse deny** (pre-tool-use.js) — blocks the first tool call\n613\t   with the warning as the deny reason. The AI *must* report it.\n614\t\n615\tChannels 1 and 2 fire on every prompt where overlaps exist. Channel 3\n616\tfires once per session (PID-keyed). Together they guarantee the user\n617\tsees the warning through at least one path.\n618\t\n619\t---\n620\t\n621\t## The result\n622\t\n623\tThe full hook mapping, both tools running the same scripts:\n624\t\n625\t| Hook Script          | Claude Code Event     | Copilot CLI Event     |\n626\t|----------------------|-----------------------|-----------------------|\n627\t| `session-start.js`   | SessionStart          | sessionStart          |\n628\t| `session-end.js`     | SessionEnd            | sessionEnd            |\n629\t| `inject-context.js`  | UserPromptSubmit      | userPromptSubmitted   |\n630\t| `pre-tool-use.js`    | PreToolUse            | preToolUse            |\n631\t| `track-work.js`      | PostToolUse           | postToolUse           |\n632\t| `on-error.js`        | PostToolUseFailure    | errorOccurred         |\n633\t\n634\tSix hooks, one codebase, two runtimes. The `--copilot-cli` flag and\n635\t`COPILOT_CLI` env var flow through every hook, triggering the behavioral\n636\tdifferences where the two runtimes diverge:\n637\t\n638\t- Output protocol (`output` vs `additionalContext`)\n639\t- Thread lifecycle (`closed` vs `open`, with auto-close for stale threads)\n640\t- State cleanup (full cleanup vs preserve across turns)\n641\t- Status messaging (\"closed\" vs \"updated\")\n642\t- Dedup handling (clear per session vs PID-keyed persistence)\n643\t- User-facing warnings (injected context vs preToolUse deny + stderr)\n644\t- Thread garbage collection (manual gc vs auto-close after 24h inactivity)\n645\t\n646\tEverything else — the attribution engine, git blame pipeline, Git Notes,\n647\tthread scoring, resume search — works identically in both tools.\n648\t\n649\t---\n650\t\n651\t## What we'd do differently\n652\t\n653\tThe biggest lesson: **hook output is not user output.** We spent twelve\n654\treleases learning that injected context goes to the model, not the\n655\tuser, and the model decides what to surface. If you need guaranteed\n656\tuser visibility from a hook, the only reliable mechanism in Copilot CLI\n657\tis a `preToolUse` deny. We'd design around that constraint from day one\n658\tinstead of discovering it through trial and error.\n659\t\n660\tThe silent protocol mismatch (`output` vs `additionalContext`) wasted\n661\tmore debugging time than any other single issue. No errors, no warnings,\n662\tcontext just vanished. We log every emit call during development now.\n663\t\n664\tAssuming \"session\" means the same thing everywhere was wrong. Copilot\n665\tCLI's per-turn model means session IDs, dedup state, and cleanup logic\n666\tall need different semantics. If we were starting over, we'd design for\n667\tper-turn first and treat per-session as the special case.\n668\t\n669\tConcurrent sessions sharing filesystem state was a recurring headache.\n670\tThe `COPILOT_LOADER_PID` discovery solved the dedup problem, but we\n671\tcould have avoided three intermediate releases if we'd looked for a\n672\tper-session environment variable sooner.\n673\t\n674\tThe instruction file fallback turned out better than expected. Even when\n675\thooks aren't configured at all, the AI reads\n676\t`.github/copilot-instructions.md` and self-serves the same\n677\tfunctionality by calling CLI commands directly. We almost didn't build\n678\tthat, and it's saved us more than once.\n679\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01D475eJNgTQdgVyWqSroyRi","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"64afa796-c267-4c8e-8417-2eb789437695","promptId":"3fda5e08-48fc-4ad0-8c08-6299bb101b4e","sessionId":"c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1c3052566c2e","slug":"generic-stirring-candy","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"64afa796-c267-4c8e-8417-2eb789437695","timestamp":"2026-07-19T06:36:15.102Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"# Making Sticky Note work with Copilot CLI\n\n*We built Sticky Note for Claude Code first. Getting it to work in\nCopilot CLI meant dealing with a bunch of runtime differences nobody\nwarns you about.*\n\n---\n\n## Where we started\n\nSticky Note was born as a Claude Code project. Claude Code has a rich hook\nsystem: `SessionStart`, `SessionEnd`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `PreToolUse`,\n`PostToolUse`. You drop JavaScript files into `.claude/hooks/`, wire them\nup in `settings.json`, and they fire automatically at the right moments.\n\nThe hooks read stdin for context (tool inputs, transcripts, session IDs),\nwrite JSON to stdout to inject context back into the conversation, and\nthat's it.\n\nThen we wanted Sticky Note to work in Copilot CLI too.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Copilot CLI hook limitations\n\nBefore getting into solutions, here are the specific limitations we hit.\nCopilot CLI's hook system is newer than Claude Code's, and several gaps\nforced us into workarounds:\n\n1. **Per-turn lifecycle, not per-session.** Copilot CLI fires\n   `sessionStart` and `sessionEnd` on *every user prompt*, not once per\n   conversation. There is no concept of a persistent session boundary.\n   This means any state management that assumes \"start once, end once\"\n   breaks immediately — threads get prematurely closed, dedup tracking\n   gets wiped, and transient files get cleaned up mid-conversation.\n\n   *How Claude Code does it:* `SessionStart` fires once when you open a\n   session. `SessionEnd` fires once when you close it (or it times out).\n   A session can span dozens of prompts and hundreds of tool calls, and\n   the hooks only fire at the bookends. This makes state management\n   straightforward — initialize on start, finalize on end.\n\n2. **Different output protocol with no shared spec.** Copilot CLI\n   expects `{ \"additionalContext\": \"...\" }` on stdout to inject context.\n   There's no shared protocol or content negotiation — and critically,\n   **sending the wrong key produces no error**. Your hook runs, writes\n   to stdout, exits successfully, and the context silently vanishes.\n   This is the most dangerous kind of incompatibility because nothing\n   tells you it's broken.\n\n   *How Claude Code does it:* Hooks write `{ \"output\": \"...\" }` to\n   stdout to inject context into the conversation. The key name is\n   documented, and while the failure mode is similarly silent, Claude\n   Code's hook ecosystem is more established so the protocol is less\n   likely to surprise you.\n\n3. **No transcript access.** Copilot CLI does not provide hooks with\n   access to the conversation history. Hooks that need to analyze what\n   happened during a session (extracting narratives, detecting failed\n   approaches, summarizing work) have to reconstruct context from the\n   audit trail instead of reading it directly.\n\n   *How Claude Code does it:* The hook input includes a\n   `transcript_path` pointing to a JSONL file containing the full\n   conversation — every user prompt, assistant response, tool call, and\n   tool result. Our `session-end.js` hook reads this transcript to\n   extract narratives, detect error patterns, identify failed approaches,\n   and build a rich thread summary. It's the single most useful piece of\n   data for understanding what happened in a session.\n\n4. **No native session identity.** Copilot CLI's per-turn model means\n   session IDs may or may not persist between turns depending on the\n   implementation. We had to add our own session ID persistence layer\n   (a `.sticky-session` file) to maintain continuity across turns in\n   the same conversation.\n\n   *How Claude Code does it:* A stable `session_id` is provided in the\n   hook input payload and remains consistent across all hook invocations\n   within a session. Hooks can use it directly without any persistence\n   layer — `SessionStart` gets the same ID as `SessionEnd`, and every\n   `PreToolUse` and `PostToolUse` in between.\n\n5. **Hook config lives in a different location and format.** Copilot CLI\n   uses `.github/hooks/hooks.json`. There's no hook config inheritance or\n   shared format, so you maintain two config files pointing at the same\n   scripts.\n\n   *How Claude Code does it:* Hook configuration lives in\n   `.claude/settings.json` in the project root, alongside the hook\n   scripts in `.claude/hooks/`. Config and scripts are co-located, and\n   the settings file supports matchers (tool name, file pattern) to\n   selectively fire hooks — a feature Copilot CLI's config doesn't\n   currently offer.\n\n6. **Cross-platform command differences.** Copilot CLI runs on Windows\n   natively (PowerShell), whereas Claude Code primarily targets\n   Unix-like environments. Every hook command needs both a `bash` and\n   `powershell` variant, including different syntax for setting\n   environment variables (`VAR=1 cmd` vs `$env:VAR='1'; cmd`).\n\n   *How Claude Code does it:* Hooks are specified as a single `command`\n   string that runs in the system shell. Since Claude Code runs\n   primarily on macOS and Linux, you typically only need the bash\n   variant. Windows support isn't a primary concern for Claude Code's\n   hook system.\n\n7. **Instruction file location differs.** Copilot CLI reads\n   `.github/copilot-instructions.md`. Since the instruction file is the\n   fallback when hooks aren't running at all, we need to maintain both —\n   and the Copilot CLI version includes extra self-serve guidance that\n   assumes hooks may not be present.\n\n   *How Claude Code does it:* Instructions live in `CLAUDE.md` at the\n   repo root. Claude Code reads this file automatically at session start,\n   and because Claude Code's hooks are reliable and well-established,\n   the instructions can focus on conventions and guidelines rather than\n   including fallback \"do this manually if hooks aren't working\" sections.\n\n 8. **Hook output is invisible to users.** This is the one that bit us\n    hardest. Both runtimes let hooks inject context into the AI's working\n    memory via stdout (`additionalContext` or `output`). But that context\n    goes to the *model*, not the *user*. The AI decides whether to surface\n    it. For background context (thread data, attribution), that's fine —\n    the AI uses it silently. For urgent warnings (someone else is editing\n    your files), it's a problem. The AI routinely absorbs the warning and\n    says nothing.\n\n    We tried stronger language in the injected text (\"TELL THE USER\n    IMMEDIATELY\"), prescribed exact banner formats, and added instructions\n    to `copilot-instructions.md`. None of it was reliable — the model\n    sometimes complied, sometimes didn't.\n\n    *How Claude Code does it:* Claude Code doesn't have this problem in\n    the same way because its model tends to surface injected context more\n    reliably. We still hit absorption issues occasionally, but less often.\n\n    The solution we found — using `preToolUse` deny responses as a\n    user-visible message channel — is covered in Challenge 8 below.\n\nThese aren't bugs. They're what happens when two tools get built\nindependently with different assumptions. But they mean that\n\"write a hook once, run it everywhere\" is aspirational for now. Every\nhook in Sticky Note has Copilot CLI-specific branches to work\naround these differences.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 1: different hook config, same scripts\n\nClaude Code reads hooks from `.claude/settings.json`. Copilot CLI reads\nthem from `.github/hooks/hooks.json`. Two config files, two formats, but\nwe didn't want to maintain two sets of hook scripts.\n\n**The solution:** One set of hook scripts lives in `.claude/hooks/`. The\nCopilot CLI config in `.github/hooks/hooks.json` points at the *same*\nscripts, but passes a `--copilot-cli` flag and sets a `COPILOT_CLI=1`\nenvironment variable:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"hooks\": {\n    \"sessionStart\": [\n      {\n        \"type\": \"command\",\n        \"bash\": \"COPILOT_CLI=1 node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\",\n        \"powershell\": \"$env:COPILOT_CLI='1'; node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nEvery hook script checks for this flag early and branches behavior where\nneeded:\n\n```javascript\nfunction _isCopilotCli() {\n  return process.argv.includes(\"--copilot-cli\") || !!process.env.COPILOT_CLI;\n}\n```\n\nThis meant we could keep a single codebase for all hooks while handling\nthe runtime differences with simple conditionals.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 2: different event names\n\nClaude Code and Copilot CLI use different naming conventions for the same\nlifecycle events:\n\n| Lifecycle Event          | Claude Code         | Copilot CLI            |\n|--------------------------|---------------------|------------------------|\n| Session starts           | `SessionStart`      | `sessionStart`         |\n| Session ends             | `SessionEnd`        | `sessionEnd`           |\n| User submits prompt      | `UserPromptSubmit`  | `userPromptSubmitted`  |\n| Before tool executes     | `PreToolUse`        | `preToolUse`           |\n| After tool executes      | `PostToolUse`       | `postToolUse`          |\n| Error occurs             | `PostToolUseFailure`| `errorOccurred`        |\n\nClaude Code uses PascalCase. Copilot CLI uses camelCase. The hook input\npayload includes a `hook_event_name` field, so we use the casing to\nauto-detect which runtime we're in:\n\n```javascript\nfunction detectTool(hookInput) {\n  if (process.argv.includes(\"--copilot-cli\")) return \"copilot-cli\";\n  if (process.env.COPILOT_CLI) return \"copilot-cli\";\n\n  if (hookInput) {\n    const event = hookInput.hook_event_name || \"\";\n    // PascalCase = Claude Code, camelCase = Copilot CLI\n    if (event && event[0] === event[0].toUpperCase()\n             && event[0] !== event[0].toLowerCase()) return \"claude-code\";\n    if (event && event[0] === event[0].toLowerCase()\n             && event[0] !== event[0].toUpperCase()) return \"copilot-cli\";\n    // Only Claude Code provides transcript access\n    if (\"transcript_path\" in hookInput) return \"claude-code\";\n  }\n\n  return \"unknown\";\n}\n```\n\nThis four-layer check (explicit flag → env var → event name casing →\ntranscript presence) means the right runtime gets detected even if one\nsignal is missing. The extra `!== toLowerCase()`/`!== toUpperCase()`\nguards prevent false positives on digits or symbols in the event name.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 3: different output protocols\n\nThis was the subtlest difference. Both runtimes read JSON from stdout to\ninject context into the conversation, but they expect **different keys**:\n\n- **Claude Code** expects `{ \"output\": \"text to inject\" }`\n- **Copilot CLI** expects `{ \"additionalContext\": \"text to inject\" }`\n\nIf you send `output` to Copilot CLI, it silently ignores it. If you send\n`additionalContext` to Claude Code, same thing. No error, just silence —\nyour context injection simply doesn't work.\n\nEvery hook that injects context uses a wrapper:\n\n```javascript\nfunction _emit(text) {\n  if (_isCopilotCli()) {\n    process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify({ additionalContext: text }) + \"\\n\");\n  } else {\n    process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify({ output: text }) + \"\\n\");\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis was one of those bugs that took forever to find because there was\nno error. Things just silently didn't work until we checked the protocol\ndifference.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 4: per-turn vs per-session lifecycle\n\nThis is the big one.\n\nIn **Claude Code**, `SessionStart` fires once when you open a session and\n`SessionEnd` fires once when you close it. A session is a long-lived\nconversation — you might have 50 tool calls across 20 prompts, and the\nhooks fire at the bookends.\n\nIn **Copilot CLI**, `sessionStart` and `sessionEnd` fire **on every\nturn**. Each user prompt is its own mini-session. This difference\nbreaks a bunch of assumptions:\n\n### Thread status: open vs closed\n\nIn Claude Code, `session-end.js` closes the thread because the session\nis truly over. In Copilot CLI, closing the thread on every turn would\nmean it flips between open and closed dozens of times in a conversation.\n\n**Fix:** Copilot CLI threads stay `\"open\"`:\n\n```javascript\nstatus: isCopilotCli ? \"open\" : \"closed\",\nclosed_at: isCopilotCli ? null : now,\n```\n\n### Dedup state\n\n`session-start.js` clears the injection dedup set\n(`.sticky-injected`) at the start of each session so threads can be\nre-injected next time. But in Copilot CLI, \"session start\" fires every\nturn, so clearing the dedup set would re-inject the same threads on\nevery single prompt.\n\n**Fix:** Skip the clear for Copilot CLI:\n\n```javascript\n// Copilot CLI fires SessionStart per-turn; clearing would lose dedup state.\nif (!isCopilotCli) {\n  clearInjectedSet();\n  clearActiveResumeThreadId();\n}\n```\n\n### Transient file cleanup\n\nAt session end, Claude Code cleans up transient files (`.sticky-session`,\n`.sticky-head`, `.sticky-injected`, presence data). In Copilot CLI, doing\nthis after every turn would destroy state that the next turn needs.\n\n**Fix:** Skip cleanup for Copilot CLI:\n\n```javascript\n// Copilot CLI fires per-turn; clearing would break state across turns.\nif (!isCopilotCli) {\n  clearPresence(user);\n  clearSessionFile();\n  clearHeadFile();\n  clearInjectedSet();\n}\n```\n\n### Status messages\n\nEven the human-facing output adapts:\n\n```javascript\nconst statusLabel = isCopilotCli ? \"updated\" : \"closed\";\nconst statusMsg = `[STICKY-NOTE] Session ${statusLabel} - thread ${\n  isCopilotCli ? \"updated\" : \"created\"\n}`;\n```\n\nA Claude Code user sees \"Session closed — thread created.\" A Copilot CLI\nuser sees \"Session updated — thread updated.\" Same hook, different\nwording that matches the actual lifecycle.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 5: cross-platform commands\n\nCopilot CLI runs on Windows, macOS, and Linux. Claude Code primarily\ntargets macOS and Linux. The hooks need to work everywhere.\n\nThe `hooks.json` config includes **both bash and PowerShell** variants\nfor every hook:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"bash\": \"COPILOT_CLI=1 node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\",\n  \"powershell\": \"$env:COPILOT_CLI='1'; node .claude/hooks/session-start.js --copilot-cli\"\n}\n```\n\nSetting environment variables works differently (`VAR=1 command` in bash\nvs `$env:VAR='1'; command` in PowerShell), so both forms are provided.\nThe runtime picks the right one for the OS.\n\nInside the hooks themselves, all path handling uses `path.join()` and a\n`normalizeSep()` helper that converts backslashes to forward slashes for\nconsistent storage:\n\n```javascript\nfunction normalizeSep(p) {\n  return typeof p === \"string\" ? p.replace(/\\\\/g, \"/\") : p;\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that file paths recorded on Windows can be matched against\nfile paths recorded on macOS, and vice versa.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 6: the instruction file split\n\nClaude Code reads AI instructions from `CLAUDE.md` at the repo root.\nCopilot CLI reads from `.github/copilot-instructions.md`. Same purpose,\ndifferent locations, different audiences.\n\nWe maintain both, but with a difference: the Copilot CLI instructions\ninclude a **self-serve fallback** that Claude Code doesn't need.\n\nSince Copilot CLI's hook support was initially limited (and some\ndeployments may not run hooks at all), the instructions tell the AI to\nself-serve context directly:\n\n```markdown\n### Proactive context injection (V2.5)\n\nCopilot CLI does not have lifecycle hooks, so **you must self-serve context**.\n\n#### 1. At session start, check for stuck/open threads\nBefore doing any work, read `.sticky-note/sticky-note.json`...\n\n#### 2. Before editing a file, check for prior thread context\nThe first time you edit or read a file in a session, run:\n  npx sticky-note get-line-attribution --file <path>\n```\n\nThis means Sticky Note works in Copilot CLI even when hooks aren't\nconfigured. The AI reads the instructions and calls the CLI commands\nitself. Hooks just make it automatic.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 7: cross-tool thread continuity\n\nA thread started in Claude Code should be resumable in Copilot CLI, and\nvice versa. This required:\n\n1. **Tool-agnostic thread format:** Every thread records a `tool` field\n   (`\"claude-code\"` or `\"copilot-cli\"`), but the thread structure itself\n   is identical. No tool-specific fields.\n\n2. **Session ID compatibility:** Both tools generate UUIDs for session\n   IDs. The `session-start.js` hook normalizes this — if the runtime\n   doesn't provide a session ID, the hook generates one:\n\n   ```javascript\n   if (sessionId === \"unknown\") {\n     sessionId = crypto.randomUUID();\n   }\n   saveSessionId(sessionId);\n   ```\n\n3. **Prompts stored for cross-tool resume:** Each thread stores the\n   user's prompts (up to 20, truncated to 300 chars). When resuming a\n   thread in a different tool, the prompts provide enough context to\n   understand what happened:\n\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"prompts\": [\n       \"fix the auth token refresh race condition\",\n       \"that didn't work, try using a mutex instead\",\n       \"run the tests again\"\n     ]\n   }\n   ```\n\n4. **Smart resume works across tools:** The `resume-thread` command\n   searches by text similarity against narratives and prompts — it\n   doesn't filter by tool. \"Pick up where the last session left off\"\n   works whether that session was Claude Code or Copilot CLI.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 8: getting messages to the user\n\nThis one took us twelve releases to solve.\n\n### The problem\n\nOverlap detection landed in v2.6.0: when you start working and someone\nelse has an open thread touching the same files, you should know about\nit. The detection logic was straightforward. Getting the warning in\nfront of the user was not.\n\nHook output (`additionalContext` in Copilot CLI) goes to the AI model's\ncontext window. The model *sees* it, but it doesn't necessarily *show*\nit. For thread context and attribution data, that's the right behavior —\nthe AI should absorb it silently and use it when relevant. But for\n\"hey, Alice is actively editing the same files you're about to touch,\"\nsilent absorption defeats the purpose.\n\n### What we tried (and what failed)\n\n**Attempt 1: sessionStart output.** Put the overlap warning in\n`session-start.js` output. Copilot CLI silently dropped it. Turns out\n`sessionStart` hook output isn't reliably surfaced at all.\n\n**Attempt 2: userPromptSubmitted output.** Moved the warning into\n`inject-context.js` so it arrives with every prompt. The AI received it\nbut treated it as background context. No mention to the user.\n\n**Attempt 3: stronger language.** Added directives like \"TELL THE USER\nIMMEDIATELY\" and \"You MUST surface this warning\" to the injected text.\nSometimes the AI complied. Sometimes it didn't. Not reliable enough for\na safety-critical warning.\n\n**Attempt 4: exact banner format.** Prescribed the exact text the AI\nshould display, down to the emoji and formatting. Added matching rules\nto both `CLAUDE.md` and `copilot-instructions.md`. Better compliance\nrate, but still not guaranteed.\n\n**Attempt 5: stderr.** Wrote a concise banner directly to `stderr` so\nit appears in the user's terminal regardless of what the AI does:\n\n```javascript\nprocess.stderr.write(\"\\n⚠️  OVERLAP DETECTED — someone else is touching your files:\\n\");\nprocess.stderr.write(`   [STUCK] alice: src/auth.ts — token refresh race condition\\n`);\n```\n\nThis works for humans reading the terminal, but AI assistants don't\nrelay stderr content. So the user sees a raw banner scrolling past in\nthe hook output, potentially before the AI has even started responding.\nBetter than nothing, but not great.\n\n### What actually works: preToolUse deny\n\nThe breakthrough was realizing that Copilot CLI's `preToolUse` hook can\nreturn a **deny response**:\n\n```javascript\nreturn {\n  permissionDecision: \"deny\",\n  permissionDecisionReason: \"⚠️ Overlap detected: alice is working on src/auth.ts...\"\n};\n```\n\nWhen a tool call is denied, the AI *has* to tell the user about it —\nit needs to explain why the action failed. This is the one output\nchannel where the model can't silently absorb the message, because the\ntool call didn't succeed and the user needs to know why.\n\nThe pattern: on the first tool call of a session, if file overlaps\nexist, deny it with the warning as the reason. Copilot CLI auto-retries\nthe tool call. On retry, the overlap check passes (already warned), and\nthe tool proceeds normally. The user sees the warning, the work\ncontinues, and the deny is invisible except for a brief pause.\n\n```javascript\nfunction _checkOverlapsAndDeny() {\n  if (!_isCopilotCli()) return null;\n  if (isOverlapWarned()) return null;\n\n  // ... detect file overlaps with other users' open threads ...\n\n  if (warnings.length === 0) return null;\n\n  markOverlapWarned();\n\n  return {\n    permissionDecision: \"deny\",\n    permissionDecisionReason: formatOverlapWarning(warnings),\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### The dedup problem\n\nOne deny per session. Sounds simple. It wasn't.\n\nCopilot CLI sessions share the filesystem. The `.sticky-session` file\nthat tracks session identity gets reused across concurrent sessions on\nthe same machine. So \"already warned for this session\" leaked between\nsessions: warn Session A, Session B thinks it was already warned, skips\nthe warning entirely.\n\nWe tried several approaches:\n\n**TTL-based dedup (v2.6.9):** If the warning was shown more than 60\nseconds ago, reset and re-warn. The idea was that a retry comes within\nseconds (so it passes), but a new session would be more than 60 seconds\nlater. Broke because long-running sessions would get re-denied every 60\nseconds.\n\n**Signal file (v2.6.10):** `inject-context.js` writes an\n`.overlap-pending` file when overlaps are detected. `pre-tool-use.js`\nreads and deletes it atomically on the first tool call. No shared state,\nno TTL. Broke because the signal file was shared across concurrent\nsessions — Session A's pending file got consumed by Session B.\n\n**PID-keyed dedup (v2.6.11):** The fix that stuck. Copilot CLI sets a\n`COPILOT_LOADER_PID` environment variable that's unique per session and\ninherited by hook child processes. The warned state is stored as a JSON\nobject keyed by PID:\n\n```javascript\n// .sticky-note/.overlap-warned\n{\n  \"12345\": { \"warned_at\": \"2026-03-14T...\" },\n  \"67890\": { \"warned_at\": \"2026-03-14T...\" }\n}\n```\n\nEach session gets its own deny state. A 1-hour TTL cleans up stale\nentries from dead sessions. No cross-session interference.\n\n### Auto-closing orphaned threads\n\nThe per-turn lifecycle creates another problem: Copilot CLI threads\nnever close. There's no reliable session-end signal, so threads stay\n`\"open\"` indefinitely. This pollutes overlap detection — a thread from\nyesterday looks active today.\n\nThe fix: `session-start.js` now runs `autoCloseCopilotCliThreads()`,\nwhich closes any `copilot-cli` thread that's been inactive for longer\nthan a configurable threshold (default 24 hours):\n\n```javascript\nif (thread.tool === \"copilot-cli\" && thread.status === \"open\") {\n  const hoursInactive = (now - lastActivity) / (1000 * 60 * 60);\n  if (hoursInactive >= autoCloseHours) {\n    thread.status = \"closed\";\n  }\n}\n```\n\nConfigurable via `copilot_cli_auto_close_hours` in\n`sticky-note-config.json`.\n\n### The final architecture\n\nOverlap warnings now use a three-channel approach:\n\n1. **`additionalContext`** (inject-context.js) — full formatted warning\n   with CRITICAL INSTRUCTION directive. The AI *might* surface it.\n2. **stderr** (inject-context.js) — concise terminal banner. The user\n   *will* see it scroll past in hook output.\n3. **preToolUse deny** (pre-tool-use.js) — blocks the first tool call\n   with the warning as the deny reason. The AI *must* report it.\n\nChannels 1 and 2 fire on every prompt where overlaps exist. Channel 3\nfires once per session (PID-keyed). Together they guarantee the user\nsees the warning through at least one path.\n\n---\n\n## The result\n\nThe full hook mapping, both tools running the same scripts:\n\n| Hook Script          | Claude Code Event     | Copilot CLI Event     |\n|----------------------|-----------------------|-----------------------|\n| `session-start.js`   | SessionStart          | sessionStart          |\n| `session-end.js`     | SessionEnd            | sessionEnd            |\n| `inject-context.js`  | UserPromptSubmit      | userPromptSubmitted   |\n| `pre-tool-use.js`    | PreToolUse            | preToolUse            |\n| `track-work.js`      | PostToolUse           | postToolUse           |\n| `on-error.js`        | PostToolUseFailure    | errorOccurred         |\n\nSix hooks, one codebase, two runtimes. The `--copilot-cli` flag and\n`COPILOT_CLI` env var flow through every hook, triggering the behavioral\ndifferences where the two runtimes diverge:\n\n- Output protocol (`output` vs `additionalContext`)\n- Thread lifecycle (`closed` vs `open`, with auto-close for stale threads)\n- State cleanup (full cleanup vs preserve across turns)\n- Status messaging (\"closed\" vs \"updated\")\n- Dedup handling (clear per session vs PID-keyed persistence)\n- User-facing warnings (injected context vs preToolUse deny + stderr)\n- Thread garbage collection (manual gc vs auto-close after 24h inactivity)\n\nEverything else — the attribution engine, git blame pipeline, Git Notes,\nthread scoring, resume search — works identically in both tools.\n\n---\n\n## What we'd do differently\n\nThe biggest lesson: **hook output is not user output.** We spent twelve\nreleases learning that injected context goes to the model, not the\nuser, and the model decides what to surface. If you need guaranteed\nuser visibility from a hook, the only reliable mechanism in Copilot CLI\nis a `preToolUse` deny. We'd design around that constraint from day one\ninstead of discovering it through trial and error.\n\nThe silent protocol mismatch (`output` vs `additionalContext`) wasted\nmore debugging time than any other single issue. No errors, no warnings,\ncontext just vanished. We log every emit call during development now.\n\nAssuming \"session\" means the same thing everywhere was wrong. Copilot\nCLI's per-turn model means session IDs, dedup state, and cleanup logic\nall need different semantics. If we were starting over, we'd design for\nper-turn first and treat per-session as the special case.\n\nConcurrent sessions sharing filesystem state was a recurring headache.\nThe `COPILOT_LOADER_PID` discovery solved the dedup problem, but we\ncould have avoided three intermediate releases if we'd looked for a\nper-session environment variable sooner.\n\nThe instruction file fallback turned out better than expected. Even when\nhooks aren't configured at all, the AI reads\n`.github/copilot-instructions.md` and self-serves the same\nfunctionality by calling CLI commands directly. We almost didn't build\nthat, and it's saved us more than once.\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/BandaruDheeraj/sticky-note/docs/making-sticky-note-work-with-copilot-cli.md","numLines":679,"startLine":1,"totalLines":679},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"bc091aa6-9460-4144-84bd-e054437818d0","version":"2.1.215"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T02:16:46.077Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
