;\n  }\n}\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/src/ledger-io.ts","numLines":38,"startLine":1,"totalLines":38},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"90de7771-aeaa-496c-840d-3ec70ae4cf15","version":"2.1.216"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T02:16:45.210Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t<p align=\"center\">\n2\t  <!-- Replace with your own logo -->\n3\t  <img src=\"docs/images/logo.png\" alt=\"Sticky Note\" width=\"600\" />\n4\t</p>\n5\t<p align=\"center\">\n6\t  <strong>Human-to-human handoff for AI coding assistants.</strong><br/>\n7\t  Git-backed shared memory that captures session threads and surfaces<br/>\n8\t  teammate context — automatically, inside the tools you already use.\n9\t</p>\n10\t\n11\t<p align=\"center\">\n12\t  <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/sticky-note-cli\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/npm/v/sticky-note-cli.svg?style=flat-square&color=f59e0b\" alt=\"npm version\" /></a>\n13\t  <a href=\"LICENSE\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue.svg?style=flat-square\" alt=\"MIT License\" /></a>\n14\t  <a href=\"https://nodejs.org\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/node-%3E%3D16-brightgreen.svg?style=flat-square\" alt=\"Node.js >= 16\" /></a>\n15\t  <a href=\"#supported-tools\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/works%20with-Claude%20%C2%B7%20Copilot%20%C2%B7%20Codex-8b5cf6?style=flat-square\" alt=\"Works with Claude, Copilot, Codex\" /></a>\n16\t</p>\n17\t\n18\t<br/>\n19\t\n20\t<p align=\"center\">\n21\t  <img src=\"docs/images/demo.gif\" alt=\"Sticky Note demo\" width=\"600\" />\n22\t</p>\n23\t\n24\t<p align=\"center\">\n25\t  <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/BandaruDheeraj/sticky-note/main/docs/images/architecture.svg\" alt=\"Architecture diagram\" width=\"800\" />\n26\t</p>\n27\t\n28\t<br/>\n29\t\n30\t> [!NOTE]\n31\t> **Sticky Note is evolving.** Features, APIs, and file formats may change as we learn what works best for teams.\n32\t\n33\t---\n34\t\n35\t## The Problem\n36\t\n37\tWhen multiple developers on a team use AI coding assistants, each assistant operates in its own bubble. Your agent doesn't know that your teammate's agent just spent 45 minutes debugging token rotation in the same file you're about to refactor. It doesn't know what they tried and abandoned. It doesn't know they're stuck.\n38\t\n39\tThe result: duplicated effort, conflicting changes, and context that evaporates the moment a session ends.\n40\t\n41\tThere are plenty of agent-to-agent orchestration frameworks out there, but that's not how most teams actually work. We work with other people — people who also happen to use agents. We needed a way for those agents to share context without changing how anyone works.\n42\t\n43\tSo we built Sticky Note.\n44\t\n45\t## The Solution\n46\t\n47\tSticky Note captures what happened in each AI session (files touched, status,\n48\tnarrative, failed approaches) and surfaces it to teammates automatically on\n49\ttheir next session by relevance.\n50\t\n51\t---\n52\t\n53\t## What's New in V2.7\n54\t\n55\t- **Team Environment Sync**: Share your complete AI dev environment through git. Skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, and permissions auto-provision when teammates start a session — zero manual setup\n56\t- **`bootstrap` command**: Interactive secrets provisioning for MCP servers that need credentials. Generates `.env.example` for the team\n57\t- **`env` commands**: `env status` shows provisioning state, `env add-server` adds MCP servers interactively\n58\t- **Dual-target provisioning**: Skills deployed to both Claude Code plugins and Copilot CLI extensions automatically\n59\t\n60\t### What's New in V2.6\n61\t\n62\t- **MCP server**: 8-tool MCP server for reliable AI-to-sticky-note communication. Auto-registers in `.mcp.json` on first session. Works with Claude Code, Copilot CLI, and any MCP-compatible client\n63\t- **Styled overlap banners**: Overlap warnings now use structured box-drawing format with 🔴/🟡 status indicators, ANSI-colored stderr output\n64\t- **Overlap detection**: Warns when you're working on files another teammate has open/stuck threads on. Three delivery channels: MCP tool, injected context, and preToolUse deny\n65\t- **File claiming**: `npx sticky-note-cli claim src/auth.ts \"refactoring auth flow\"` — declare intent to work on files\n66\t- **Auto-close Copilot CLI threads**: Threads from Copilot CLI sessions auto-close after configurable inactivity (default 24h), since Copilot CLI has no session-end signal\n67\t- **PID-keyed session isolation**: Concurrent Copilot CLI sessions get independent overlap dedup via `COPILOT_LOADER_PID`\n68\t\n69\t### What's New in V2.5\n70\t\n71\t- **Smart injection (two-tier)**: Stuck threads injected eagerly at session start; all other threads injected lazily when you first touch a file they authored — via built-in git blame attribution\n72\t- **Thread resume (local)**: `npx sticky-note-cli resume-thread --query \"auth fix\" --user alice` — natural language thread discovery with text similarity + file attribution ranking\n73\t- **Built-in attribution engine**: `npx sticky-note-cli get-line-attribution --file src/auth.ts` — maps file lines to commit SHAs via git blame, resolves to threads with line ranges. No external dependencies.\n74\t- **Git Notes storage**: Session attribution stored in `refs/notes/sticky-note` — survives rebase/amend\n75\t- **Three-tier SHA resolution**: Git Notes → Audit JSONL → File+date heuristic — attribution never breaks\n76\t- **PreToolUse hook**: New hook fires before each tool call for lazy injection with line-range detail\n77\t- **Thread schema additions**: `contributors[]`, `resumed_by`, `resumed_at`, `resume_history[]` — multi-contributor threads with full resume chain\n78\t\n79\t### What's New in V2\n80\t\n81\t- **Per-user audit & presence**: Audit logs and presence in per-user files under `audit/` and `presence/` — committed and shared with the team\n82\t- **Relevance scoring**: Context injected based on file overlap, branch match, and recency\n83\t- **Richer threads**: Narrative summaries, failed approaches, work type, activities\n84\t- **Tombstone expiry**: Old threads are automatically cleaned up via `gc`\n85\t- **Presence tracking**: See who's currently active with `npx sticky-note-cli who`\n86\t- **Codex support**: Wrapper script for post-session capture\n87\t- **Separate config**: Team settings in `sticky-note-config.json`\n88\t\n89\t---\n90\t\n91\t## Quick Start\n92\t\n93\t### 1. Install\n94\t\n95\t```bash\n96\tnpx sticky-note-cli init\n97\t```\n98\t\n99\tThis runs an interactive setup that:\n100\t- [OK] Checks for git and Node.js 16+\n101\t- 📋 Asks for team config (MCP servers, conventions, stale days)\n102\t- 📁 Creates all hook scripts and config files\n103\t\n104\t### 2. Commit\n105\t\n106\t```bash\n107\tgit add .claude .github .sticky-note .gitignore .gitattributes CLAUDE.md\n108\tgit commit -m \"feat: add sticky-note hooks\"\n109\t```\n110\t\n111\t### 3. Push & Pull\n112\t\n113\t```bash\n114\tgit push        # Share with team\n115\tgit pull        # Teammates — no additional setup needed\n116\t```\n117\t\n118\t### 4. Work\n119\t\n120\tOpen Claude Code or Copilot CLI and start working. Sticky Note runs\n121\tin the background via hooks — capturing threads and surfacing context.\n122\t\n123\t**First thing to try** — ask your AI agent:\n124\t\n125\t> \"Show me the active sticky note threads\"\n126\t\n127\t---\n128\t\n129\t## How Context Gets Injected\n130\t\n131\tSticky Note injects context through **four mechanisms**: two that run\n132\tautomatically via hooks, one triggered manually via the CLI, and one\n133\tthat's always available as a static file.\n134\t\n135\t### 1. Static Instruction Files (always available)\n136\t\n137\t`npx sticky-note-cli init` deploys AI instruction files that teach each tool\n138\thow to interact with Sticky Note:\n139\t\n140\t| File | Consumed by | Installed to |\n141\t|------|-------------|--------------|\n142\t| `CLAUDE.md` | Claude Code | repo root |\n143\t| `copilot-instructions.md` | Copilot CLI | `.github/` |\n144\t\n145\tThese files contain thread field references, status icons, resume\n146\tinstructions, display formats, and query examples. They're wrapped in\n147\t`<!-- sticky-note:start/end -->` markers so `npx sticky-note-cli update` can\n148\trefresh them without overwriting your own content.\n149\t\n150\t### 2. Session Start Hook (`session-start.js`)\n151\t\n152\tRuns once when a session begins. V2.5 uses **eager injection** for stuck\n153\tthreads only — non-stuck threads are held for lazy injection.\n154\t\n155\t- **Resumed thread** — If a `.sticky-resume` signal file exists, the full\n156\t  thread payload is injected: narrative, files touched, failed approaches,\n157\t  conversation prompts, and the complete resume chain history.\n158\t- **Stuck threads** — All stuck threads are injected eagerly (V2.5).\n159\t- **Team config** — Conventions, MCP servers, and skills from\n160\t  `sticky-note-config.json`.\n161\t- **Active presence** — Developers seen in the last hour and the\n162\t  files they're working on.\n163\t\n164\tThe hook also clears the injected-this-session tracking set, snapshots\n165\t`HEAD`, generates a session ID, and ages stale threads.\n166\t\n167\t### 3. Per-Prompt Injection (`inject-context.js`)\n168\t\n169\tRuns on **every user prompt**. Scores all live threads by relevance and\n170\tinjects the top 3–5 (under token budget) as additional context.\n171\t**V2.5:** Skips threads already injected by session-start or PreToolUse.\n172\t\n173\t| Signal | Weight | Description |\n174\t|--------|--------|-------------|\n175\t| File overlap | 3 per file (max 5) | Thread files match your recent git changes |\n176\t| Branch match | 2 | Thread is on your current branch |\n177\t| Recency | 2 | Decays 0.2 per day from last activity |\n178\t| Stuck status | +2 | Boost for threads marked stuck |\n179\t| Prompt keywords | 1 | File names mentioned in your prompt |\n180\t| Same developer | 1 | Your own previous threads |\n181\t| Resume signal | +10 | Thread targeted by `.sticky-resume` |\n182\t\n183\t### 3b. PreToolUse Hook (`pre-tool-use.js`) — V2.5\n184\t\n185\tRuns **before each tool call**. Lazy injection tier:\n186\t\n187\t1. Extracts target file from tool input\n188\t2. Runs `git blame --line-porcelain <file>` → commit SHAs per line\n189\t3. Three-tier SHA resolution: Git Notes → Audit JSONL → File+date heuristic\n190\t4. Resolves session IDs → loads threads with line ranges\n191\t5. Injects matching threads (if not already injected this session)\n192\t\n193\tNo external dependencies — uses built-in git blame.\n194\t\n195\t### 4. Resume Flow (`npx sticky-note-cli resume <id>`)\n196\t\n197\tA manual trigger that enables **cross-tool handoff** (e.g., Claude Code →\n198\tCopilot CLI):\n199\t\n200\t```\n201\tnpx sticky-note-cli resume <thread-id>\n202\t```\n203\t\n204\t1. Writes the thread UUID to a `.sticky-resume` signal file.\n205\t2. Outputs the thread's full context to the terminal (narrative, files,\n206\t   failed approaches, conversation prompts).\n207\t3. On the next session start, `session-start.js` detects the signal,\n208\t   reopens the thread as `open`, and injects the complete payload.\n209\t4. `inject-context.js` gives the resumed thread a +10 score boost on\n210\t   every prompt for the duration of the session.\n211\t5. `session-end.js` clears the signal file and updates the thread's\n212\t   `resume_chain` with the new session.\n213\t\n214\tThis means a thread started in Claude Code can be resumed in Copilot CLI\n215\t(or vice versa) with full context preserved — including the original\n216\tconversation prompts.\n217\t\n218\t---\n219\t\n220\t## How Context Gets Collected\n221\t\n222\tThe injection hooks above are fed by four **collection hooks** that run\n223\tsilently in the background:\n224\t\n225\t### Tool Tracking (`track-work.js`)\n226\t\n227\tRuns after every tool use. Appends a JSONL audit entry with the tool\n228\tname, file path, and session ID. Also updates the user's presence file\n229\t(`presence/<username>.json`) so `session-start.js` can show who's active.\n230\t\n231\t### Session End (`session-end.js`)\n232\t\n233\tRuns when a session ends. Captures the full thread record:\n234\t\n235\t- **Files touched** — from audit trail, transcript parsing, and git diff\n236\t  against the HEAD snapshot from session start.\n237\t- **Narrative** — last assistant message (300 char max).\n238\t- **Work type** — inferred from activity patterns (bug-fix, feature,\n239\t  debugging, testing, documentation, etc.).\n240\t- **Failed approaches** — extracted where error + retry patterns both match.\n241\t- **Prompts** — stored for cross-tool resume (up to 20, 300 chars each).\n242\t- **Tool calls** — counts per tool (Edit, Read, Write, etc.).\n243\t\n244\tAlso runs a lazy tombstone sweep: closed threads older than `stale_days`\n245\tare expired to minimal footprint.\n246\t\n247\t### Error Capture (`on-error.js`)\n248\t\n249\tRuns when a tool execution fails. Creates or updates the session thread\n250\twith status `stuck` and appends the error to `failed_approaches`. Next\n251\tsession's `inject-context.js` ranks stuck threads higher so teammates\n252\tsee them.\n253\t\n254\t### Stop Handler (`on-stop.js`, Claude Code only)\n255\t\n256\tRuns when the user stops a session. Builds a structured handoff summary\n257\t(what was done, what failed, current status, next steps) and saves it to\n258\tthe thread's `handoff_summary` field.\n259\t\n260\t---\n261\t\n262\t## What Gets Captured\n263\t\n264\t| Data              | Captured | Example                              |\n265\t|-------------------|----------|--------------------------------------|\n266\t| Files touched     | [OK]        | `src/auth.ts`, `lib/db.py`          |\n267\t| Thread status     | [OK]        | open, stuck, stale, closed, expired  |\n268\t| Author            | [OK]        | OS username                          |\n269\t| Timestamp         | [OK]        | ISO 8601                             |\n270\t| Narrative         | [OK]        | \"Fixed auth token refresh flow\"      |\n271\t| Failed approaches | [OK]        | What was tried, errors, files        |\n272\t| Work type         | [OK]        | bug-fix, feature, debugging, etc.    |\n273\t| Code content      | [ERR]        | Never captured                       |\n274\t| Conversation      | [ERR]        | Never captured                       |\n275\t| Credentials       | [ERR]        | Never captured                       |\n276\t\n277\t---\n278\t\n279\t## Thread Lifecycle\n280\t\n281\t```\n282\topen → stale  (auto, after stale_days with no activity)\n283\topen → closed (on session end)\n284\tstuck → closed (on session end or manual)\n285\tclosed → open  (via `npx sticky-note-cli resume <id>`)\n286\tclosed → expired (auto, tombstoned by gc after stale_days)\n287\t```\n288\t\n289\tExpired threads keep only their ID, status, user, and closed timestamp.\n290\t\n291\t---\n292\t\n293\t## File Structure\n294\t\n295\t```\n296\tCLAUDE.md                         # AI instructions for Claude Code\n297\t\n298\t.claude/\n299\t├── settings.json             # Claude Code hook config\n300\t└── hooks/\n301\t    ├── sticky-utils.js       # Shared utilities\n302\t    ├── session-start.js      # Load & inject teammate context\n303\t    ├── session-end.js        # Capture session thread\n304\t    ├── inject-context.js     # Per-prompt relevance scoring\n305\t    ├── pre-tool-use.js       # Lazy injection via git blame attribution (V2.5)\n306\t    ├── sticky-attribution.js # Built-in attribution engine (V2.5)\n307\t    ├── sticky-git-notes.js   # Git Notes utilities (V2.5)\n308\t    ├── track-work.js         # JSONL audit + presence + line tracking (V2.5)\n309\t    ├── parse-transcript.js   # Narrative + failed approach extraction\n310\t    ├── on-stop.js            # Handoff summary on stop\n311\t    ├── on-error.js           # Stuck thread on error\n312\t    ├── post-rewrite.js       # Git Notes rewrite survival (V2.5)\n313\t    └── sticky-codex.sh       # Optional Codex wrapper\n314\t\n315\t.github/\n316\t├── copilot-instructions.md   # AI instructions for Copilot CLI\n317\t└── hooks/\n318\t    └── hooks.json            # Copilot CLI hook config\n319\t\n320\t.sticky-note/\n321\t├── sticky-note.json          # Shared threads (git-tracked)\n322\t├── sticky-note-config.json   # Team config (git-tracked)\n323\t├── audit/                    # Per-user audit logs (git-tracked)\n324\t│   └── <username>.jsonl      # One file per team member\n325\t├── presence/                 # Per-user presence (git-tracked)\n326\t│   └── <username>.json       # One file per team member\n327\t├── .sticky-resume            # Resume signal (local only)\n328\t├── .sticky-injected          # Injection tracking (local only, V2.5)\n329\t└── .sticky-active-resume     # Active resume marker (local only, V2.5)\n330\t```\n331\t\n332\t---\n333\t\n334\t## CLI Commands\n335\t\n336\t```bash\n337\tnpx sticky-note-cli init           # Interactive setup\n338\tnpx sticky-note-cli init --codex   # Setup with Codex wrapper\n339\tnpx sticky-note-cli update         # Update hook scripts (preserves data)\n340\tnpx sticky-note-cli status         # Diagnostic report (includes attribution health)\n341\tnpx sticky-note-cli threads        # List threads with status icons\n342\tnpx sticky-note-cli resume         # List resumable threads\n343\tnpx sticky-note-cli resume <id>    # Resume a previous thread\n344\tnpx sticky-note-cli resume --clear # Cancel active resume\n345\tnpx sticky-note-cli resume-thread  # Smart resume: --query, --user, --file (V2.5)\n346\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit          # Query merged audit trail (all users)\n347\tnpx sticky-note-cli who            # Show active and recent team members\n348\tnpx sticky-note-cli overlap        # Detect file overlaps with teammates (V2.6)\n349\tnpx sticky-note-cli claim <files>  # Declare intent to work on files (V2.6)\n350\tnpx sticky-note-cli switch <branch> # Safe branch switch (auto-stashes data)\n351\tnpx sticky-note-cli gc             # Tombstone expired threads\n352\tnpx sticky-note-cli reset          # Wipe all threads (--force, --keep-audit)\n353\tnpx sticky-note-cli get-line-attribution # File→thread attribution with line ranges (V2.5)\n354\tnpx sticky-note-cli checkpoint         # Set work-topic checkpoint for attribution (V2.5)\n355\tnpx sticky-note-cli sync               # Commit .sticky-note/ changes (--push to also push)\n356\tnpx sticky-note-cli bootstrap         # Provision MCP servers that need secrets\n357\tnpx sticky-note-cli env status         # Show environment provisioning status\n358\tnpx sticky-note-cli env add-server     # Add MCP server to team environment\n359\tnpx sticky-note mcp-server             # Launch MCP server (stdio JSON-RPC)\n360\tnpx sticky-note-cli --version      # Show version\n361\tnpx sticky-note-cli --help         # Show help\n362\t```\n363\t\n364\t### Audit Filters\n365\t\n366\t```bash\n367\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit --user alice\n368\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit --file src/auth.ts\n369\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit --since 2025-01-01\n370\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit --session abc-123\n371\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit --limit 100\n372\t```\n373\t\n374\t---\n375\t\n376\t## Configuration\n377\t\n378\tEdit `.sticky-note/sticky-note-config.json`:\n379\t\n380\t```json\n381\t{\n382\t  \"stale_days\": 14,\n383\t  \"copilot_cli_auto_close_hours\": 24,\n384\t  \"inject_token_budget\": 1000,\n385\t  \"mcp_servers\": [],\n386\t  \"skills\": [],\n387\t  \"conventions\": [\"Use TypeScript strict mode\", \"Test before commit\"],\n388\t  \"hook_version\": \"2.6.12\"\n389\t}\n390\t```\n391\t\n392\t| Key            | Description                              | Default |\n393\t|----------------|------------------------------------------|---------|\n394\t| `stale_days`   | Days before threads expire + gc cleanup  | `14`    |\n395\t| `copilot_cli_auto_close_hours` | Hours before idle Copilot CLI threads auto-close | `24` |\n396\t| `inject_token_budget` | Max tokens for per-prompt thread injection | `1000` |\n397\t| `mcp_servers`  | Shared MCP server references             | `[]`    |\n398\t| `skills`       | Team skill definitions                   | `[]`    |\n399\t| `conventions`  | Team coding conventions (injected)       | `[]`    |\n400\t\n401\t---\n402\t\n403\t## Requirements\n404\t\n405\t- **Git** repository (any host)\n406\t- **Node.js 16+** (for hook scripts and `npx` CLI)\n407\t- **Claude Code**, **Copilot CLI**, and/or **Codex**\n408\t\n409\t---\n410\t\n411\t## Supported Tools\n412\t\n413\t| Tool        | Hook Config                 | Integration                     |\n414\t|-------------|-----------------------------|---------------------------------|\n415\t| Claude Code | `.claude/settings.json`     | Full — all 6 hooks + transcript |\n416\t| Copilot CLI | `.github/hooks/hooks.json`  | Full — all hooks                |\n417\t| Codex       | `sticky-codex.sh` wrapper   | Post-session capture            |\n418\t\n419\tAll tools call the same JavaScript hooks and share the same data files.\n420\t\n421\t> **Third-party tools** (Cursor, Windsurf, Zed, Cline) can use the MCP server\n422\t> for full thread access without hooks. See below.\n423\t\n424\t---\n425\t\n426\t## MCP Server\n427\t\n428\tSticky Note includes an MCP server that gives AI tools direct access to\n429\tthread data, overlap detection, and environment status via standard\n430\t[Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/).\n431\t\n432\t### Auto-registration\n433\t\n434\tThe session-start hook automatically registers the MCP server in `.mcp.json`\n435\ton the first session. No manual setup needed — it's available from the second\n436\tsession onward.\n437\t\n438\t### Manual registration\n439\t\n440\tAdd to `.mcp.json` at your project root:\n441\t\n442\t```json\n443\t{\n444\t  \"mcpServers\": {\n445\t    \"sticky-note\": {\n446\t      \"type\": \"stdio\",\n447\t      \"command\": \"npx\",\n448\t      \"args\": [\"-y\", \"-p\", \"sticky-note-cli\", \"sticky-note\", \"mcp-server\"]\n449\t    }\n450\t  }\n451\t}\n452\t```\n453\t\n454\t### Tools\n455\t\n456\t| Tool | Description |\n457\t|------|-------------|\n458\t| `get_session_context(id)` | Full thread payload by ID |\n459\t| `get_stuck_threads()` | All stuck threads with failed approaches |\n460\t| `search_threads(query)` | Keyword search across threads |\n461\t| `get_audit_trail(file, user, since, ...)` | Query per-user audit logs |\n462\t| `get_presence()` | Active developers and their files |\n463\t| `check_overlaps(files)` | Detect file conflicts before editing |\n464\t| `get_environment_status()` | Environment sync status (provisioned vs missing) |\n465\t| `get_thread_context_for_files(files)` | Thread attribution for files |\n466\t\n467\t### Why MCP?\n468\t\n469\tHook output goes to the AI model's context window — the AI decides whether\n470\tto surface it. MCP tool responses are part of the AI's active reasoning,\n471\tso the data always gets processed. This makes the MCP server the most\n472\treliable channel for overlap warnings and environment notifications.\n473\t\n474\t---\n475\t\n476\t## Team Environment Sync\n477\t\n478\tShare your complete AI development environment with the team — MCP servers,\n479\tskills, agents, commands, and permissions — through git, with zero manual setup.\n480\t\n481\t### How it works\n482\t\n483\t```\n484\tTeam lead: npx sticky-note init     → commits hooks + environment/\n485\tTeammate:  git pull && start session → hook auto-provisions everything\n486\t```\n487\t\n488\tThe session-start hook is the provisioning engine. It copies skills to\n489\tplugin directories, writes MCP servers to `.mcp.json`, and merges\n490\tpermissions — all automatically when a teammate starts their AI session.\n491\t\n492\t### Directory structure\n493\t\n494\t```\n495\t.sticky-note/environment/\n496\t├── manifest.json         # MCP servers, permissions, env vars\n497\t├── skills/               # Team skill definitions (.md files)\n498\t├── agents/               # Team agent definitions (.md files)\n499\t└── commands/             # Team command definitions (.md files)\n500\t```\n501\t\n502\t### Adding team resources\n503\t\n504\t**Skills/agents/commands:** Drop a `.md` file into the appropriate directory.\n505\tIt auto-provisions on next session and auto-commits with your next commit.\n506\t\n507\t**MCP servers:** `npx sticky-note env add-server` or edit `manifest.json`.\n508\t\n509\t### Secrets handling\n510\t\n511\tMCP servers that need secrets (e.g., `${GITHUB_TOKEN}`) are skipped by\n512\tauto-provisioning. Run `npx sticky-note bootstrap` to provide credentials\n513\tinteractively — they're saved to `.env` (gitignored), never committed.\n514\t\n515\tThe MCP server's `get_environment_status()` tool tells the AI what's missing\n516\tso it can prompt the user.\n517\t\n518\t---\n519\t\n520\t## Concurrent Usage & Merge Strategy\n521\t\n522\t`npx sticky-note-cli init` adds a `.gitattributes` rule:\n523\t\n524\t```\n525\t.sticky-note/sticky-note.json merge=union\n526\t```\n527\t\n528\tThis tells git to **keep lines from both sides** instead of conflicting.\n529\tThreads have unique UUIDs, so concurrent pushes merge cleanly.\n530\t\n531\tPer-user audit logs (`audit/<username>.jsonl`) and presence files\n532\t(`presence/<username>.json`) are written by one user at a time, so they\n533\tnever conflict.\n534\t\n535\t### Branch Switching\n536\t\n537\tSticky-note data (audit logs, presence, thread state) is **branch-independent** —\n538\tit's metadata about the repo, not part of the source code. However, because\n539\tthese files are git-tracked and updated on every tool call, a raw\n540\t`git checkout` or `git switch` will fail if there are uncommitted changes.\n541\t\n542\tUse one of these approaches:\n543\t\n544\t```bash\n545\t# Recommended: sticky-note CLI wrapper\n546\tnpx sticky-note-cli switch <branch>\n547\t\n548\t# Or: git alias (set up by npx sticky-note-cli init)\n549\tgit sw <branch>\n550\t```\n551\t\n552\tBoth auto-stash `.sticky-note/` before switching and restore it after.\n553\tSee [docs/branch-switching.md](docs/branch-switching.md) for the full\n554\tdesign discussion.\n555\t\n556\t---\n557\t\n558\t## FAQ\n559\t\n560\t**Q: Does this capture my code or conversations?**\n561\tA: No. Only file paths, timestamps, usernames, and status metadata.\n562\t\n563\t**Q: What happens with merge conflicts in sticky-note.json?**\n564\tA: `merge=union` in `.gitattributes` handles most cases automatically.\n565\t\n566\t**Q: Can I close a thread manually?**\n567\tA: Edit `sticky-note.json` and change the thread's `status` to `\"closed\"`,\n568\tor run `npx sticky-note-cli gc` to tombstone expired threads.\n569\t\n570\t**Q: Does this work offline?**\n571\tA: Yes. Everything is local until you `git push`.\n572\t\n573\t**Q: How do I set up Codex?**\n574\tA: Run `npx sticky-note-cli init --codex`, then alias the wrapper:\n575\t`alias sticky-codex=\".claude/hooks/sticky-codex.sh\"`\n576\t\n577\t---\n578\t\n579\t## License\n580\t\n581\t[MIT](LICENSE) — fully open source, no restrictions.\n582\t\n583\t---\n584\t\n585\t## Contributing\n586\t\n587\tSee [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) for guidelines.\n588\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q8PJQ72XLskNAjyA7gv1i3","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","dirty_fingerprint":"bc65e878d80c1722a0adee9428cbf850f1d73e21ecfd82d0c4182876ebf93c10","head":"173cfc66bb28f4c20e474f522f197eeef0c627de","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1c3052566c2e#agent:a264be797d1ae6615","parent":"claude-code:c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1c3052566c2e","seq":9},"id":"ev1-bdd012c3bf868a710e0e0db7f30643a52c7e74f55810c93b54869aa4f32c57d0","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-19T06:35:24.892Z","producer":{"session_id":"c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1c3052566c2e","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.215","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"a264be797d1ae6615","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":true,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t<p align=\"center\">\n2\t  <!-- Replace with your own logo -->\n3\t  <img src=\"docs/images/logo.png\" alt=\"Sticky Note\" width=\"600\" />\n4\t</p>\n5\t<p align=\"center\">\n6\t  <strong>Human-to-human handoff for AI coding assistants.</strong><br/>\n7\t  Git-backed shared memory that captures session threads and surfaces<br/>\n8\t  teammate context — automatically, inside the tools you already use.\n9\t</p>\n10\t\n11\t<p align=\"center\">\n12\t  <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/sticky-note-cli\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/npm/v/sticky-note-cli.svg?style=flat-square&color=f59e0b\" alt=\"npm version\" /></a>\n13\t  <a href=\"LICENSE\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue.svg?style=flat-square\" alt=\"MIT License\" /></a>\n14\t  <a href=\"https://nodejs.org\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/node-%3E%3D16-brightgreen.svg?style=flat-square\" alt=\"Node.js >= 16\" /></a>\n15\t  <a href=\"#supported-tools\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/works%20with-Claude%20%C2%B7%20Copilot%20%C2%B7%20Codex-8b5cf6?style=flat-square\" alt=\"Works with Claude, Copilot, Codex\" /></a>\n16\t</p>\n17\t\n18\t<br/>\n19\t\n20\t<p align=\"center\">\n21\t  <img src=\"docs/images/demo.gif\" alt=\"Sticky Note demo\" width=\"600\" />\n22\t</p>\n23\t\n24\t<p align=\"center\">\n25\t  <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/BandaruDheeraj/sticky-note/main/docs/images/architecture.svg\" alt=\"Architecture diagram\" width=\"800\" />\n26\t</p>\n27\t\n28\t<br/>\n29\t\n30\t> [!NOTE]\n31\t> **Sticky Note is evolving.** Features, APIs, and file formats may change as we learn what works best for teams.\n32\t\n33\t---\n34\t\n35\t## The Problem\n36\t\n37\tWhen multiple developers on a team use AI coding assistants, each assistant operates in its own bubble. Your agent doesn't know that your teammate's agent just spent 45 minutes debugging token rotation in the same file you're about to refactor. It doesn't know what they tried and abandoned. It doesn't know they're stuck.\n38\t\n39\tThe result: duplicated effort, conflicting changes, and context that evaporates the moment a session ends.\n40\t\n41\tThere are plenty of agent-to-agent orchestration frameworks out there, but that's not how most teams actually work. We work with other people — people who also happen to use agents. We needed a way for those agents to share context without changing how anyone works.\n42\t\n43\tSo we built Sticky Note.\n44\t\n45\t## The Solution\n46\t\n47\tSticky Note captures what happened in each AI session (files touched, status,\n48\tnarrative, failed approaches) and surfaces it to teammates automatically on\n49\ttheir next session by relevance.\n50\t\n51\t---\n52\t\n53\t## What's New in V2.7\n54\t\n55\t- **Team Environment Sync**: Share your complete AI dev environment through git. Skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, and permissions auto-provision when teammates start a session — zero manual setup\n56\t- **`bootstrap` command**: Interactive secrets provisioning for MCP servers that need credentials. Generates `.env.example` for the team\n57\t- **`env` commands**: `env status` shows provisioning state, `env add-server` adds MCP servers interactively\n58\t- **Dual-target provisioning**: Skills deployed to both Claude Code plugins and Copilot CLI extensions automatically\n59\t\n60\t### What's New in V2.6\n61\t\n62\t- **MCP server**: 8-tool MCP server for reliable AI-to-sticky-note communication. Auto-registers in `.mcp.json` on first session. Works with Claude Code, Copilot CLI, and any MCP-compatible client\n63\t- **Styled overlap banners**: Overlap warnings now use structured box-drawing format with 🔴/🟡 status indicators, ANSI-colored stderr output\n64\t- **Overlap detection**: Warns when you're working on files another teammate has open/stuck threads on. Three delivery channels: MCP tool, injected context, and preToolUse deny\n65\t- **File claiming**: `npx sticky-note-cli claim src/auth.ts \"refactoring auth flow\"` — declare intent to work on files\n66\t- **Auto-close Copilot CLI threads**: Threads from Copilot CLI sessions auto-close after configurable inactivity (default 24h), since Copilot CLI has no session-end signal\n67\t- **PID-keyed session isolation**: Concurrent Copilot CLI sessions get independent overlap dedup via `COPILOT_LOADER_PID`\n68\t\n69\t### What's New in V2.5\n70\t\n71\t- **Smart injection (two-tier)**: Stuck threads injected eagerly at session start; all other threads injected lazily when you first touch a file they authored — via built-in git blame attribution\n72\t- **Thread resume (local)**: `npx sticky-note-cli resume-thread --query \"auth fix\" --user alice` — natural language thread discovery with text similarity + file attribution ranking\n73\t- **Built-in attribution engine**: `npx sticky-note-cli get-line-attribution --file src/auth.ts` — maps file lines to commit SHAs via git blame, resolves to threads with line ranges. No external dependencies.\n74\t- **Git Notes storage**: Session attribution stored in `refs/notes/sticky-note` — survives rebase/amend\n75\t- **Three-tier SHA resolution**: Git Notes → Audit JSONL → File+date heuristic — attribution never breaks\n76\t- **PreToolUse hook**: New hook fires before each tool call for lazy injection with line-range detail\n77\t- **Thread schema additions**: `contributors[]`, `resumed_by`, `resumed_at`, `resume_history[]` — multi-contributor threads with full resume chain\n78\t\n79\t### What's New in V2\n80\t\n81\t- **Per-user audit & presence**: Audit logs and presence in per-user files under `audit/` and `presence/` — committed and shared with the team\n82\t- **Relevance scoring**: Context injected based on file overlap, branch match, and recency\n83\t- **Richer threads**: Narrative summaries, failed approaches, work type, activities\n84\t- **Tombstone expiry**: Old threads are automatically cleaned up via `gc`\n85\t- **Presence tracking**: See who's currently active with `npx sticky-note-cli who`\n86\t- **Codex support**: Wrapper script for post-session capture\n87\t- **Separate config**: Team settings in `sticky-note-config.json`\n88\t\n89\t---\n90\t\n91\t## Quick Start\n92\t\n93\t### 1. Install\n94\t\n95\t```bash\n96\tnpx sticky-note-cli init\n97\t```\n98\t\n99\tThis runs an interactive setup that:\n100\t- [OK] Checks for git and Node.js 16+\n101\t- 📋 Asks for team config (MCP servers, conventions, stale days)\n102\t- 📁 Creates all hook scripts and config files\n103\t\n104\t### 2. Commit\n105\t\n106\t```bash\n107\tgit add .claude .github .sticky-note .gitignore .gitattributes CLAUDE.md\n108\tgit commit -m \"feat: add sticky-note hooks\"\n109\t```\n110\t\n111\t### 3. Push & Pull\n112\t\n113\t```bash\n114\tgit push        # Share with team\n115\tgit pull        # Teammates — no additional setup needed\n116\t```\n117\t\n118\t### 4. Work\n119\t\n120\tOpen Claude Code or Copilot CLI and start working. Sticky Note runs\n121\tin the background via hooks — capturing threads and surfacing context.\n122\t\n123\t**First thing to try** — ask your AI agent:\n124\t\n125\t> \"Show me the active sticky note threads\"\n126\t\n127\t---\n128\t\n129\t## How Context Gets Injected\n130\t\n131\tSticky Note injects context through **four mechanisms**: two that run\n132\tautomatically via hooks, one triggered manually via the CLI, and one\n133\tthat's always available as a static file.\n134\t\n135\t### 1. Static Instruction Files (always available)\n136\t\n137\t`npx sticky-note-cli init` deploys AI instruction files that teach each tool\n138\thow to interact with Sticky Note:\n139\t\n140\t| File | Consumed by | Installed to |\n141\t|------|-------------|--------------|\n142\t| `CLAUDE.md` | Claude Code | repo root |\n143\t| `copilot-instructions.md` | Copilot CLI | `.github/` |\n144\t\n145\tThese files contain thread field references, status icons, resume\n146\tinstructions, display formats, and query examples. They're wrapped in\n147\t`<!-- sticky-note:start/end -->` markers so `npx sticky-note-cli update` can\n148\trefresh them without overwriting your own content.\n149\t\n150\t### 2. Session Start Hook (`session-start.js`)\n151\t\n152\tRuns once when a session begins. V2.5 uses **eager injection** for stuck\n153\tthreads only — non-stuck threads are held for lazy injection.\n154\t\n155\t- **Resumed thread** — If a `.sticky-resume` signal file exists, the full\n156\t  thread payload is injected: narrative, files touched, failed approaches,\n157\t  conversation prompts, and the complete resume chain history.\n158\t- **Stuck threads** — All stuck threads are injected eagerly (V2.5).\n159\t- **Team config** — Conventions, MCP servers, and skills from\n160\t  `sticky-note-config.json`.\n161\t- **Active presence** — Developers seen in the last hour and the\n162\t  files they're working on.\n163\t\n164\tThe hook also clears the injected-this-session tracking set, snapshots\n165\t`HEAD`, generates a session ID, and ages stale threads.\n166\t\n167\t### 3. Per-Prompt Injection (`inject-context.js`)\n168\t\n169\tRuns on **every user prompt**. Scores all live threads by relevance and\n170\tinjects the top 3–5 (under token budget) as additional context.\n171\t**V2.5:** Skips threads already injected by session-start or PreToolUse.\n172\t\n173\t| Signal | Weight | Description |\n174\t|--------|--------|-------------|\n175\t| File overlap | 3 per file (max 5) | Thread files match your recent git changes |\n176\t| Branch match | 2 | Thread is on your current branch |\n177\t| Recency | 2 | Decays 0.2 per day from last activity |\n178\t| Stuck status | +2 | Boost for threads marked stuck |\n179\t| Prompt keywords | 1 | File names mentioned in your prompt |\n180\t| Same developer | 1 | Your own previous threads |\n181\t| Resume signal | +10 | Thread targeted by `.sticky-resume` |\n182\t\n183\t### 3b. PreToolUse Hook (`pre-tool-use.js`) — V2.5\n184\t\n185\tRuns **before each tool call**. Lazy injection tier:\n186\t\n187\t1. Extracts target file from tool input\n188\t2. Runs `git blame --line-porcelain <file>` → commit SHAs per line\n189\t3. Three-tier SHA resolution: Git Notes → Audit JSONL → File+date heuristic\n190\t4. Resolves session IDs → loads threads with line ranges\n191\t5. Injects matching threads (if not already injected this session)\n192\t\n193\tNo external dependencies — uses built-in git blame.\n194\t\n195\t### 4. Resume Flow (`npx sticky-note-cli resume <id>`)\n196\t\n197\tA manual trigger that enables **cross-tool handoff** (e.g., Claude Code →\n198\tCopilot CLI):\n199\t\n200\t```\n201\tnpx sticky-note-cli resume <thread-id>\n202\t```\n203\t\n204\t1. Writes the thread UUID to a `.sticky-resume` signal file.\n205\t2. Outputs the thread's full context to the terminal (narrative, files,\n206\t   failed approaches, conversation prompts).\n207\t3. On the next session start, `session-start.js` detects the signal,\n208\t   reopens the thread as `open`, and injects the complete payload.\n209\t4. `inject-context.js` gives the resumed thread a +10 score boost on\n210\t   every prompt for the duration of the session.\n211\t5. `session-end.js` clears the signal file and updates the thread's\n212\t   `resume_chain` with the new session.\n213\t\n214\tThis means a thread started in Claude Code can be resumed in Copilot CLI\n215\t(or vice versa) with full context preserved — including the original\n216\tconversation prompts.\n217\t\n218\t---\n219\t\n220\t## How Context Gets Collected\n221\t\n222\tThe injection hooks above are fed by four **collection hooks** that run\n223\tsilently in the background:\n224\t\n225\t### Tool Tracking (`track-work.js`)\n226\t\n227\tRuns after every tool use. Appends a JSONL audit entry with the tool\n228\tname, file path, and session ID. Also updates the user's presence file\n229\t(`presence/<username>.json`) so `session-start.js` can show who's active.\n230\t\n231\t### Session End (`session-end.js`)\n232\t\n233\tRuns when a session ends. Captures the full thread record:\n234\t\n235\t- **Files touched** — from audit trail, transcript parsing, and git diff\n236\t  against the HEAD snapshot from session start.\n237\t- **Narrative** — last assistant message (300 char max).\n238\t- **Work type** — inferred from activity patterns (bug-fix, feature,\n239\t  debugging, testing, documentation, etc.).\n240\t- **Failed approaches** — extracted where error + retry patterns both match.\n241\t- **Prompts** — stored for cross-tool resume (up to 20, 300 chars each).\n242\t- **Tool calls** — counts per tool (Edit, Read, Write, etc.).\n243\t\n244\tAlso runs a lazy tombstone sweep: closed threads older than `stale_days`\n245\tare expired to minimal footprint.\n246\t\n247\t### Error Capture (`on-error.js`)\n248\t\n249\tRuns when a tool execution fails. Creates or updates the session thread\n250\twith status `stuck` and appends the error to `failed_approaches`. Next\n251\tsession's `inject-context.js` ranks stuck threads higher so teammates\n252\tsee them.\n253\t\n254\t### Stop Handler (`on-stop.js`, Claude Code only)\n255\t\n256\tRuns when the user stops a session. Builds a structured handoff summary\n257\t(what was done, what failed, current status, next steps) and saves it to\n258\tthe thread's `handoff_summary` field.\n259\t\n260\t---\n261\t\n262\t## What Gets Captured\n263\t\n264\t| Data              | Captured | Example                              |\n265\t|-------------------|----------|--------------------------------------|\n266\t| Files touched     | [OK]        | `src/auth.ts`, `lib/db.py`          |\n267\t| Thread status     | [OK]        | open, stuck, stale, closed, expired  |\n268\t| Author            | [OK]        | OS username                          |\n269\t| Timestamp         | [OK]        | ISO 8601                             |\n270\t| Narrative         | [OK]        | \"Fixed auth token refresh flow\"      |\n271\t| Failed approaches | [OK]        | What was tried, errors, files        |\n272\t| Work type         | [OK]        | bug-fix, feature, debugging, etc.    |\n273\t| Code content      | [ERR]        | Never captured                       |\n274\t| Conversation      | [ERR]        | Never captured                       |\n275\t| Credentials       | [ERR]        | Never captured                       |\n276\t\n277\t---\n278\t\n279\t## Thread Lifecycle\n280\t\n281\t```\n282\topen → stale  (auto, after stale_days with no activity)\n283\topen → closed (on session end)\n284\tstuck → closed (on session end or manual)\n285\tclosed → open  (via `npx sticky-note-cli resume <id>`)\n286\tclosed → expired (auto, tombstoned by gc after stale_days)\n287\t```\n288\t\n289\tExpired threads keep only their ID, status, user, and closed timestamp.\n290\t\n291\t---\n292\t\n293\t## File Structure\n294\t\n295\t```\n296\tCLAUDE.md                         # AI instructions for Claude Code\n297\t\n298\t.claude/\n299\t├── settings.json             # Claude Code hook config\n300\t└── hooks/\n301\t    ├── sticky-utils.js       # Shared utilities\n302\t    ├── session-start.js      # Load & inject teammate context\n303\t    ├── session-end.js        # Capture session thread\n304\t    ├── inject-context.js     # Per-prompt relevance scoring\n305\t    ├── pre-tool-use.js       # Lazy injection via git blame attribution (V2.5)\n306\t    ├── sticky-attribution.js # Built-in attribution engine (V2.5)\n307\t    ├── sticky-git-notes.js   # Git Notes utilities (V2.5)\n308\t    ├── track-work.js         # JSONL audit + presence + line tracking (V2.5)\n309\t    ├── parse-transcript.js   # Narrative + failed approach extraction\n310\t    ├── on-stop.js            # Handoff summary on stop\n311\t    ├── on-error.js           # Stuck thread on error\n312\t    ├── post-rewrite.js       # Git Notes rewrite survival (V2.5)\n313\t    └── sticky-codex.sh       # Optional Codex wrapper\n314\t\n315\t.github/\n316\t├── copilot-instructions.md   # AI instructions for Copilot CLI\n317\t└── hooks/\n318\t    └── hooks.json            # Copilot CLI hook config\n319\t\n320\t.sticky-note/\n321\t├── sticky-note.json          # Shared threads (git-tracked)\n322\t├── sticky-note-config.json   # Team config (git-tracked)\n323\t├── audit/                    # Per-user audit logs (git-tracked)\n324\t│   └── <username>.jsonl      # One file per team member\n325\t├── presence/                 # Per-user presence (git-tracked)\n326\t│   └── <username>.json       # One file per team member\n327\t├── .sticky-resume            # Resume signal (local only)\n328\t├── .sticky-injected          # Injection tracking (local only, V2.5)\n329\t└── .sticky-active-resume     # Active resume marker (local only, V2.5)\n330\t```\n331\t\n332\t---\n333\t\n334\t## CLI Commands\n335\t\n336\t```bash\n337\tnpx sticky-note-cli init           # Interactive setup\n338\tnpx sticky-note-cli init --codex   # Setup with Codex wrapper\n339\tnpx sticky-note-cli update         # Update hook scripts (preserves data)\n340\tnpx sticky-note-cli status         # Diagnostic report (includes attribution health)\n341\tnpx sticky-note-cli threads        # List threads with status icons\n342\tnpx sticky-note-cli resume         # List resumable threads\n343\tnpx sticky-note-cli resume <id>    # Resume a previous thread\n344\tnpx sticky-note-cli resume --clear # Cancel active resume\n345\tnpx sticky-note-cli resume-thread  # Smart resume: --query, --user, --file (V2.5)\n346\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit          # Query merged audit trail (all users)\n347\tnpx sticky-note-cli who            # Show active and recent team members\n348\tnpx sticky-note-cli overlap        # Detect file overlaps with teammates (V2.6)\n349\tnpx sticky-note-cli claim <files>  # Declare intent to work on files (V2.6)\n350\tnpx sticky-note-cli switch <branch> # Safe branch switch (auto-stashes data)\n351\tnpx sticky-note-cli gc             # Tombstone expired threads\n352\tnpx sticky-note-cli reset          # Wipe all threads (--force, --keep-audit)\n353\tnpx sticky-note-cli get-line-attribution # File→thread attribution with line ranges (V2.5)\n354\tnpx sticky-note-cli checkpoint         # Set work-topic checkpoint for attribution (V2.5)\n355\tnpx sticky-note-cli sync               # Commit .sticky-note/ changes (--push to also push)\n356\tnpx sticky-note-cli bootstrap         # Provision MCP servers that need secrets\n357\tnpx sticky-note-cli env status         # Show environment provisioning status\n358\tnpx sticky-note-cli env add-server     # Add MCP server to team environment\n359\tnpx sticky-note mcp-server             # Launch MCP server (stdio JSON-RPC)\n360\tnpx sticky-note-cli --version      # Show version\n361\tnpx sticky-note-cli --help         # Show help\n362\t```\n363\t\n364\t### Audit Filters\n365\t\n366\t```bash\n367\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit --user alice\n368\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit --file src/auth.ts\n369\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit --since 2025-01-01\n370\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit --session abc-123\n371\tnpx sticky-note-cli audit --limit 100\n372\t```\n373\t\n374\t---\n375\t\n376\t## Configuration\n377\t\n378\tEdit `.sticky-note/sticky-note-config.json`:\n379\t\n380\t```json\n381\t{\n382\t  \"stale_days\": 14,\n383\t  \"copilot_cli_auto_close_hours\": 24,\n384\t  \"inject_token_budget\": 1000,\n385\t  \"mcp_servers\": [],\n386\t  \"skills\": [],\n387\t  \"conventions\": [\"Use TypeScript strict mode\", \"Test before commit\"],\n388\t  \"hook_version\": \"2.6.12\"\n389\t}\n390\t```\n391\t\n392\t| Key            | Description                              | Default |\n393\t|----------------|------------------------------------------|---------|\n394\t| `stale_days`   | Days before threads expire + gc cleanup  | `14`    |\n395\t| `copilot_cli_auto_close_hours` | Hours before idle Copilot CLI threads auto-close | `24` |\n396\t| `inject_token_budget` | Max tokens for per-prompt thread injection | `1000` |\n397\t| `mcp_servers`  | Shared MCP server references             | `[]`    |\n398\t| `skills`       | Team skill definitions                   | `[]`    |\n399\t| `conventions`  | Team coding conventions (injected)       | `[]`    |\n400\t\n401\t---\n402\t\n403\t## Requirements\n404\t\n405\t- **Git** repository (any host)\n406\t- **Node.js 16+** (for hook scripts and `npx` CLI)\n407\t- **Claude Code**, **Copilot CLI**, and/or **Codex**\n408\t\n409\t---\n410\t\n411\t## Supported Tools\n412\t\n413\t| Tool        | Hook Config                 | Integration                     |\n414\t|-------------|-----------------------------|---------------------------------|\n415\t| Claude Code | `.claude/settings.json`     | Full — all 6 hooks + transcript |\n416\t| Copilot CLI | `.github/hooks/hooks.json`  | Full — all hooks                |\n417\t| Codex       | `sticky-codex.sh` wrapper   | Post-session capture            |\n418\t\n419\tAll tools call the same JavaScript hooks and share the same data files.\n420\t\n421\t> **Third-party tools** (Cursor, Windsurf, Zed, Cline) can use the MCP server\n422\t> for full thread access without hooks. See below.\n423\t\n424\t---\n425\t\n426\t## MCP Server\n427\t\n428\tSticky Note includes an MCP server that gives AI tools direct access to\n429\tthread data, overlap detection, and environment status via standard\n430\t[Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/).\n431\t\n432\t### Auto-registration\n433\t\n434\tThe session-start hook automatically registers the MCP server in `.mcp.json`\n435\ton the first session. No manual setup needed — it's available from the second\n436\tsession onward.\n437\t\n438\t### Manual registration\n439\t\n440\tAdd to `.mcp.json` at your project root:\n441\t\n442\t```json\n443\t{\n444\t  \"mcpServers\": {\n445\t    \"sticky-note\": {\n446\t      \"type\": \"stdio\",\n447\t      \"command\": \"npx\",\n448\t      \"args\": [\"-y\", \"-p\", \"sticky-note-cli\", \"sticky-note\", \"mcp-server\"]\n449\t    }\n450\t  }\n451\t}\n452\t```\n453\t\n454\t### Tools\n455\t\n456\t| Tool | Description |\n457\t|------|-------------|\n458\t| `get_session_context(id)` | Full thread payload by ID |\n459\t| `get_stuck_threads()` | All stuck threads with failed approaches |\n460\t| `search_threads(query)` | Keyword search across threads |\n461\t| `get_audit_trail(file, user, since, ...)` | Query per-user audit logs |\n462\t| `get_presence()` | Active developers and their files |\n463\t| `check_overlaps(files)` | Detect file conflicts before editing |\n464\t| `get_environment_status()` | Environment sync status (provisioned vs missing) |\n465\t| `get_thread_context_for_files(files)` | Thread attribution for files |\n466\t\n467\t### Why MCP?\n468\t\n469\tHook output goes to the AI model's context window — the AI decides whether\n470\tto surface it. MCP tool responses are part of the AI's active reasoning,\n471\tso the data always gets processed. This makes the MCP server the most\n472\treliable channel for overlap warnings and environment notifications.\n473\t\n474\t---\n475\t\n476\t## Team Environment Sync\n477\t\n478\tShare your complete AI development environment with the team — MCP servers,\n479\tskills, agents, commands, and permissions — through git, with zero manual setup.\n480\t\n481\t### How it works\n482\t\n483\t```\n484\tTeam lead: npx sticky-note init     → commits hooks + environment/\n485\tTeammate:  git pull && start session → hook auto-provisions everything\n486\t```\n487\t\n488\tThe session-start hook is the provisioning engine. It copies skills to\n489\tplugin directories, writes MCP servers to `.mcp.json`, and merges\n490\tpermissions — all automatically when a teammate starts their AI session.\n491\t\n492\t### Directory structure\n493\t\n494\t```\n495\t.sticky-note/environment/\n496\t├── manifest.json         # MCP servers, permissions, env vars\n497\t├── skills/               # Team skill definitions (.md files)\n498\t├── agents/               # Team agent definitions (.md files)\n499\t└── commands/             # Team command definitions (.md files)\n500\t```\n501\t\n502\t### Adding team resources\n503\t\n504\t**Skills/agents/commands:** Drop a `.md` file into the appropriate directory.\n505\tIt auto-provisions on next session and auto-commits with your next commit.\n506\t\n507\t**MCP servers:** `npx sticky-note env add-server` or edit `manifest.json`.\n508\t\n509\t### Secrets handling\n510\t\n511\tMCP servers that need secrets (e.g., `${GITHUB_TOKEN}`) are skipped by\n512\tauto-provisioning. Run `npx sticky-note bootstrap` to provide credentials\n513\tinteractively — they're saved to `.env` (gitignored), never committed.\n514\t\n515\tThe MCP server's `get_environment_status()` tool tells the AI what's missing\n516\tso it can prompt the user.\n517\t\n518\t---\n519\t\n520\t## Concurrent Usage & Merge Strategy\n521\t\n522\t`npx sticky-note-cli init` adds a `.gitattributes` rule:\n523\t\n524\t```\n525\t.sticky-note/sticky-note.json merge=union\n526\t```\n527\t\n528\tThis tells git to **keep lines from both sides** instead of conflicting.\n529\tThreads have unique UUIDs, so concurrent pushes merge cleanly.\n530\t\n531\tPer-user audit logs (`audit/<username>.jsonl`) and presence files\n532\t(`presence/<username>.json`) are written by one user at a time, so they\n533\tnever conflict.\n534\t\n535\t### Branch Switching\n536\t\n537\tSticky-note data (audit logs, presence, thread state) is **branch-independent** —\n538\tit's metadata about the repo, not part of the source code. However, because\n539\tthese files are git-tracked and updated on every tool call, a raw\n540\t`git checkout` or `git switch` will fail if there are uncommitted changes.\n541\t\n542\tUse one of these approaches:\n543\t\n544\t```bash\n545\t# Recommended: sticky-note CLI wrapper\n546\tnpx sticky-note-cli switch <branch>\n547\t\n548\t# Or: git alias (set up by npx sticky-note-cli init)\n549\tgit sw <branch>\n550\t```\n551\t\n552\tBoth auto-stash `.sticky-note/` before switching and restore it after.\n553\tSee [docs/branch-switching.md](docs/branch-switching.md) for the full\n554\tdesign discussion.\n555\t\n556\t---\n557\t\n558\t## FAQ\n559\t\n560\t**Q: Does this capture my code or conversations?**\n561\tA: No. Only file paths, timestamps, usernames, and status metadata.\n562\t\n563\t**Q: What happens with merge conflicts in sticky-note.json?**\n564\tA: `merge=union` in `.gitattributes` handles most cases automatically.\n565\t\n566\t**Q: Can I close a thread manually?**\n567\tA: Edit `sticky-note.json` and change the thread's `status` to `\"closed\"`,\n568\tor run `npx sticky-note-cli gc` to tombstone expired threads.\n569\t\n570\t**Q: Does this work offline?**\n571\tA: Yes. Everything is local until you `git push`.\n572\t\n573\t**Q: How do I set up Codex?**\n574\tA: Run `npx sticky-note-cli init --codex`, then alias the wrapper:\n575\t`alias sticky-codex=\".claude/hooks/sticky-codex.sh\"`\n576\t\n577\t---\n578\t\n579\t## License\n580\t\n581\t[MIT](LICENSE) — fully open source, no restrictions.\n582\t\n583\t---\n584\t\n585\t## Contributing\n586\t\n587\tSee [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) for guidelines.\n588\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q8PJQ72XLskNAjyA7gv1i3","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"b584f8ec-3ebd-41eb-86f9-e33c07f8a3cb","promptId":"07367244-5cb8-4fc2-b615-0918c8d4acee","sessionId":"c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1c3052566c2e","slug":"generic-stirring-candy","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b584f8ec-3ebd-41eb-86f9-e33c07f8a3cb","timestamp":"2026-07-19T06:35:24.892Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"<p align=\"center\">\n  <!-- Replace with your own logo -->\n  <img src=\"docs/images/logo.png\" alt=\"Sticky Note\" width=\"600\" />\n</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n  <strong>Human-to-human handoff for AI coding assistants.</strong><br/>\n  Git-backed shared memory that captures session threads and surfaces<br/>\n  teammate context — automatically, inside the tools you already use.\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n  <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/sticky-note-cli\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/npm/v/sticky-note-cli.svg?style=flat-square&color=f59e0b\" alt=\"npm version\" /></a>\n  <a href=\"LICENSE\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue.svg?style=flat-square\" alt=\"MIT License\" /></a>\n  <a href=\"https://nodejs.org\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/node-%3E%3D16-brightgreen.svg?style=flat-square\" alt=\"Node.js >= 16\" /></a>\n  <a href=\"#supported-tools\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/works%20with-Claude%20%C2%B7%20Copilot%20%C2%B7%20Codex-8b5cf6?style=flat-square\" alt=\"Works with Claude, Copilot, Codex\" /></a>\n</p>\n\n<br/>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n  <img src=\"docs/images/demo.gif\" alt=\"Sticky Note demo\" width=\"600\" />\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n  <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/BandaruDheeraj/sticky-note/main/docs/images/architecture.svg\" alt=\"Architecture diagram\" width=\"800\" />\n</p>\n\n<br/>\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Sticky Note is evolving.** Features, APIs, and file formats may change as we learn what works best for teams.\n\n---\n\n## The Problem\n\nWhen multiple developers on a team use AI coding assistants, each assistant operates in its own bubble. Your agent doesn't know that your teammate's agent just spent 45 minutes debugging token rotation in the same file you're about to refactor. It doesn't know what they tried and abandoned. It doesn't know they're stuck.\n\nThe result: duplicated effort, conflicting changes, and context that evaporates the moment a session ends.\n\nThere are plenty of agent-to-agent orchestration frameworks out there, but that's not how most teams actually work. We work with other people — people who also happen to use agents. We needed a way for those agents to share context without changing how anyone works.\n\nSo we built Sticky Note.\n\n## The Solution\n\nSticky Note captures what happened in each AI session (files touched, status,\nnarrative, failed approaches) and surfaces it to teammates automatically on\ntheir next session by relevance.\n\n---\n\n## What's New in V2.7\n\n- **Team Environment Sync**: Share your complete AI dev environment through git. Skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, and permissions auto-provision when teammates start a session — zero manual setup\n- **`bootstrap` command**: Interactive secrets provisioning for MCP servers that need credentials. Generates `.env.example` for the team\n- **`env` commands**: `env status` shows provisioning state, `env add-server` adds MCP servers interactively\n- **Dual-target provisioning**: Skills deployed to both Claude Code plugins and Copilot CLI extensions automatically\n\n### What's New in V2.6\n\n- **MCP server**: 8-tool MCP server for reliable AI-to-sticky-note communication. Auto-registers in `.mcp.json` on first session. Works with Claude Code, Copilot CLI, and any MCP-compatible client\n- **Styled overlap banners**: Overlap warnings now use structured box-drawing format with 🔴/🟡 status indicators, ANSI-colored stderr output\n- **Overlap detection**: Warns when you're working on files another teammate has open/stuck threads on. Three delivery channels: MCP tool, injected context, and preToolUse deny\n- **File claiming**: `npx sticky-note-cli claim src/auth.ts \"refactoring auth flow\"` — declare intent to work on files\n- **Auto-close Copilot CLI threads**: Threads from Copilot CLI sessions auto-close after configurable inactivity (default 24h), since Copilot CLI has no session-end signal\n- **PID-keyed session isolation**: Concurrent Copilot CLI sessions get independent overlap dedup via `COPILOT_LOADER_PID`\n\n### What's New in V2.5\n\n- **Smart injection (two-tier)**: Stuck threads injected eagerly at session start; all other threads injected lazily when you first touch a file they authored — via built-in git blame attribution\n- **Thread resume (local)**: `npx sticky-note-cli resume-thread --query \"auth fix\" --user alice` — natural language thread discovery with text similarity + file attribution ranking\n- **Built-in attribution engine**: `npx sticky-note-cli get-line-attribution --file src/auth.ts` — maps file lines to commit SHAs via git blame, resolves to threads with line ranges. No external dependencies.\n- **Git Notes storage**: Session attribution stored in `refs/notes/sticky-note` — survives rebase/amend\n- **Three-tier SHA resolution**: Git Notes → Audit JSONL → File+date heuristic — attribution never breaks\n- **PreToolUse hook**: New hook fires before each tool call for lazy injection with line-range detail\n- **Thread schema additions**: `contributors[]`, `resumed_by`, `resumed_at`, `resume_history[]` — multi-contributor threads with full resume chain\n\n### What's New in V2\n\n- **Per-user audit & presence**: Audit logs and presence in per-user files under `audit/` and `presence/` — committed and shared with the team\n- **Relevance scoring**: Context injected based on file overlap, branch match, and recency\n- **Richer threads**: Narrative summaries, failed approaches, work type, activities\n- **Tombstone expiry**: Old threads are automatically cleaned up via `gc`\n- **Presence tracking**: See who's currently active with `npx sticky-note-cli who`\n- **Codex support**: Wrapper script for post-session capture\n- **Separate config**: Team settings in `sticky-note-config.json`\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\n### 1. Install\n\n```bash\nnpx sticky-note-cli init\n```\n\nThis runs an interactive setup that:\n- [OK] Checks for git and Node.js 16+\n- 📋 Asks for team config (MCP servers, conventions, stale days)\n- 📁 Creates all hook scripts and config files\n\n### 2. Commit\n\n```bash\ngit add .claude .github .sticky-note .gitignore .gitattributes CLAUDE.md\ngit commit -m \"feat: add sticky-note hooks\"\n```\n\n### 3. Push & Pull\n\n```bash\ngit push        # Share with team\ngit pull        # Teammates — no additional setup needed\n```\n\n### 4. Work\n\nOpen Claude Code or Copilot CLI and start working. Sticky Note runs\nin the background via hooks — capturing threads and surfacing context.\n\n**First thing to try** — ask your AI agent:\n\n> \"Show me the active sticky note threads\"\n\n---\n\n## How Context Gets Injected\n\nSticky Note injects context through **four mechanisms**: two that run\nautomatically via hooks, one triggered manually via the CLI, and one\nthat's always available as a static file.\n\n### 1. Static Instruction Files (always available)\n\n`npx sticky-note-cli init` deploys AI instruction files that teach each tool\nhow to interact with Sticky Note:\n\n| File | Consumed by | Installed to |\n|------|-------------|--------------|\n| `CLAUDE.md` | Claude Code | repo root |\n| `copilot-instructions.md` | Copilot CLI | `.github/` |\n\nThese files contain thread field references, status icons, resume\ninstructions, display formats, and query examples. They're wrapped in\n`<!-- sticky-note:start/end -->` markers so `npx sticky-note-cli update` can\nrefresh them without overwriting your own content.\n\n### 2. Session Start Hook (`session-start.js`)\n\nRuns once when a session begins. V2.5 uses **eager injection** for stuck\nthreads only — non-stuck threads are held for lazy injection.\n\n- **Resumed thread** — If a `.sticky-resume` signal file exists, the full\n  thread payload is injected: narrative, files touched, failed approaches,\n  conversation prompts, and the complete resume chain history.\n- **Stuck threads** — All stuck threads are injected eagerly (V2.5).\n- **Team config** — Conventions, MCP servers, and skills from\n  `sticky-note-config.json`.\n- **Active presence** — Developers seen in the last hour and the\n  files they're working on.\n\nThe hook also clears the injected-this-session tracking set, snapshots\n`HEAD`, generates a session ID, and ages stale threads.\n\n### 3. Per-Prompt Injection (`inject-context.js`)\n\nRuns on **every user prompt**. Scores all live threads by relevance and\ninjects the top 3–5 (under token budget) as additional context.\n**V2.5:** Skips threads already injected by session-start or PreToolUse.\n\n| Signal | Weight | Description |\n|--------|--------|-------------|\n| File overlap | 3 per file (max 5) | Thread files match your recent git changes |\n| Branch match | 2 | Thread is on your current branch |\n| Recency | 2 | Decays 0.2 per day from last activity |\n| Stuck status | +2 | Boost for threads marked stuck |\n| Prompt keywords | 1 | File names mentioned in your prompt |\n| Same developer | 1 | Your own previous threads |\n| Resume signal | +10 | Thread targeted by `.sticky-resume` |\n\n### 3b. PreToolUse Hook (`pre-tool-use.js`) — V2.5\n\nRuns **before each tool call**. Lazy injection tier:\n\n1. Extracts target file from tool input\n2. Runs `git blame --line-porcelain <file>` → commit SHAs per line\n3. Three-tier SHA resolution: Git Notes → Audit JSONL → File+date heuristic\n4. Resolves session IDs → loads threads with line ranges\n5. Injects matching threads (if not already injected this session)\n\nNo external dependencies — uses built-in git blame.\n\n### 4. Resume Flow (`npx sticky-note-cli resume <id>`)\n\nA manual trigger that enables **cross-tool handoff** (e.g., Claude Code →\nCopilot CLI):\n\n```\nnpx sticky-note-cli resume <thread-id>\n```\n\n1. Writes the thread UUID to a `.sticky-resume` signal file.\n2. Outputs the thread's full context to the terminal (narrative, files,\n   failed approaches, conversation prompts).\n3. On the next session start, `session-start.js` detects the signal,\n   reopens the thread as `open`, and injects the complete payload.\n4. `inject-context.js` gives the resumed thread a +10 score boost on\n   every prompt for the duration of the session.\n5. `session-end.js` clears the signal file and updates the thread's\n   `resume_chain` with the new session.\n\nThis means a thread started in Claude Code can be resumed in Copilot CLI\n(or vice versa) with full context preserved — including the original\nconversation prompts.\n\n---\n\n## How Context Gets Collected\n\nThe injection hooks above are fed by four **collection hooks** that run\nsilently in the background:\n\n### Tool Tracking (`track-work.js`)\n\nRuns after every tool use. Appends a JSONL audit entry with the tool\nname, file path, and session ID. Also updates the user's presence file\n(`presence/<username>.json`) so `session-start.js` can show who's active.\n\n### Session End (`session-end.js`)\n\nRuns when a session ends. Captures the full thread record:\n\n- **Files touched** — from audit trail, transcript parsing, and git diff\n  against the HEAD snapshot from session start.\n- **Narrative** — last assistant message (300 char max).\n- **Work type** — inferred from activity patterns (bug-fix, feature,\n  debugging, testing, documentation, etc.).\n- **Failed approaches** — extracted where error + retry patterns both match.\n- **Prompts** — stored for cross-tool resume (up to 20, 300 chars each).\n- **Tool calls** — counts per tool (Edit, Read, Write, etc.).\n\nAlso runs a lazy tombstone sweep: closed threads older than `stale_days`\nare expired to minimal footprint.\n\n### Error Capture (`on-error.js`)\n\nRuns when a tool execution fails. Creates or updates the session thread\nwith status `stuck` and appends the error to `failed_approaches`. Next\nsession's `inject-context.js` ranks stuck threads higher so teammates\nsee them.\n\n### Stop Handler (`on-stop.js`, Claude Code only)\n\nRuns when the user stops a session. Builds a structured handoff summary\n(what was done, what failed, current status, next steps) and saves it to\nthe thread's `handoff_summary` field.\n\n---\n\n## What Gets Captured\n\n| Data              | Captured | Example                              |\n|-------------------|----------|--------------------------------------|\n| Files touched     | [OK]        | `src/auth.ts`, `lib/db.py`          |\n| Thread status     | [OK]        | open, stuck, stale, closed, expired  |\n| Author            | [OK]        | OS username                          |\n| Timestamp         | [OK]        | ISO 8601                             |\n| Narrative         | [OK]        | \"Fixed auth token refresh flow\"      |\n| Failed approaches | [OK]        | What was tried, errors, files        |\n| Work type         | [OK]        | bug-fix, feature, debugging, etc.    |\n| Code content      | [ERR]        | Never captured                       |\n| Conversation      | [ERR]        | Never captured                       |\n| Credentials       | [ERR]        | Never captured                       |\n\n---\n\n## Thread Lifecycle\n\n```\nopen → stale  (auto, after stale_days with no activity)\nopen → closed (on session end)\nstuck → closed (on session end or manual)\nclosed → open  (via `npx sticky-note-cli resume <id>`)\nclosed → expired (auto, tombstoned by gc after stale_days)\n```\n\nExpired threads keep only their ID, status, user, and closed timestamp.\n\n---\n\n## File Structure\n\n```\nCLAUDE.md                         # AI instructions for Claude Code\n\n.claude/\n├── settings.json             # Claude Code hook config\n└── hooks/\n    ├── sticky-utils.js       # Shared utilities\n    ├── session-start.js      # Load & inject teammate context\n    ├── session-end.js        # Capture session thread\n    ├── inject-context.js     # Per-prompt relevance scoring\n    ├── pre-tool-use.js       # Lazy injection via git blame attribution (V2.5)\n    ├── sticky-attribution.js # Built-in attribution engine (V2.5)\n    ├── sticky-git-notes.js   # Git Notes utilities (V2.5)\n    ├── track-work.js         # JSONL audit + presence + line tracking (V2.5)\n    ├── parse-transcript.js   # Narrative + failed approach extraction\n    ├── on-stop.js            # Handoff summary on stop\n    ├── on-error.js           # Stuck thread on error\n    ├── post-rewrite.js       # Git Notes rewrite survival (V2.5)\n    └── sticky-codex.sh       # Optional Codex wrapper\n\n.github/\n├── copilot-instructions.md   # AI instructions for Copilot CLI\n└── hooks/\n    └── hooks.json            # Copilot CLI hook config\n\n.sticky-note/\n├── sticky-note.json          # Shared threads (git-tracked)\n├── sticky-note-config.json   # Team config (git-tracked)\n├── audit/                    # Per-user audit logs (git-tracked)\n│   └── <username>.jsonl      # One file per team member\n├── presence/                 # Per-user presence (git-tracked)\n│   └── <username>.json       # One file per team member\n├── .sticky-resume            # Resume signal (local only)\n├── .sticky-injected          # Injection tracking (local only, V2.5)\n└── .sticky-active-resume     # Active resume marker (local only, V2.5)\n```\n\n---\n\n## CLI Commands\n\n```bash\nnpx sticky-note-cli init           # Interactive setup\nnpx sticky-note-cli init --codex   # Setup with Codex wrapper\nnpx sticky-note-cli update         # Update hook scripts (preserves data)\nnpx sticky-note-cli status         # Diagnostic report (includes attribution health)\nnpx sticky-note-cli threads        # List threads with status icons\nnpx sticky-note-cli resume         # List resumable threads\nnpx sticky-note-cli resume <id>    # Resume a previous thread\nnpx sticky-note-cli resume --clear # Cancel active resume\nnpx sticky-note-cli resume-thread  # Smart resume: --query, --user, --file (V2.5)\nnpx sticky-note-cli audit          # Query merged audit trail (all users)\nnpx sticky-note-cli who            # Show active and recent team members\nnpx sticky-note-cli overlap        # Detect file overlaps with teammates (V2.6)\nnpx sticky-note-cli claim <files>  # Declare intent to work on files (V2.6)\nnpx sticky-note-cli switch <branch> # Safe branch switch (auto-stashes data)\nnpx sticky-note-cli gc             # Tombstone expired threads\nnpx sticky-note-cli reset          # Wipe all threads (--force, --keep-audit)\nnpx sticky-note-cli get-line-attribution # File→thread attribution with line ranges (V2.5)\nnpx sticky-note-cli checkpoint         # Set work-topic checkpoint for attribution (V2.5)\nnpx sticky-note-cli sync               # Commit .sticky-note/ changes (--push to also push)\nnpx sticky-note-cli bootstrap         # Provision MCP servers that need secrets\nnpx sticky-note-cli env status         # Show environment provisioning status\nnpx sticky-note-cli env add-server     # Add MCP server to team environment\nnpx sticky-note mcp-server             # Launch MCP server (stdio JSON-RPC)\nnpx sticky-note-cli --version      # Show version\nnpx sticky-note-cli --help         # Show help\n```\n\n### Audit Filters\n\n```bash\nnpx sticky-note-cli audit --user alice\nnpx sticky-note-cli audit --file src/auth.ts\nnpx sticky-note-cli audit --since 2025-01-01\nnpx sticky-note-cli audit --session abc-123\nnpx sticky-note-cli audit --limit 100\n```\n\n---\n\n## Configuration\n\nEdit `.sticky-note/sticky-note-config.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"stale_days\": 14,\n  \"copilot_cli_auto_close_hours\": 24,\n  \"inject_token_budget\": 1000,\n  \"mcp_servers\": [],\n  \"skills\": [],\n  \"conventions\": [\"Use TypeScript strict mode\", \"Test before commit\"],\n  \"hook_version\": \"2.6.12\"\n}\n```\n\n| Key            | Description                              | Default |\n|----------------|------------------------------------------|---------|\n| `stale_days`   | Days before threads expire + gc cleanup  | `14`    |\n| `copilot_cli_auto_close_hours` | Hours before idle Copilot CLI threads auto-close | `24` |\n| `inject_token_budget` | Max tokens for per-prompt thread injection | `1000` |\n| `mcp_servers`  | Shared MCP server references             | `[]`    |\n| `skills`       | Team skill definitions                   | `[]`    |\n| `conventions`  | Team coding conventions (injected)       | `[]`    |\n\n---\n\n## Requirements\n\n- **Git** repository (any host)\n- **Node.js 16+** (for hook scripts and `npx` CLI)\n- **Claude Code**, **Copilot CLI**, and/or **Codex**\n\n---\n\n## Supported Tools\n\n| Tool        | Hook Config                 | Integration                     |\n|-------------|-----------------------------|---------------------------------|\n| Claude Code | `.claude/settings.json`     | Full — all 6 hooks + transcript |\n| Copilot CLI | `.github/hooks/hooks.json`  | Full — all hooks                |\n| Codex       | `sticky-codex.sh` wrapper   | Post-session capture            |\n\nAll tools call the same JavaScript hooks and share the same data files.\n\n> **Third-party tools** (Cursor, Windsurf, Zed, Cline) can use the MCP server\n> for full thread access without hooks. See below.\n\n---\n\n## MCP Server\n\nSticky Note includes an MCP server that gives AI tools direct access to\nthread data, overlap detection, and environment status via standard\n[Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/).\n\n### Auto-registration\n\nThe session-start hook automatically registers the MCP server in `.mcp.json`\non the first session. No manual setup needed — it's available from the second\nsession onward.\n\n### Manual registration\n\nAdd to `.mcp.json` at your project root:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"sticky-note\": {\n      \"type\": \"stdio\",\n      \"command\": \"npx\",\n      \"args\": [\"-y\", \"-p\", \"sticky-note-cli\", \"sticky-note\", \"mcp-server\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Tools\n\n| Tool | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| `get_session_context(id)` | Full thread payload by ID |\n| `get_stuck_threads()` | All stuck threads with failed approaches |\n| `search_threads(query)` | Keyword search across threads |\n| `get_audit_trail(file, user, since, ...)` | Query per-user audit logs |\n| `get_presence()` | Active developers and their files |\n| `check_overlaps(files)` | Detect file conflicts before editing |\n| `get_environment_status()` | Environment sync status (provisioned vs missing) |\n| `get_thread_context_for_files(files)` | Thread attribution for files |\n\n### Why MCP?\n\nHook output goes to the AI model's context window — the AI decides whether\nto surface it. MCP tool responses are part of the AI's active reasoning,\nso the data always gets processed. This makes the MCP server the most\nreliable channel for overlap warnings and environment notifications.\n\n---\n\n## Team Environment Sync\n\nShare your complete AI development environment with the team — MCP servers,\nskills, agents, commands, and permissions — through git, with zero manual setup.\n\n### How it works\n\n```\nTeam lead: npx sticky-note init     → commits hooks + environment/\nTeammate:  git pull && start session → hook auto-provisions everything\n```\n\nThe session-start hook is the provisioning engine. It copies skills to\nplugin directories, writes MCP servers to `.mcp.json`, and merges\npermissions — all automatically when a teammate starts their AI session.\n\n### Directory structure\n\n```\n.sticky-note/environment/\n├── manifest.json         # MCP servers, permissions, env vars\n├── skills/               # Team skill definitions (.md files)\n├── agents/               # Team agent definitions (.md files)\n└── commands/             # Team command definitions (.md files)\n```\n\n### Adding team resources\n\n**Skills/agents/commands:** Drop a `.md` file into the appropriate directory.\nIt auto-provisions on next session and auto-commits with your next commit.\n\n**MCP servers:** `npx sticky-note env add-server` or edit `manifest.json`.\n\n### Secrets handling\n\nMCP servers that need secrets (e.g., `${GITHUB_TOKEN}`) are skipped by\nauto-provisioning. Run `npx sticky-note bootstrap` to provide credentials\ninteractively — they're saved to `.env` (gitignored), never committed.\n\nThe MCP server's `get_environment_status()` tool tells the AI what's missing\nso it can prompt the user.\n\n---\n\n## Concurrent Usage & Merge Strategy\n\n`npx sticky-note-cli init` adds a `.gitattributes` rule:\n\n```\n.sticky-note/sticky-note.json merge=union\n```\n\nThis tells git to **keep lines from both sides** instead of conflicting.\nThreads have unique UUIDs, so concurrent pushes merge cleanly.\n\nPer-user audit logs (`audit/<username>.jsonl`) and presence files\n(`presence/<username>.json`) are written by one user at a time, so they\nnever conflict.\n\n### Branch Switching\n\nSticky-note data (audit logs, presence, thread state) is **branch-independent** —\nit's metadata about the repo, not part of the source code. However, because\nthese files are git-tracked and updated on every tool call, a raw\n`git checkout` or `git switch` will fail if there are uncommitted changes.\n\nUse one of these approaches:\n\n```bash\n# Recommended: sticky-note CLI wrapper\nnpx sticky-note-cli switch <branch>\n\n# Or: git alias (set up by npx sticky-note-cli init)\ngit sw <branch>\n```\n\nBoth auto-stash `.sticky-note/` before switching and restore it after.\nSee [docs/branch-switching.md](docs/branch-switching.md) for the full\ndesign discussion.\n\n---\n\n## FAQ\n\n**Q: Does this capture my code or conversations?**\nA: No. Only file paths, timestamps, usernames, and status metadata.\n\n**Q: What happens with merge conflicts in sticky-note.json?**\nA: `merge=union` in `.gitattributes` handles most cases automatically.\n\n**Q: Can I close a thread manually?**\nA: Edit `sticky-note.json` and change the thread's `status` to `\"closed\"`,\nor run `npx sticky-note-cli gc` to tombstone expired threads.\n\n**Q: Does this work offline?**\nA: Yes. Everything is local until you `git push`.\n\n**Q: How do I set up Codex?**\nA: Run `npx sticky-note-cli init --codex`, then alias the wrapper:\n`alias sticky-codex=\".claude/hooks/sticky-codex.sh\"`\n\n---\n\n## License\n\n[MIT](LICENSE) — fully open source, no restrictions.\n\n---\n\n## Contributing\n\nSee [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) for guidelines.\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/BandaruDheeraj/sticky-note/README.md","numLines":588,"startLine":1,"totalLines":588},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"5ee65094-46ca-4bbd-916a-168c90dc5d7a","version":"2.1.215"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded