bleFrom, readNote);\n274\t  }\n275\t  const events: EvidenceEvent[] = [];\n276\t  for (const anchor of anchors) {\n277\t    events.push(...(await readNote(anchor)));\n278\t  }\n279\t  events.push(...(await readPending(repo)));\n280\t  const filtered = events.filter(\n281\t    (e) =>\n282\t      (!opts.kind || e.kind === opts.kind) &&\n283\t      (!opts.source || e.producer.source === opts.source) &&\n284\t      (!opts.conversation || e.conversation?.id === opts.conversation ||\n285\t        e.conversation?.id.startsWith(opts.conversation)),\n286\t  );\n287\t  return sortEvents(filtered);\n288\t}\n289\t\n290\t/**\n291\t * Reachability with re-anchor resolution. Plain reachability drops any\n292\t * conversation whose anchor commit was rewritten away (a GitHub squash merge\n293\t * discards the branch's commits, so their notes fall out of `rev-list` even\n294\t * though the work survives in the squash commit). A `re_anchor` event —\n295\t * anchored to the successor commit, so it is discoverable exactly when the\n296\t * successor is in view — repairs this: an anchor counts as reachable when the\n297\t * rev reaches it, or when a mapping whose successor is reachable names it as\n298\t * superseded. Superseded commits can themselves anchor further mappings\n299\t * (a squash commit later rewritten again), so resolution loops to a fixpoint:\n300\t * grow the reachable set, read the notes that just became visible, repeat.\n301\t * The set only grows and each note is read once, so it terminates.\n302\t */\n303\tasync function resolveAnchors(\n304\t  repo: RepoInfo,\n305\t  anchors: string[],\n306\t  rev: string,\n307\t  readNote: (anchor: string) => Promise<EvidenceEvent[]>,\n308\t): Promise<string[]> {\n309\t  const reachable = await revList(repo, rev);\n310\t  const visited = new Set<string>();\n311\t  const mappings: ReAnchorMapping[] = [];\n312\t  for (;;) {\n313\t    const frontier = anchors.filter((a) => reachable.has(a) && !visited.has(a));\n314\t    for (const anc