2\\t\\n3\\tPicked up from a Mac setup session on 2026-07-15/16. Context notes included so a fresh Claude Code session can pick these up without re-deriving everything.\\n4\\t\\n5\\t## 1. iCloud Documents & Desktop cleanup\\n6\\t\\n7\\t- [ ] General organization pass on iCloud Documents/Desktop (described as \\\"nightmarish\\\")\\n8\\t- [ ] **Security issue to resolve:** there's a folder on the iCloud Desktop called `password screenshots` — screenshots of passwords stored as plain image files. Decide: migrate those credentials into the Apple Passwords app (already installed, macOS default) or a dedicated password manager, then securely delete the screenshot folder once migrated.\\n9\\t\\n10\\t## 2. LaTeX distribution\\n11\\t\\n12\\t- [x] Decide on and install a LaTeX setup — wanted \\\"something\\\" but hadn't settled on which distribution.\\n13\\t  - Went with BasicTeX + tlmgr (latexmk, collection-fontsrecommended/latexextra/bibtexextra/binextra). LaTeX Workshop installed in VSCodium (Open VSX), configured for manual build (no autocompile) with absolute binary paths + explicit PATH env to work around macOS's stale-session-PATH issue after a fresh install. Verified end-to-end on a real Overleaf-synced paper (fonts, math, bibtex, custom .sty).\\n14\\t- Worth considering: MiKTeX (was on the old MacBook Air) vs. TeX Live / BasicTeX (the standard on Linux, so closer to cluster parity — MiKTeX is more historically Windows-native even though it's cross-platform now). BasicTeX + `tlmgr` gives a small footprint with on-demand package installs if the full TeX Live install feels heavy.\\n15\\t\\n16\\t## 3. SSH jump host: personal Mac → work laptop → work cluster, usable from VSCodium\\n17\\t\\n18\\t- **Why now:** company is rolling out a policy where personal laptops will only be allowed to SSH into the *work laptop*, not directly into the cluster — the work laptop will still be allowed to SSH into the cluster. So the personal Mac needs to hop through the work laptop to reach the cluster.\\n19\\t- [ ] Add a `ProxyJump` entry to `~/.ssh/config` — something like:\\n20\\t  ```\\n21\\t  Host work-laptop\\n22\\t      HostName <work-laptop-address>\\n23\\t      User <username>\\n24\\t      IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_work-laptop\\n25\\t\\n26\\t  Host work-cluster\\n27\\t      HostName <cluster-address>\\n28\\t      User <username>\\n29\\t      ProxyJump work-laptop\\n30\\t      IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_work-cluster\\n31\\t  ```\\n32\\t- [ ] Register the `id_ed25519_work-laptop.pub` key with the work laptop's `authorized_keys`, and `id_ed25519_work-cluster.pub` with the cluster's — both keys already exist on this Mac (generated during initial setup) but were never registered anywhere.\\n33\\t- ⚠️ **Known blocker to resolve:** VS Code's Remote-SSH extension (`ms-vscode-remote.remote-ssh`) is Microsoft-proprietary and isn't distributed through Open VSX — the marketplace VSCodium uses by default. Since this setup deliberately chose VSCodium over regular VS Code, Remote-SSH may not install normally. Options to evaluate next session: the community `open-remote-ssh` fork (Open VSX-compatible), switching back to regular VS Code just for this workflow, or another remote-dev extension that isn't MS-gated.\\n34\\t\\n35\\t## 4. Reconnect reMarkable tablet — DONE 2026-07-16\\n36\\t\\n37\\t- [x] reMarkable app + reManager are both installed, tablet paired and reManager confirmed syncing.\\n38\\t- [x] Mac ⇄ tablet SSH over local WiFi (dedicated key `~/.ssh/id_ed25519_remarkable`, `ssh remarkable` alias in `~/.ssh/config`).\\n39\\t- [x] Switched the tablet's outbound SSH target from the old (sold) MacBook Air to this Mac. Root cause chain, for future reference:\\n40\\t  - The tablet's reMarkable-OS kernel has no TUN device, so its Tailscale runs userspace-only — plain `ssh 100.x` never routes anywhere. The prior setup already solved this correctly with `/home/root/bin/tssh`, which proxies through `tailscale nc` as an OpenSSH `ProxyCommand`, using the real OpenSSH client at `/opt/bin/ssh` (not the tablet's built-in Dropbear `ssh`, which can't parse modern `openssh-key-v1`-format keys at all — that's a real, confirmed Dropbear limitation, not a config issue).\\n41\\t  - The only actually stale thing was `/home/root/bin/mac`, a one-line shortcut hardcoded to the old MacBook Air's Tailscale IP (`100.72.225.106`). Updated it to this Mac's Tailscale IP (`100.105.108.44`). Verified end-to-end: `mac \\\"echo ok\\\"` from the tablet works.\\n42\\t  - Remote Login (SSH server) enabled on this Mac (was off by default on the fresh install).\\n43\\t  - Dead end explored and reverted: temporarily restricted this Mac's sshd `KexAlgorithms` to non-quantum-safe algorithms chasing a red herring — reverted, back to full defaults.\\n44\\t\\n45\\t## 5. Expose ds4 as a semi-public API for the reMarkable (and friends), only while the laptop is plugged in\\n46\\t\\n47\\t- **Goal:** let the reMarkable tablet — and some external people (\\\"friends\\\") — hit the local `ds4-server` (DeepSeek V4 Flash, already downloaded/working) over the network, but not have it running unconditionally or fully open to the world.\\n48\\t- [ ] Decide exposure mechanism — Tailscale is already installed, two real options:\\n49\\t  - **Tailscale Serve** — private to devices on the tailnet only; \\\"friends\\\" would need to be invited onto the tailnet (simplest, most secure, but not truly public)\\n50\\t  - **Tailscale Funnel** — actually publicly reachable over HTTPS from the open internet, no tailnet invite needed for friends, but wider exposure surface\\n51\\t- [ ] Decide access control — \\\"friends\\\" implies more than one external user; given DeepSeek V4 Flash inference is resource-heavy, probably wants an API key / allowlist rather than wide-open access.\\n52\\t- [ ] Make it conditional on AC power (\\\"periods when my laptop is plugged in\\\") — likely a `launchd` job (or a script triggered by `pmset -g batt` power-source changes) that starts `ds4-server` + the Tailscale Serve/Funnel when plugged in and stops them on battery, rather than running 24/7.\\n53\\t\"}]},\"uuid\":\"76be498f-2349-4213-a5bf-5993e77e4a7b\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-07-17T04:46:16.327Z\",\"toolUseResult\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/evintunador/TODO.md\",\"content\":\"# Mac Setup — Carried-Over TODOs\\n\\nPicked up from a Mac setup session on 2026-07-15/16. Context notes included so a fresh Claude Code session can pick these up without re-deriving everything.\\n\\n## 1. iCloud Documents & Desktop cleanup\\n\\n- [ ] General organization pass on iCloud Documents/Desktop (described as \\\"nightmarish\\\")\\n- [ ] **Security issue to resolve:** there's a folder on the iCloud Desktop called `password screenshots` — screenshots of passwords stored as plain image files. Decide: migrate those credentials into the Apple Passwords app (already installed, macOS default) or a dedicated password manager, then securely delete the screenshot folder once migrated.\\n\\n## 2. LaTeX distribution\\n\\n- [x] Decide on and install a LaTeX setup — wanted \\\"something\\\" but hadn't settled on which distribution.\\n  - Went with BasicTeX + tlmgr (latexmk, collection-fontsrecommended/latexextra/bibtexextra/binextra). LaTeX Workshop installed in VSCodium (Open VSX), configured for manual build (no autocompile) with absolute binary paths + explicit PATH env to work around macOS's stale-session-PATH issue after a fresh install. Verified end-to-end on a real Overleaf-synced paper (fonts, math, bibtex, custom .sty).\\n- Worth considering: MiKTeX (was on the old MacBook Air) vs. TeX Live / BasicTeX (the standard on Linux, so closer to cluster parity — MiKTeX is more historically Windows-native even though it's cross-platform now). BasicTeX + `tlmgr` gives a small footprint with on-demand package installs if the full TeX Live install feels heavy.\\n\\n## 3. SSH jump host: personal Mac → work laptop → work cluster, usable from VSCodium\\n\\n- **Why 