utes anywhere. The prior setup already solved this correctly with `/home/root/bin/tssh`, which proxies through `tailscale nc` as an OpenSSH `ProxyCommand`, using the real OpenSSH client at `/opt/bin/ssh` (not the tablet's built-in Dropbear `ssh`, which can't parse modern `openssh-key-v1`-format keys at all — that's a real, confirmed Dropbear limitation, not a config issue).\\n  - The only actually stale thing was `/home/root/bin/mac`, a one-line shortcut hardcoded to the old MacBook Air's Tailscale IP (`100.72.225.106`). Updated it to this Mac's Tailscale IP (`100.105.108.44`). Verified end-to-end: `mac \\\"echo ok\\\"` from the tablet works.\\n  - Remote Login (SSH server) enabled on this Mac (was off by default on the fresh install).\\n  - Dead end explored and reverted: temporarily restricted this Mac's sshd `KexAlgorithms` to non-quantum-safe algorithms chasing a red herring — reverted, back to full defaults.\\n\\n## 5. Expose ds4 as a semi-public API for the reMarkable (and friends), only while the laptop is plugged in\\n\\n- **Goal:** let the reMarkable tablet — and some external people (\\\"friends\\\") — hit the local `ds4-server` (DeepSeek V4 Flash, already downloaded/working) over the network, but not have it running unconditionally or fully open to the world.\\n- [ ] Decide exposure mechanism — Tailscale is already installed, two real options:\\n  - **Tailscale Serve** — private to devices on the tailnet only; \\\"friends\\\" would need to be invited onto the tailnet (simplest, most secure, but not truly public)\\n  - **Tailscale Funnel** — actually publicly reachable over HTTPS from the open internet, no tailnet invite needed for friends, but wider exposure surface\\n- [ ] Decide access control — \\\"friends\\\" implies more than one external user; given DeepSeek V4 Flash inference is resource-heavy, probably wants an API key / allowlist rather than wide-open access.\\n- [ ] Make it conditional on AC power (\\\"periods when my laptop is plugged in\\\") —